Spectacular "wingsuit" jump over Bogota
Athlete Jhonathan Florez jumped Thursday from a helicopter over Bogota, the Colombian capital.
Equipped with a wingsuit (a combination equipped with wings), it passed at 160 km/h above the famous Monserrate Shrine, located more than 3 000 meters above sea level, where many onlookers had gathered to observe his feat.
A black box in your car?
While U.S. highway planners are struggling to find the money needed to repair decrepitable highway infrastructure, many people see a solution in the form of a small black box that attaches above your car’s dashboard.
The aircraft, which records all miles traveled by a motorist and pass on information to officials, are at the center of a controversial attempt in Washington and at government planning offices to overhaul the outdated financing system of major U.S. roads.
The generally uninteresting sector of the planning of the main axes suddenly provoked a lively debate and turbulent alliances.
Libertarians have joined environmental groups to lobby for the government to use small boxes to keep track of the miles you’re going through, and possibly the route you’re driving, and then using the information to draft a tax bill.
The Tea Party is daunted.
The American Civil Liberties Union is also very concerned and expresses concern about the protection of privacy.
And while members of Congress are unable to reach an agreement on whether to continue, several states did not wait.
They’re looking at how, over the next decade, they’ll be able to switch to a system that allows drivers to pay based on the number of miles traveled.
Thousands of motorists have already embarked these black boxes, sometimes equipped with a GPS monitoring system, for an experimental trip.
This is really essential for our nation.
“It’s not like we’re having the choice,” said Hasan Ikhrata, managing director of the Southern California Association of Governments, which expects the state to start recording the miles traveled by every California driver by 2025.
There's going to be a change in the way we're paying those taxes.
Technology is there to do that.
The pressure stems from the fact that the country’s Highway Trust Fund, funded with taxes that Americans pay at the pump, is financially dry.
Americans don't buy as much gas as before.
Cars can travel more miles with a gallon.
The federal tax itself is 18.4 cents per gallon has not increased for 20 years.
Politicians are reluctant to raise the tax, even by 1 cent, while gasoline prices are high.
"The gas tax just isn't sustainable," said Lee Munnich, a transportation policy specialist at the University of Minnesota.
Its state has recently installed trackers on 500 cars to test a payment system per mile.
“This turns out to be the most logical long-term alternative,” he said.
Wonks calls this a mileage user fee.
It is not surprising that the idea seduces the urban liberals, since taxes could be used to change driving habits so as to reduce congestion and gas emissions, for example.
California planners are interested in the system as they develop strategies to meet the goals set out in the state’s ambitious global warming laws.
But Representative Bill Shuster (R-Pa.), chairman of the House Transport Committee, said he also saw it as the most viable long-term alternative.
Supporters of the Free Market of the Reason Foundation also appreciate the idea of making drivers pay by the mile.
“This isn’t just a tax rushing into a big black hole,” said Adrian Moore, vice president of politics at the Reason Foundation.
People pay more directly for the benefits they receive.
The movement is also supported by two former U.S. Secretarys of Transportation who, in a 2011 report, urged Congress to move in the way of payment per mile.
The U.S. Senate approved a US$90 million pilot project last year that would have involved about 10 000 cars.
But the Speaker of the House killed the proposal, responding to the concerns of rural legislators representing voters who have to travel many miles every day to get to their jobs or in town.
However, several states and major cities are making progress on their side.
The most determined is Oregon, which has mobilized 5 000 drivers to conduct the most important experience in the country.
These motorists will soon pay the state for mileage fees instead of gasoline taxes.
Nevada has already completed a pilot project.
New York City is considering one.
Illinois is attempting a test on a limited number of trucks.
And the I-95 coalition, which includes 17 agencies involved in road transportation in states along the U.S. east coast (including Maryland, Pennsylvania, Virginia, and Florida) is investigating how it could implement change.
The concept is not universal success.
In Nevada, where about 50 volunteer motorists have recently equipped their car with a device, drivers were concerned that the government could keep up with their every move.
“The concerns about Big Brother and this kind of thing are a big problem,” said Alauddin Khan, who heads strategic performance management at the Nevada Department of Transportation.
This isn't something people want.
From the beginning of the trial, the ACLU of Nevada warned on its site: “It would be relatively easy to turn these devices into full-fledged location devices.”
It is not necessary to build an enormous and cumbersome technological infrastructure that will inevitably be expanded to keep records of people’s daily whereabouts.
Nevada is one of the few states that are now scrambling to find affordable technology that would allow the state to know how many miles run a car, but without knowing the exact location or time.
If we can do this, Khan added, citizens will be more comfortable.
The search for this technology led some state agencies to use a small California start-up called True Mileage.
Originally, the company did not intend to help motorists who pay taxes to states.
It sought to break into the emerging auto insurance market, in which drivers would pay according to their mileage.
But the devices she tests have appealed to long-line planners because they don’t use GPS and provide a limited amount of information, periodically downloaded by modem.
“People will be more inclined to do that if you don’t record their speed and locate them,” said Ryan Morrison, CEO of True Mileage.
There have been major mistakes in pilot programmes in some states.
There are much less expensive ways and much less intrusive methods to proceed.
In Oregon, planners try the experience by offering motorists different choices.
They can choose a device with or without GPS.
They can still choose not to have a device at all, preferring instead to pay a package based on the average number of miles traveled by all residents of the state.
Other states hope to sell the concept to wary citizens by allowing devices to do more, rather that not enough.
In New York City, transportation officials are looking to develop a taxation device that would also be equipped to pay parking fees, provide “payment-to-drive” insurance, and create a set of real-time speed data from other drivers that motorists could use to avoid traffic jams.
"Motorists would be encouraged to participate because of the benefits that the system offers them," said a document on urban development.
However, some transportation planners are questioning if the whole debate about pay-per-mile isn’t just a huge diversion.
At the San Francisco Bay Area Metropolitan Transportation Commission, officials say Congress could very simply handle the bankruptcy of the Highway Trust Fund by increasing gas taxes.
An additional one-time or annual tax could be imposed on drivers of hybrid vehicles and on others whose vehicles do not consume a lot of gasoline, so that everyone pays their fair share.
“You don’t need to resort to radical surgery when you just need aspirin,” Randy Rentschler, the commission’s director of legislation and public affairs.
If we do this, hundreds of millions of motorists will worry about protecting their privacy and a lot of other things.
David Bowie: four unreleased listening
The English musician hasn’t finished surprising this year.
From The Next Day, released in January, he concocted a luxury reissue scheduled for November 4, with several unreleased.
Four have already emerged on the Web.
David Bowie's announcement of the release of a new David Bowie album had left everyone in a panic.
On his 66th birthday on January 8, 2013, he said that a new album would be released in March.
After ten years of silence (the last record, Reality, dates from 2003) and too few public appearances, the English musician proved that one could still count on him to sparkle the pop scene.
Feast for His Fans
Not surprised, David Bowie had more than one trick in his bag with The Next Day.
The Thin White Duke has planned to reissue this album on November 4.
For the occasion, he concocted a real feast for his fans.
This reissue, entitled The Next Day Extra, will appear in the form of three records: the original album, unreleased studio sessions and remixes, as well as a DVD containing the four clips already unveiled.
The Next Day Extra will contain a total of ten additional tracks compared to the original album: the three tracks of the Deluxe edition, five songs specially unveiled for the occasion, as well as two remixes.
David Bowie also made the presentations of this beautiful box set in a video.
He introduces each of the discs, but also the accessories provided with: exclusive photos and covers, a notebook to share his own impressions, a libretto with the lyrics...
And, above all, he gives a taste of his new track Atomica, typically in the vein of The Next Day, with very forward guitars and popularly controlled rock electricity.
Unprecedented already listening
But Atomica isn't the only title to reveal her charms.
The Informer, Like A Rocket Man, Born In A UFO are also available on the web.
The Informer is double-sided: a disturbing intro before a luminous cavalcade that gradually slows down to give way to a pop ballad.
On Like A Rocket Man, did Bowie want to make a little reference to Elton John's Rocket Man, or even to Gravity?
With this playful title, the singer seems in any case in his element when he no longer has his feet on the ground.
Space Oddity, next door, was a lot more serious.
On Born In A UFO, David Bowie once again refers to his strangeness: would he come from another planet?
Guitar riffs, bewitching, make you want to leave Earth.
Bowie in any case has fun playing chameleon with these titles: in turn informant, man aboard a rocket, Martian potential...
He conceals and reveals at the same time, plays to adopt different personalities, as he did throughout his career, especially with his characters Ziggy Stardust and Aladdin Sane.
It is not surprising that he therefore has a mask in his hand in the photography of the campaign L'Invitation au Voyage, of the Louis Vuitton brand, whose new face he embodies.
It will appear in one of their commercials, broadcast from 10 November.
Defence Minister Rob Nicholson insisted that wounded soldiers were not summarily released from the Canadian Armed Forces, and stressed that a transition process was followed by all soldiers prior to their return to civilian life.
Attacked by the Liberals and New Democrats in the House of Commons, Nicholson assured that prior to their release, members of the military were following a transition plan in collaboration with their superiors.
All wounded soldiers receive appropriate care for their return to civilian life and none of them are released until they are ready, he said.
Critics accuse the government of wanting to save money by not allowinging injured soldiers - who do not respond to the "universality of service" rule of the military demanding that personnel be able to perform a variety of tasks - to achieve the 10-year eligibility period required for pension benefits.
In particular, they cited two cases reported by The Canadian Press, including that of a soldier released last Friday.
Corporal David Hawkins, a reservist in London, Ontario with post-traumatic stress syndrome, has been released from the military despite his request to stay another year in order to receive a fully indexed retreat.
His case came after Corporal Glen Kirkland, who told a parliamentary committee last month that he had been pushed out before he was ready because he did not respond to the universality rule of service.
Mr. Hawkins stressed that a soldier could be prepared to leave, with plans and consultation sessions, but that this was totally different than wanting to leave the ranks.
"I told them I wasn't ready," he said in an interview with The Canadian Press on Wednesday.
I asked for months if there was a way for me to stay, and they said no.
Since the beginning of major fighting in Afghanistan, the army has struggled to determine what latitude it can give to wounded soldiers who want to remain in the ranks, but who are not fit for combat.
Under the current rules, seriously wounded soldiers have up to three years to recover.
If they do not meet the criteria for overseas deployments, they may be forced to leave the army.
Data presented last year to Parliament indicate that among the 1218 soldiers released for medical reasons, 199 did not reach the duration of service required to obtain pension benefits.
Liberal veterans critic Jim Karygiannis on Wednesday called for the reinstatement of Corporal Hawkins, while New Democrat Jack Harris called for an immediate end to “this shameful practice.”
Disnes betting on tablets to launch an animated series
The U.S. media and entertainment group Disney has decided to favor tablets over its own TV channels for the upcoming release of a new children's series.
The first nine episodes of Sheriff Callie's Wild West will be available from November 24 on the website watchdisneyjunior.com or via its app for phones and tablets.
Its global launch on the Disney group's channels is only scheduled for 2014, details the press release of its Disney Junior division.
The cartoon, intended for children from 2 to 7 years old, tells the adventures of pussy Callie, sheriff of a city in the American West where she reigns order with a magical lasso.
Each episode consists of two 11-minute stories.
“Interacting with smartphones and tablets is second natus for children today,” said Albert Cheng, vice president of digital products at Disney/ABC Television Group.
This type of experience is part of Disney’s efforts to “expand the lifetime of its series and build new relationships with its audience through digital platforms that are increasingly important,” he added.
A survey published earlier this week by Common Sense Media showed an explosion in the use of mobile devices by young children in the United States: 38% of those under the age of 2 have already used a tablet or a phone, and 72% of people under the age of 8, compared respectively to 10% and 38% two years ago.
Snowden is ready to “cooperate” with Germany on U.S. surveillance
Edward Snowden, the U.S. intelligence whistleblower, said he was willing to travel to Berlin to testify before the German parliament if the U.S. National Security Agency and its director Keith Alexander did not provide answers about their activities.
German MP Hans-Christian Ströbele met with Snowden in Russia on Thursday, where he enjoys the right of asylum, to discuss his testimony in Germany.
In a letter that the MP presented to the media in Berlin on Friday, Snowden said: “Although the outcome of my efforts has obviously been positive, my government continues to treat the difference of opinion as a leak and seeks to penalize political discourse with accusations against which it is impossible to defend itself.”
However, telling the truth is not a crime.
In his letter, Mr. Snowden wrote that he believes the support of the international community could persuade the U.S. government to drop the criminal charges against him.
As part of the lawsuit filed by the U.S. Department of Justice, he is charged with espionage and theft of state property.
Hans-Peter Friedrich, German Interior Minister, told the Zeit Online: “If Mr Snowden is ready to speak to German officials, we will make sure to make this possible.”
Relations between the United States and Germany have been strained following complaints that the NSA had wiretapped German Chancellor Angela Merkel’s mobile phone.
Thomas Oppermann, the MP who heads the parliamentary group overseeing foreign secret services, said that if there was a chance to hear Mr. Snowden as a witness "without endangering him and completely ruining relations with the United States", it was necessary to take advantage of it.
Ströbele, the German Green MP, posted a photo of him with Mr Snowden on his Twitter account.
During his visit to Russia, he was accompanied by two German journalists.
Ströbele said that, according to the lawyer of the former NSA consultant, Snowden would not be able to return to Russia if he left the country.
If Mr. Snowden testified in Germany, he should be assured that he would be "safe" there, he said.
Snowden wrote in his letter that he had been the subject of a “serious and sustained” campaign of persecution that forced him to leave his country.
However, he said that he was encouraged by the global response to “my act of political expression.”
Citizens around the world as well as senior officials — including in the United States — judged the revelation of an inexplicable system of ongoing surveillance was of public utility.
The letter puts forward an offer of cooperation with the German authorities “when the difficulties of this humanitarian situation have been resolved”.
Cogeco Cable may soon offer interactive TV
Cogeco Cable subscribers may well have access to apps like Facebook, Twitter, and ultimately the video-on-demand service Netflix via their television in the future that doesn’t seem so distant.
Cogeco's subsidiary said on Thursday that it was currently conducting preliminary tests of the beta version of this platform with some of its users.
"This will allow us to highlight more user-friendly interfaces as well as more options," said Louis Audet, president and CEO of Cogeco.
Cogeco Cable thus follows suit against its competitors, such as Bell, even if Cogeco's subsidiary does not yet have a specific date with respect to this new platform.
“You have to adapt and change or resist change and fail,” Audet said.
The ultimate goal remains to offer tools that our clients do not currently have access to.
Telecommunications giant Rogers has already indicated that it could offer Netflix if certain technical details, which have not been cited, could be settled.
In the United States, the popular video service claims to be in discussions with some major cable companies so that its service is available through their broadcast platforms.
Cogeco and Cogeco Cable's CEO also praised the Harper government's announcement during its Speech from the Throne on October 16.
Ottawa wishes to force cable and satellite TV providers to offer customers the opportunity to pay for pay-per-view services.
“It’s been about two and a half years since we’ve been saying that the idea of forcing consumers to buy important channel packages doesn’t work,” Audet said.
He hoped, however, that the consultations conducted by the Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission (CRTC) would lead to interesting recommendations.
"There will have to emerge from these discussions a kind of new framework of reference to define our new Canadian cultural policy with respect to television," said Cogeco CEO.
The CRTC has been consulting with the public since last week and is expected to continue with the industry next spring.
Cogeco reported a net profit of 43.8 million in the fourth quarter, or 82¢ per share.
This is a decline compared to the net profit of 44.9 million, or 83¢ per share, of the same period last year.
The Montreal-based company explains this decline by depreciation costs related to new acquisitions.
Cogeco acquired U.S.-based Atlantic Broadband cable carrier for 1.36 billion in 2012.
This was the company's first major acquisition after the one that had failed in Portugal.
Last December, the Montreal-based company also purchased Peer 1 Network Entreprises, a Vancouver-based Internet provider, for 526 million.
As for Cogeco’s revenues, they grew 41.5% in the fourth quarter to reach 504.7 million.
There are 1.8 billion for the current fiscal year.
The net profit of its main subsidiary, Cogeco Cable, was 43.9 million, or 90¢ per share, down from 45.7 million, or 93 ¢ per share, from the same period last year.
Cogeco Cable’s revenues, however, increased by 45% to 470.4 million.
The company lost 15 237 customers in the fourth quarter.
However, for the 2013 fiscal year, Cogeco Cable's number of customers is up 5546.
Mr. Audet doesn't worry about the number of clients fluctuating this way.
“For me, this is not an indication of a change in trend,” he observed.
It varies from quarter to quarter with very strong competition.
A horse in beef products
Horse meat has been detected in cans of beef products, sold by tweast British low-cost retailers, the food hygiene agency announced today.
Routine tests have revealed that the products, developed in January in Romania and sold by the Home Bargains and Quality Save chains, contained horse DNA up from 1 to 5%.
"Since horse meat is not mentioned in the list of ingredients, it should not have been present in the product," the agency explained.
A scandal over the presence of horse meat in cooked dishes erupted in Europe at the beginning of the year, following tests carried out in Ireland.
According to examinations of the European Commission, France has been the country most affected by the presence of this type of meat in products supposed to contain only beef.
A collision between two vehicles left four people injured, early Friday, on Highway 131 in Lanaudière.
Shortly before 4am, a motorist driving in a northerly direction, in Saint-Félix-de-Valois, lost control of his vehicle and struck a car arriving in the other direction.
The four occupants of the two vehicles were injured, but there was no fear for their lives.
Traffic had returned to normal around 6am on Friday.
Frontier Airlines plans to charge the carry-to-hand luggage
Frontier Airlines plans to charge up to $100 to passengers who carry hand luggage on its flights.
Frontier Airlines plans to start charging up to $100 for hand luggage and $2 for coffee or soda, although in its announcement on Wednesday, it indicated that passengers could take the unopened can with them down the plane.
The new carry-to-hand baggage fees are for bags stored in the upper compartment, so small bags installed under the seat will always be free of charge.
Frontier stated that it would charge $25 if the fee was paid in advance and $100 if the travelers paid only once at the boarding gate.
Frontier spokeswoman Kate O'Malley said the $100 was planned to encourage travelers to pay fees in advance.
“We don’t want to charge for that price,” she said.
The airlines began charging the first and second checked baggage in 2008.
Passengers who tried to avoid these costs put as many things as they could into hand luggage stored in the upper compartments, so there was often no more space in those compartments.
These fees are a way to make sure passengers carry less things on board.
O'Malley said these new fees won't really be used to raise money.
It’s about making Frontier’s customers the most loyal that it’s becoming increasingly difficult to find space in the upper compartments.
Passengers who purchase their tickets on the airline's website will not have to pay.
Thus, a passenger queuing at a Frontier boarding gate could carry a bag free of charge, while the next person in the queue may have to pay $100 for a similar bag.
O'Malley explained that Frontier's site and check-in procedures are in the process of changing to ensure that passengers are aware of these fees before arriving at the door.
Frontier's carry-to-hand baggage fees will not be applied until the summer, although no date has been set.
Passengers often ruspet about baggage fees and other charges, but airlines love them.
They believe that baggage handling costs are significant and that passengers who want this service would have to pay for it.
Many people on Wall Street consider the addition of baggage fees to be a sign that airlines are charging far enough to cover the cost of air travel after years of losses.
However, many of them did not touch the baggage fee.
Spirit Airlines Inc. had applied the first carry-to-hand baggage fees three years ago, and low-cost Allegiant tracked a little later.
The only other airline imposing such fees is the Hungarian airline Wizz Air, said the consultant with airlines Jay Sorensen, who closely follows the additional fees.
He estimated, in a December 2011 report, that Spirit’s carry-to-hand baggage fees increased $50 million per year.
Sorensen, a former Midwest Airlines executive, recently traveled on Spirit and wondered what he was going to find at the boarding gate when passengers would discover the company’s unusual carry-on baggage fees.
“I’ve never seen such a smooth boarding procedure in my entire professional career,” he said.
I was expecting to see squeaks of teeth and a fight bursting at the door.
The plane was full, he added, "the passengers boarded at lightning speed."
Frontier also does like Spirit and applies a $2 fee for coffee, tea, soda or fruit juice.
Frontier has indicated that passengers taking a soda or fruit juice can take the unopened can with them, and those who have coffee can be booked for free.
It will always dispense water free of charge.
U.S. Airways briefly tried to charge for the beverages in 2008 but came back seven months later after the passengers complained and while no other major airlines had followed.
Frontier's decision to charge a carry-on baggage fee when passengers don't buy their ticket directly from the airline is its latest effort to encourage customers to go to its site.
Airlines pay online travel sellers such as Orbitz $10 to $25 for each ticket sold.
This prompted all airlines to encourage passengers to buy directly from them instead of contacting an online travel agency.
However, Frontier went further in this area.
In September, she started to offer only half of loyalty miles to customers who bought their ticket through an online travel agency.
On Wednesday, it reduced the bonuses by 25% on a trip.
For example, a 1 000-mile Frontier trip purchased from an online travel agency would earn 250 miles.
It also allows passengers to choose their seats in advance only if they buy their ticket directly from the Frontier website.
Frontier has a loyal clientele in his hometown, Denver, but her business is declining and she's losing money.
Proceeds fell by 9% and its flight capacity declined by almost 13% in the first quarter, according to financial results released Wednesday by its parent company, Republic Airways Holdings Inc.
Republic has tried to rehabilitate Frontier’s finances by selling the airline.
The NSA implicates an “internal error” and not hackers for the failure of its site
The Shadow National Security Agency said on Friday that it was a bug that had caused its public site outage for a few hours, not hackers as some claimed online.
“NSA.gov remained inaccessible for a few hours tonight due to an internal error that occurred during a planned update,” the spying agency said in an email statement.
The problem will be solved tonight.
The allegations that the outage was due to a Distributed Denial-of-Service [DSD] attack are false.
Earlier tonight, online server trackers noted that the NSA site had been down for at least 6 hours, and some users still can’t access the site.
Earlier, an NSA spokesman told ABC News that the agency’s sensitive internal network was “not at all” compromised.
No classified information is in danger, the spokesman said.
At least one group of online hacktivists claimed to be responsible for the breakdown of the NSA site by a DSD attack.
DSD attacks are aimed at flooding a targeted site until servers are overloaded and the site is collapsing.
Cyber-tactics is not very sophisticated and attacks are not intended to enter the internal network of the targeted system.
The once ultra-secret NSA, a time dubbed the “No Such Agency,” has found itself in the spotlight and sharply criticized in recent months following a wave of revelations about its extensive communications monitoring programs domestically and abroad – which are part of the NSA’s secret files stolen from the agency and leaked by the NSA’s disillusioned former consultant, Edward Snowden.
This growing controversy around the agency has caused a lot of speculation that tonight’s incident was the result of targeted cyber operations.
The Delta City Center now closed
The Delta Centre-Ville hotel closed Thursday in Montreal, after 36 years of existence.
The investment fund that owned the building sold it to developers who will turn it into student residences.
Three hundred and fifty people worked at the hotel.
Of these, 200 have still not found a new job.
Delta promises not to let go of its employees.
Employers came to meet with employees directly on-site, and employees met individually to assess their needs.
“This support will continue for the next six months,” said Félix Bisson, Delta’s Regional Director of Labour Relations.
The closure of the Delta occurs in a very competitive hotel market.
The investment fund that owned that building had choices to make.
Either he reinvested in the building to continue to operate it, so there were investments of tens of millions while the competition is very fierce, there are a lot of new hotels that appeared in Montreal.
"Or he was selling it to someone else and that's what happened," says Paul Arsenault, holder of the Transat Tourism Chair at UQAM's School of Management Sciences.
Other hotels in Montreal will also be converted in the coming months, such as the Crown Plaza which will become a residence for seniors.
In the meantime, four hotel projects with nearly 600 rooms will emerge within two years.
For the CEO of Titan, “Goodyear Amiens’ buying project starts with zero employees”
After throwing the towel with crash in January for the partial takeover of the Goodyear site of Amiens-Nord promised at the closure, Maurice Taylor, the CEO of the American pneumatician Titan, declares, today, ready to save 333 jobs out of the 1,137 that the factory has.
Arnaud Montebourg, Minister of Productive Recovery, had already announced, Monday, October 21.
Having given up of the plan to purchase the plant in January, you return today.
You had pronounced violent attacks and insults, speaking of "so-called workers", who "work three hours" a day, "crazy trade unions", targeting the CGT.
You don't understand this about-face.
Is it to please Mr. Montebourg?
I'm not trying to please anyone.
Excluding my wife.
Mr. Montebourg, is a charming young man who tries to save industrial jobs among the highest paid.
If I've hurt someone by my words, I'm sorry.
But France also has a high level of taxation and unemployment.
Is the truth hurting you?
Working seven hours a day when in other countries we work eight hours handicaps France.
In India, China and many other countries, we work ten to twelve hours a day.
But I have no prejudices about France.
What I see is a factory that manufactures good agricultural tires, has good equipment, is well located and has spaces to expand.
Why does Titan need this factory so badly?
Titan doesn't have to buy that factory.
But, with the correct price and competent workers, it’s worth trying.
What kind of deal do you expect between CGT and Goodyear?
If, since the announcement of the closure of the factory, Goodyear had offered employees a good severance allowance, I think that 100% of the employees would have accepted it.
Now, let's imagine that Titan's buying the closed factory from Goodyear.
From then on, Titan could move the machines to Poland or to any other country in the European Union that still has its own currency.
I think Mr. Montebourg knows this.
However, he wants to keep the Amiens factory with at least 333 well-paid jobs.
Titan agrees to recruit them from the approximately 1,200 current Goodyear employees.
So Mr. Montebourg needs a commitment from Titan before trying to get the CGT to sit at the table with Goodyear.
The first step is for the CGT and Goodyear to seal a severance benefit agreement for all employees.
Therefore, there will be no more employees in the factory.
Mr. Montebourg said that you were ready to guarantee these 333 jobs for four years.
Do you confirm this?
The only number I have mentioned to the minister is 333.
I know he'd want a four-year guarantee.
But, as I told you, the CGT and Goodyear must first agree on severance payments.
If all employees accept them, the plan to purchase the factory starts with zero employees.
How can we give duration guarantees when there are no more employees left on the site?
If Mr. Montebourg succeeds that the CGT and Goodyear agree and that Titan buys the factory, we intend to stay in Amiens-Nord for more than four years.
Bombardier's profit down, as aircraft deliveries and orders decline
Bombardier Inc., a Canadian aeronautical and rail manufacturer, reported a 15% drop in its net profit on Thursday, under the pressure of a decline in aircraft orders and deliveries during the third quarter and contractual problems in its Trains division.
Montreal-based Bombardier also did not provide flight test data for its all-new CSeries aircraft or information on whether the aircraft will meet its ambitious commercial commissioning schedule before next September.
After the maiden flight of the test aircraft about a month and a half ago, it flew only three times, and then the question arose as to whether the testing phase was well underway.
The results did not live up to the forecasts and the stock price fell by 8% on the Toronto Stock Exchange.
Cameron Doerksen, an analyst at the National Bank Financial, lowered its rating on Thursday, from “outperformance” to “sector performance” due to the stock exchange’s low upside potential in the next quarter or two quarters.
“Although the decline in aircraft deliveries has been largely anticipated, we are really disappointed by the profit margin in the Transport division,” Cameron Doerksen said in a note to customers.
We believe that Bombardier will record new orders for its CSeries aircraft as the in-flight testing program progresses.
However, if no new orders are announced in the coming months, we expect the market to become more skeptical about the program.
Bombardier hopes that the CSeries family of aircraft will be able to catapult it into the lower segment of a market now dominated by Boeing and Airbus.
The first test plane was unveiled in March and flew for the first time in September after months of delay.
But the number of firm orders for CSeries aircraft has so far been modest up to 177. It appears that potential buyers are waiting for the results of flight tests to validate the company’s claims regarding the energy efficiency and the potential savings of the new airliner.
Currently, the company has registered a total of 403 orders and purchase commitments from 15 customers and operators.
Pierre Beaudoin, President and Chief Executive Officer, is confident that Bombardier will meet its goal of 300 firm orders prior to the commercial commissioning of the first aircraft.
Leaders also reassured analysts and the media on Thursday by telling them that the program was progressing according to schedule.
"The test aircraft did not remain on the ground any longer than expected," Beaudoin said in a conference call, adding that ground testing and software updates were planned during the plane's downtime.
Each builder plans differently.
We had decided to carry out a first flight and provide an update period, and that is what we did.
This will happen again throughout the flight program.
The second of the five test planes are expected to fly in the coming weeks, the others will follow shortly after, the company said.
Still, analysts are skeptical about the fact that the first customer can fly a CSeries plane just 12 months after its maiden flight.
Bombardier has indicated that it is in the process of evaluating the schedule of entry into service and will provide new information in the coming months.
“This slower flight testing – although apparently in line with Bombardier’s internal schedule – reinforces our view that entry into service will be postponed until the first quarter of 2015,” said Mr. Doerksen.
In the third quarter ended September 30, Bombardier’s net profit fell to US$147 million, or 8 cents per share, compared to US$172 million, or 9 cents per share, a year earlier.
Adjusted earnings per share remained unchanged at 9 cents.
Incomes fell slightly from 4.2 to $4.1 billion.
Analysts were expecting a result of 10 cents per share and revenues of US$4.56 billion, according to Thomson Reuters I/B/E/S.
The world’s fourth-largest aeronautical manufacturer said it had delivered 45 aircraft during the quarter, up from 57 a year earlier.
Net orders fell to 26 aircraft, up from 83 previously.
The delay in the Aeronautics division represented 32.9 billion dollars as of September 30, unchanged compared to December 31 of the previous year.
“In Aeronautics, the results were in line with our forecasts, but the low number of orders and general market conditions were disappointing,” Beaudoin said.
Aeronautical revenues fell by 13% to 2 billion dollars.
Bombardier, the world’s largest railway manufacturer, said the division’s revenues increased by nearly 11% to US$2.1 billion.
The Transport Division's backlog totalled $32.6 billion as of September 30, a slight increase from December 31 of the previous year.
The margins of the Transport division have suffered from the problems of execution concerning a few major contracts.
Leaders announced that further guidance would be provided in the fourth quarter.
Bombardier’s stock price, which also announced that Patrick Pichette, chief financial officer of Google Inc., will sit on the board of directors, fell 8.5% to C$4.83 in the afternoon of Thursday.
The Brazilian company Embraer SA, the world's third-largest aeronautist and a competitor closest to Bombardier, said Thursday a 10% decline in its quarterly profit.
Two 23 and 27-year-old speleologists had been missing in a chasm under Crolles’ Dent since Thursday night.
They were located this Friday afternoon.
The two men, one seasoned with the practice and the other did not go underground Thursday around 21h30 to make a crossing of the Dent de Crolles, located in the commune of Saint-Pierre-de-Chartreuse.
We hadn’t heard from them ever since, the source said.
"Speleologists should have come out around 5am," said Thierry Larribe, technical advisor to the Speleo rescue in charge of the rescue organization.
Dozens of people on site
About twenty rescuers, ten civilian members of the Speleo French rescue, as well as gendarmes, CRS and firefighters were there.
In the late afternoon this Friday, the two speleologists were located.
"It's another group of speleologists who, after finding them exhausted but healthy in the cavity, transmitted the information to one of the rescue teams engaged in the network," explains Le Dauphiné.
The tworty soldiers at the 13th battalion of the Chambéry Alpine Hunters were found "exhausted, but not injured".
They got lost in the network, returned in their footsteps while waiting for help, said the prefecture.
Refueled, they should come out of the cavity with the help of rescuers in the evening.
German journalists urged to flee Google and Yahoo
The union of German journalists on Thursday urged its members to stop using the online services of Google and Yahoo in the aftermath of new revelations about U.S. and U.S. intelligence activities.
“The German Federation of Journalists advises journalists to avoid until further notice using Google and Yahoo’s search engines and messengers,” it said in a statement.
She deems "scandallous" information from the Washington Post that the U.S. National Security Agency (NSA) and the UK's Government Communications Headquarters (GCHQ) have collected masses of information by infiltrating international networks that allow both firms to synchronize their servers.
"The research carried out by journalists is as confidential as the contact details of their sources and the nature of their communications with them," said Michael Konken, president of the union that claims 38,000 members.
Repainted lights: MP calls for Brigitte Grouwels to resign
Minister Brigitte Grouwels’ plan to “relift” traffic light poles in Brussels does not please everyone.
As a reminder, the Brussels Minister of Public Works and Transport launched this Thursday in the center of Brussels a test project consisting of repainting 16 traffic light poles of the reginum in the yellow and blue colors of Brussels.
The goal is both "to increase security" and "Brussel identity".
The idea is, ultimately, to repaint all Brussels traffic lights, at an estimated cost of about one million euros.
But the blue chosen by the minister is "too dark", according to Brussels MP Emmanuel De Bock who speaks of "flemishization" of the capital and demands the "resignation" of Brigitte Grouwels.
"Not content with insanely spending the money of the Brussellois in operations to relift the signalling poles in the colors of the Brussels Region, Brigitte Grouwels continues her operations of flemishization of the Capital," De Bock said in a statement.
Following her yellow-mangue-black taxis, she finally repainted herself in dark-bluel-bloir yellow the Brussels posts.
According to the member, there is now "no difference in visual continuity between Flanders and Brussels".
The people of Brussels deserve better than to see their money wasted by a CD&V minister who carries out the NVA program itself.
It's high time to stop Flander's Trojan horse.
"As a reminder, Brigitte Grouwels has only been elected with 2,245 votes or 0.5% of the people of Brussels!" concludes Mr. De Bock.
the Pont-de-Buis portico disassembled
The ecotax portico of Pont-de-Buis, around which took place Saturday a violent demonstration of opponents of this tax, was dismantled Thursday morning.
Cranes arrived on site shortly after 10 a.m., and traffic on the national was hijacked immediately.
The decision of the dismantling, announced by the prefecture of Finistère on Wednesday, was taken by the company Ecomouv, manager of the portico.
It is the last of the three ecotaxal portics in working order in the department of Finistère, the other two having been disassembled or sabotaged.
Launching an accessory store for Google Glass
An online store, offering multiple accessories dedicated to Google Glass, has just been made available to thousands of developers with a prototype smart glasses signed Google.
They can thus acquire an earphone, a charger or a storage case.
This store, strictly reserved for developers already with Google Glass, offers some accessories such as a charger and its USB cable, for $50.
For the same price, it is also possible to acquire a protective cover made of micro-fibers or an in-ear headphone.
Although Google is currently working on a Google Glass model equipped with correction lenses, no large-scale marketing date has yet been confirmed.
It's been five years since my father erased himself from the living world.
At first I denied his death, I was talking about him in the present.
I was so afraid to forget it or rather I didn’t know how I was going to keep “frequencing it.”
There is no recipe, no manual to pass the wall of the invisible and find his own.
Then there were signs.
The first time it was the very strong feeling of his presence in the passenger seat as I was driving.
Another time, a sweet awakening in the middle of the night to look at his watch that does not leave me anymore, sitting on the nightstand.
It's the image of my smiling father that accompanies me in my daily gestures.
Our mother left us after her exhausting battle with cancer.
At least that’s what I thought the day I found his body empty, clenched on that wrinkled-sleeping hospital bed.
Similarly, his funeral was an icy farewell focused around the meager glow of the candles that framed his coffin.
I thought she'd gone.
Gradually, in small daytime or nocturnal apparitions, a little timid, a little erased, she quickly returned to my mind, evolving as she regained her place in the landscape of my thought which was believed in mourning.
And then, she revealed herself while revealing to me my own facets that I had not yet perceived, masked by my relationship with her.
So I learned and understood that I hadn’t lost my mother’s person but simply a woman I didn’t really know, a woman who embodied this person, during her stay in life.
As she died, this woman had perfected her life and liberated the person I loved, and behold, I found her, full and complete.
This detour of the path of my life remains the most unexpected and the most beautiful.
It's a privilege to know that loved people never leave us.
I look like my father, "both inside and out," it seems.
I've always been told.
I've never believed him back then.
I've had a tough relationship with him until he became old, sick.
I wasn’t afraid of him anymore and I was able to love him.
One day he died.
For a long time, he accompanied me: when I quit smoking, when I was afraid, when I had pain...
He talked to me, constantly encouraged me, he lived in my body.
I saw his hands looking at mine, lent him my body.
But it was never indiscreet.
I could've had a personal life.
He left my intimacy.
It lasted a long time and one day he left.
Finally, it was comfortable and reassuring to be understood, encouraged, advised.
I don't know who said that deceased people aren't forgotten.
My parents aren't anymore, but I constantly feel them close to me.
Every event, every moment of my life makes me feel their presence because I always refer to it: what would they have said? what would they have thought? what would they have done?
I constantly dream about them, maybe not every night but several times a week that's for sure.
I often dream of the last moments that I have to share with them before it's too late, only, there's always one thing that prevents me from fulfilling this moment.
I often wake up distressed when it happens to me and I deeply feel their absence.
Sometimes, some dreams carry me on the contrary and fit into my history and indeed, they continue to live and share my life.
On the eve of my ultrasound where I had to know the sex of my baby, I dreamed that I was waking up, I was getting out of bed, my father was waiting for me on the landing and he smiled and rejoiced that I was waiting for a little boy.
The next day, I had the feeling that he had visited me in my sleep to tell me that he shared with joy what was happening to me.
And of course it was a little boy in my womb.
I loved sharing this moment with him and I take pleasure in telling this common memory lived after his death.
Five years ago, my grandma died of cancer.
A year earlier, she was on a trip with my family to Cuba.
That's the clash that his disappearance provoked in the young bachelor that I was at the time.
Nevertheless, I realized my mourning rather quickly.
But it continues to influence my life, especially in times when I find myself in trouble, or when I have to make important decisions.
Learning Arabic and taking an interest in the Middle East, to the point of making a master's degree in a great school, have not been, in hindsight, harmless choices.
In fact, I often headed to speak Arabic in my childhood, and talk about Morocco, where she had lived for decades - before returning to France at the time of independence.
The values she has instilled in me are always present, so that I sometimes think about how she would like me to act at some point.
The same goes for my attitudes: I try to live up to the generosity and uprightness of mind of one who was a fervent believer.
From now on, I feel it as a daily presence, a benevolent soul, a saving spirit.
I see his eyes resting on me.
My mother passed away nineteen years ago now.
Died after talking to me on the phone.
I have gone through all phases: misunderstanding, anger, pain, tears that trigger themselves, anywhere, anytime, in unusual places, incongruous moments.
Then time eased the pain.
Only the lack remained, this unanswered void, this need for her, to confide me, to reassure me in the hollow of her sweetness.
Still, she's there, she's silent, she's there and looking at me.
Every morning, I see his worried eyes resting on me, I see the dark circles that weigh down his gaze, the fine lines around the lips that dig the cigarettes, the folds that mark the forehead the days of worry.
My mother took possession of my face and she looks at me every morning in my mirror.
And every morning I turn my eyes away.
My wife and the mother of my 3 children died of cancer at the age of 43.
We always feel her protection, nothing serious can happen to us.
It was his promise on his bed of pain, so we gradually relearned to smile, and pronouncing his name is no longer taboo but comfort.
Of course, I talk to her in the hollow of the night when the absence hurts too much, she invites herself into my dreams when morale is a little reached.
We feel carried and protected in difficult times, the retreat of time makes us realize how conductor she was and we try to stay in the way she had traced us.
If a being has loved you very much, the absence cannot tear it out of your heart and memories.
In a way, the loved one becomes your inner energy.
I lost my father on August 22, 2008 from asbestos cancer.
I was very close to him, I always acted based on what he thought or what he liked.
I couldn't witness his burial and three weeks after his death, I gave birth to a little boy.
If I have my convictions sometimes different from his, I always wonder if my actions are consistent with his vision of things.
Besides, I've had problems with my work for these beliefs.
For me, it's impossible to get in the opposite direction, I don't sleep and I always wonder what he'll think.
I don't know if I've embraced his way of being or if I'm just like him: Is it genetic?
In any case, it will always be my reference.
It was kind of like an alter-ego, there wasn't any need to talk between us.
In short, he's here on a daily basis.
I feel his presence and it makes me happy.
It's almost thirty years since my husband died at the age of 33.
I was 28 years old and our son 6 years old.
The immense pain that invaded me has of course diminished but it is always close to me.
Very often he "disembarks" in my dreams in such a precise way, so alive that upon awakening I'm even sadder to find that it was only a dream.
The other night, he asked me if I was okay, I told him no, he answered "I'm coming down" but in such a real voice that I woke up in a burst, anxious and I turned on the bedside lamp looking everywhere, convinced that he was going to arrive.
I always live in the same house, and often I feel observed and I turn around to see if he's not there, I know it's him and I talk to him.
In every room I feel his presence and it makes me happy.
For nothing in the world I would not leave this house where we have been happy and where his spirit lives with me.
I'm 58, I've always lived alone since his departure, he's and will remain the love of my life.
I want to make it clear that I am sociable with a profession of responsibility, I laugh and I sing, I go out, I'm a grandmother, I have a lot of friends, but my heart and my soul belong to him and I never talk about him except with my son, and I never go to the cemetery.
Iraq: October was the bloodiest month since 2008
October has been the deadliest month in Iraq since April 2008.
Baghdad published official figures this Friday: 964 people lost their lives last month: 855 civilians, 65 policies and 44 soldiers.
This publication takes place on the day the Iraqi Prime Minister is received by the US President.
Noury al-Maliki wants help from the United States.
“We’re not telling the world to be by our side and support us, we have the right to ask the world because we’re part of it,” Al-Maliki said Thursday in Washington.
And also because if what happens in Iraq is not settled, it is going to expand, and what is happening in Syria is going to expand too.
And what happens when a virus of terrorism lives? it spreads.
Noury al-Maliki was speaking at the United States Institute for Peace, an independent institution established by Congress.
Outside the building, protesters were protesting against the Iraqi leader.
They brandished signs accusing him, among other things, of being a murderer and calling on the United States to refuse to provide help.
Cocainoman lawyer who has informed a drug sponsor of a police investigation has been jailed
Basharat Ditta, 42, gave information to crime lord Neil Scarbrough
The lawyer was afraid that his secret drug addiction would come to light
He was sentenced to a three-year prison sentence by the Liverpool Assize Court
A great defense lawyer, who had informed a drug sponsor of a major police investigation because he was afraid that his secret drug addiction would be revealed, was sentenced to three years in prison.
Basharat Ditta, 42, gave sensitive information to crime lord Neil Scarbrough regarding investigations into his drug-related activities after compromising himself because of his habit of using cocaine.
The lawyer, who was nicknamed "Bash" and considered by criminals to be a "preminent file", was arrested at his home in 2011 as a result of police listening operations directed towards Scarborough, which he had represented in a previous trial in connection with drug trafficking.
Police officers spotted Sarborough, 32, dropping three bags of cocaine at the lawyer's home in Blackburn, Lancashire while attending a Bars dinner with confreres.
Investigations revealed that Ditta was a “regular consumer” of this Class A drug after tests showed traces of cocaine in his hair, on his wallet and credit cards.
Over an eight-month period between January and August 2011, he sought to unlawfully obtain information about the arrest of two men on behalf of Scarborough as well as one of his associates.
The four suspects were monitored at the time by the police as part of a major investigation into heroin and cocaine trafficking taking place in the counties of Lancashire, Cumbria, Merseyside, Berkshire and West Yorkshire.
Along with 32 other men, they were later jailed after police seized heroin and cocaine for £1.5 million, as well as over £200,000 in cash during a series of raids.
Ditta, 42, provided information to criminals because he feared that his drug addiction would be exposed.
Now, at the Liverpool Assize Court, Ditta, who works in Blackburn's Forbes Solicitors law firm, has fallen into disgrace after being convicted on two counts of impeding justice after a three-week trial.
He had admitted possession of cocaine during a previous hearing.
The lawyer's fall came after police, investigating Scarborough, discovered that he had been in regular telephone contact with Ditta in February 2011.
Two investigators followed the suspect and spotted him arriving at Ditta's house and placing 60 percent pure drugs under the lawyer's trash can in a black golf glove.
Shortly after depositing, Scarborough was regularly in telephone contact with Ditta who was at a dinner at the Ewood Park football stature, pregnant with the Blackburn Rovers club.
The lawyer went home, discovered the drug, and they phoned nine times.
The court heard that Ditta was a “regular consumer” of cocaine after tests showed traces of this Class A drug in her hair, on her wallet and credit cards.
Ditta was arrested later but denied using cocaine. He stated that he had spoken to the alleged drug dealer because it was his client and argued that their discussions were subject to "legal privilege".
During his arrest, Ditta picked up her wallet and tried to remove several credit cards, but they were all seized and taken a hair sample.
During an interrogation, he stated that he had an office at his home, as well as at his place of work, and that it happened that clients would call him home for legal reasons.
But the court heard testimonies that it called major players in the drug trade, some of whom he had represented, after key arrests to tell them which investigators were aware.
Prosecutor Anne Whyte said: "If anyone needs to know that you shouldn't violate the law, it's a criminal lawyer."
Mr. Ditta is accused of abusing his position as a criminal lawyer, and of being overly involved with individual clients.
The relationship we’re talking about isn’t just a drug dealer, but a drug dealerer supplying drugs to his own lawyer.
Some of his communications will undoubtedly be legitimate communications because it was his lawyer.
But this went far beyond the ordinary limits of a relationship between a lawyer and his client.
He obstructed the police investigation as much as he could to allow him to continue his criminal activities.
Mr. Ditta didn't honor his profession, but disgraced her.
He got too close to some customers, especially Scarborough, and he allowed his independence to jeopardize.
Ditta denied any illegal action and said: “If I was a corrupt lawyer, which I am not, and if I had wanted to provide information to Mr. Scarborough, I would not have waited 15 hours, I would have done it immediately.”
But after the hearing, Superintendent Lee Halstead of the Lancashire Police said: "Mr. Ditta went from a criminal lawyer to a criminal himself as soon as he started getting drugs from criminal organisations."
His cocaine addiction compromised him forever and made him vulnerable to the ulterior motives of executive members of organized criminal groups who tasked him with obtaining valuable information about police investigations.
Lawyers should observe the strictest rules of integrity and build trust with the public.
Mr. Ditta betrayed this trust and tried to shelter behind the varnish of his profession.
Lancashire's Serious and Organised Crime Unit conducted the investigation into Mr. Ditta, who was found guilty on three counts of cocaine possession and now impeding justice, demonstrating his commitment to prosecuting criminals in court.
Let this case serve as a warning to criminals so that they know that no one is above the law.
We'll find and bring you to court.
Scarborough himself was imprisoned for 14 years after pleading guilty to complicity in heroin, cocaine and cannabis trafficking.
Thirty-five others involved in trafficking have been sentenced to a total of 153 years in prison for drug trafficking.
On his website, Ditta organized a question and answer session about himself during which he said that his dream job would be to be a lawyer to represent clients on death row in America, his last dinner guest would be Mohammed Ali and inequality would be what would motivate him to work.
