Travellers and staff rushed to the outings or on the tarmac.
Very quickly the police intervened and a man suspected of being the shooter was apprehended on the roof of an airport parking lot.
The airport is being evacuated and air traffic is interrupted.
Classical Song for Teenagers
Each time, it is the school clientele that will be targeted, an initiative that had been very successful during the previous season.
Thround fives imagined and performed by artists from the region, vignettes with a high dose of humor, teenagers are allowed to tame classical singing.
In addition to this show, the Société d'art lyrique du Royaume will return with its Lyrical Destinations, a formula that is now full at the Pulperie de Chicoutimi.
Two other concerts will take place in the summer of 2014, giving artists from the region the chance to show what they are capable of.
To these activities was added the Apéro lyrique, the benefit concert held last August, with the help of soprano colorature Marie-Ève Munger.
It was a benefit activity, alongside the artistic dimension, and it helped the non-for-profit organization to produce a balance sheet that is as soft to the ears as an air from The Bat.
Without delivering precise figures, the president of the board of directors, Yves Bergeron, took advantage of the press meeting held yesterday, in Chicoutimi, to be reassuring.
In spite of the precariousness of so many cultural institutions, the future emerges well, even in the long term.
We have stronger and more consistent support.
"Financies are afloat and we hope to last until the 50th anniversary of the operetta, a step that we will take in seven years," he said.
"I'd even say beyond that," added Hélène Gaudreault, managing director, smiling.
The 37-year-old man who took a child hostage at Gabrielle Roy de Surrey School faces six counts, according to the Royal Canadian Mounted Police.
Omar Moustapha Hassan has been accused of tasking, kidnapping, uttering threats, carrying a weapon for dangerous purposes, abduction, and failure to comply with an ordinance.
"The rapid reactions of the police officers involved and their ability to defuse the situation immediately were essential to the security conclusion of this incident," Corporal Bert Paquet said in a statement.
Omar Hassan is still in detention and will have to appear in court on Friday.
New request for a class action against the Sainte-Croix brothers
A new request for a collective appeal has been lodged against the Sainte-Croix brothers, concerning sexual assaults that would have taken place this time at St. Joseph's Oratory as well as in several orphanages, colleges and schools.
The petition is based on the applicant's testimony, identified as "J. J"., which would have been masturbated in the 1950's, first in Notre-Dame-des-Neiges by his teacher the Brother Submitted, then by his confessor, Father Bernard, at St. Joseph's Oratory, where he was serving as Mass and where his father worked as a painter.
The first recourse targeted only three institutions, and when we announced the regulations, a lot of people told us: "I was in such a place, can I register?" says lawyer Alain Arsenault, who defends the victims in both recourses.
It is the peculiarity of the class action that has just been filed to permit the addition of plaintiffs from any institution where abuse could have been perpetrated by members of the Sainte-Croix congregation.
At present, the appeal gathers the complaints of 25 people who claim to have been assaulted by the Sainte-Croix brothers.
The alleged facts are generally older than those mentioned in the first recourse, since several of the establishments concerned closed their doors in the 1960’s.
The appeal also emphasizes that the Congregation of Sainte-Croix and St. Joseph's Oratory, which is a separate entity, "allowed for sexual abuse to be perpetrated against children", that they "exercised a moral, religious and psychological coercion on the victims", that they "were aware of the sexual abuses perpetrated and nevertheless suffocated them", and that they "knowingly and knowingly
These last accusations are based, among other things, on letters written by the Sainte-Croix brothers' lawyer, Émile Perrin, in the 1990's, but also by the research made in the archives on this subject by Brother Wilson Kennedy, a former brother of Sainte-Croix who publicly denounced the abuse.
The class action must first pass the admissibility stage in Superior Court.
Once the admissibility accepted by the Court, the second stage, the hearings on the merits, are proceeded.
In the case of the first recourse, the Congregation of Sainte-Croix had agreed to settle amicably before hearings were conducted on the merits.
Large fire in a trade
A fire severely damaged a business in the borough of LaSalle, in Montreal, in the night from Thursday to Friday.
Rescuers were called around 1am, Friday, due to a fire that broke out in the basement of an Indian food restaurant on Dollard Avenue, near the intersection with Rejane Street.
The thirty firefighters who were dispatched to the scene took nearly an hour to control the blaze.
The fire "caused significant damage to the structure of the building," said Richard Bordeaux, the chief of operations of the Montreal Fire Department.
The cause of the fire was unknown, but there was no one at the restaurant when firefighters arrived at the scene.
There were no injuries, but nearly twenty dwellings, located on the first and second floors of this row of commercial premises, had to be evacuated.
The Red Cross has been requested, as residents of one of the apartments may have to be temporarily housed, the SIM said.
A lot of opposition to medical assistance in dying at the Palliative Care Congress
The Canadian Palliative Care Congress, taking place this week in Ottawa, comes days after the vote in favour of the principle of the bill on medical assistance in dying in Quebec.
This is an opportunity for several palliative care associations to reaffirm their disagreement.
If all patients had access to effective care to alleviate their suffering, in addition to being able to stay at home, very few of them would like to end their lives, according to a spokeswoman for the Canadian Palliative Care Association, Maryse Bouvette.
Focusing on palliative care in Canada, the approach to euthanasia would become minimal.
The president of the Réseau des soins palliatifs du Québec also rejects the bill on medical assistance in dying.
If it is adopted, Alberte Déry is worried about the consequences for the next generations.
“What is the meaning of life,” she laments.
The majority of palliative care homes will refuse to help patients die, according to Suzanne Fitzback, vice-president of the Alliance of Palliative Care Homes.
Ms. Fitzback, also director of Mathieu-Froment-Savoie in Gatineau, believes that the service would otherwise be useless.
We’re never asked, “I want to die, give me an injection.”
The director of the Ontario Palliative Care Association, Rick Firth, believes that Quebec's bill confuses people about the vocation of palliative care.
He doesn't believe Ontario will follow suit.
For her part, the Liberal member for Gatineau, Stéphanie Vallée, says that the notion of end-of-life will have to be clarified before the bill is passed.
The Kenyan press is outraged by the controversial media law
“It’s a scary country and it’s legitimate to ask yourself: what will prevent Parliament from sweeping the independence of the judicial system tomorrow?” the newspaper said, challenging the bill and calling for it to be declared unconstitutional.
“This law is draconian and very punitive, and we refuse,” said Cyrus Kamau, managing director of Capital Group – owner of CapitalFM, one of Kenya’s most respected independent news stations and websites.
He said that the new media court “will always be biased because it is an extension of the government” and that restrictions on content and advertising would harm Kenya’s place in the global economy.
"I hope that the president will listen to us, and we ask him to reject this bill and refer it to parliamentarians," he added.
According to The Star newspaper, the new bill will allow the government to “strangle on the media.” The Standard, meanwhile, indicated that democracy and freedom of opinion in Kenya “had suffered a blow” and blasted the bill calling it “draconian.”
The adoption of the bill comes at the same time as a set of measures to strengthen national security in the wake of the attack perpetrated by armed Islamists in the Westgate shopping center last September.
The Kenyan media aroused anger from the authorities by broadcasting footage of CCTV cameras on which the troops deployed on the scene of the attack supposedly stole the luxury shopping center.
Police chief David Kimaiyo reacted by summoning two journalists and a mediate official to interrogate them, although the summons was withdrawn following a release of shields in the media.
Under the new bill, the media can be subject to fines of up to 20 million Kenyan shillings and journalists up to 1 million with the additional risk of being “delisted” or no longer being able to receive official press accreditation.
The court also has the power to seize the property of an infringer if the offender did not pay his fine.
According to the Daily Nation, “even a single fine is enough to paralyze most FM stations.”
He also says that the measures could have a devastating effect on what he described as Kenya’s “leading blogosphere.”
By silencing the media, politicians know they can do what they want with impunity.
“No one will ever know,” Mutuma Mathiu, a journalist at The Nation, wrote, describing the Kenyan media as a key source of control in public life.
“Delivered to themselves, the politicians would bankrupt the country and bring us back to hunting and gathering.”
Kenyan lawmakers have been the target of people’s anger in the past.
In May, they took stances against the cuts ordered by the National Wages Commission and reinstated their mirobolating wages of about 532 000 shillings per tax-exempt month – which are among the highest in the world.
It’s a difficult year for pharmacists
The departure of nearly 10 pharmacists at the Centre de santé et services sociaux (CSSS) in Laval has caused quite a stir to the management of the Hôpital de la Cité-de-la-Santé, during the year 2012-2013.
Due to multiple retirements, maternity leave or departures altogether, the pharmacy department found itself severely staffed.
Almost 30% of the workforce was needed, making this financial year "very difficult", according to the head of the department, Gillian Beaudet.
However, the CSSS has decided not to hire an independent workforce, which can cost up to three times more than a full-time hired pharmacist.
We didn't use troubleshooting," Beaudet said.
Some of our activities have been streamlined or diminished within the facility to overcome, the time that things change.
Of course, we've worked hard to interest our young [pharmacy residents] so that they come and stay here.
There's a combination of circumstances that caused us to be in a bad situation last year.
Improved situation
Following this arduous period, the year 2013-2014 promises to be significantly easier for the pharmacy department of the CSSS.
Already, three pharmacists are returning to work after maternity leave and three other pharmacists have been hired in recent months.
In addition, the efforts made by the department to retain have borne fruit, because the four students currently in residence in Laval have also decided to remain employed by the CSSS.
Now it's much better," said the pharmacist.
By the end of the fiscal year, we will have seven new pharmacists and three maternity returns.
This will fill our departures from last year.
Ever-increasing needs
However, the situation remains delicate.
Several factors, such as the shortage of hospital pharmacists or a predominancy of young women in the profession, make situations such as that experienced in 2012 difficult to predict.
For us [the number of staff] is always precarious, because we have an environment that is young and we employ a lot of young women, so with pregnancies, we almost always drive to three maternity holidays when it goes well, she added.
In the last year, we've had a lot more and we don't have pharmacists who are available to make replacements, so it was more difficult.
The 22-year-old supermodel doesn’t start his career in the cinema with the little door.
Director Lars von Trier has chosen her to play Charlotte Gainsbourg young in Nymphomaniac, her next film.
This pornographic drama shoots, in eight chapters that will emerge in two parts (January 1 and 8, 2014), the erotic memories of a forties addicted to sex since her adolescence.
Stacy Martin’s resemblance to her eldest is striking: a twig silhouette, a diaphanous complexion and Franco-English origins.
Without forgetting a certain taste of the risk: the one who had not hesitated to pose naked on glossy paper will be displayed in much more sulphurous situations on the big screen.
An excerpt from Nymphomaniac raised the temperature on the Web: Stacy Martin appears in bed and in full ecstasy with Shia LaBeouf.
If the provocative filmmaker asked his actors to put their modesty in the closet, he resorted to X professionals for the most daring sex scenes, before merging all the bodies digitally: above the belt, it's the star; below, the lining.
From Björk to Charlotte Gainsbourg to Nicole Kidman, Lars von Trier has a habit of pushing her actresses into their last entrenchments, always for the best.
Stacy Martin hasn't finished talking about her.
A room of the CGR of Narbonne evacuated Thursday evening
Thursday evening, the first session of a film was interrupted at the Mega CGR of Narbonne, as a precautionary measure, following tingling to the throat felt by the spectators.
Immediately, the director of the establishment had the evacuation of the room carried out and warned the firefighters for a suspicious odor.
Approximately 70 people came out of the session.
The people were examined and a complete recognition of the room yielded nothing.
Wreathful joking against a backdrop of jet tears?
Or involuntary incident?
However, the management of the cinema has played the principle of precaution and the safety of its customers thoroughly.
The room's been aired and it's all back in order.
The cinema was able to take back its rights and the customers regain the pleasure of the images.
On screen only.
She tries to import 2 kg of cocaine into pumpkins
Taking advantage of Halloween, a woman tried to bring two kilos of cocaine hidden into pumpkins Thursday morning at Montréal-Trudeau airport.
It is during the examination of the luggage of the passenger that the drug could have been detected.
Cocaine had been distributed in three pumpkins that had previously been emptied.
The drug was then transported to the Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP) office, which took over in this investigation.
The Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) did not reveal where the woman arrived when she was intercepted.
That's part of the investigation," said Jacqueline Roby, CBSA spokesperson.
What I can tell you is that she's coming home.
Since the beginning of 2013, border services officers at Montréal-Trudeau airport have completed 173 drug seizures, including 10 cocaine seizures for a total of 44 kilograms.
In 2012, border services officers in the Quebec region carried out a total of 1653 narcotic seizures.
Aerial Raid Against Military Installations in Syria
According to the Lebanese army, Israeli planes entered Lebanese airspace early Wednesday afternoon, but strikes took place in the evening.
A shipment of SA-8 short-range surface-to-air missiles would have been targeted and destroyed.
This new IDF aviation raid on Syrian territory (the sixth since the beginning of the year, according to the Israeli daily Haaretz) has not been confirmed either by Israel or Syria.
The raid took place in circumstances roughly identical to the one that had taken place on July 5: at the time, it was also an unnamed American official who had confirmed to CNN an Israeli attack, this time targeting Yakhont ground-sea missiles, delivered by Russia to Damascus.
Israeli officials did not hide their irritation to see Washington reveal the attack, at the risk of forcing President Assad to react.
Ukraine is getting closer to bankruptcy
The rating agency Standard & Poor's lowered Ukraine's solvency rating on Friday, questioning the ability of the former Soviet republic, in recession for more than a year, to meet its financial obligations.
The rating of the country's public debt securities rises to "B-", sinking into the category of speculative investments.
It has a negative outlook, with S&P seeing at least one in three chances that it will be lowered again within a year.
“It’s less and less likely that the government’s strategy will make it possible to secure enough foreign currency to meet its high external financing needs,” the agency said.
The agency notes that Ukraine's foreign exchange reserves fell by 26% between September 2012 and September 2013 and predicts that the trend will continue.
This complicates the repayment of loans taken abroad.
These reservations, which the authorities resort massively to support the local currency, the hryvnia, collapsing, the agency is increasingly considering a devaluation, which would inflate its external debt.
Kiev also needs liquidity to pay its gas imports to Russia, which accuses it of failing to pay an invoice of US$882 million.
This announcement is bad news for the Ukrainian power in a period of sharp tensions with its Russian neighbor, furious of Kiev's willingness to sign an association agreement with the EU at the end of November.
It was made public in the aftermath of the publication of official statistics showing that the country's economy had suffered between July and September its fifth quarter of follow-up.
Its indebtedness, still relatively modest, has exploded in recent years and S&P estimates it at 33.5% of GDP, against less than 10% before the 2008-2009 crisis.
The country, in a budget deficit, has been demanding for months aid from the International Monetary Fund, which had given it in 2010 a credit of 15.3 billion dollars and has so far released only 3.4 billion.
But the Fund refuses to pay any new tranches until the country adopts unpopular reforms to reduce its deficit, in particular by increasing gas prices to the population.
At the end of a new unsuccessful mission in the country, the IMF noted this week that "significant external financing needs" constituted "vulnerability", while noting "signs of economic improvement".
However, for economist Oleksandr Joloud of the Center for Political Studies, "we can't wait for an improvement in the short term."
"There is little hope that unpopular reforms will be launched in a pre-electoral year", with presidential elections scheduled in 2015, the expert told AFP.
The only hope is an improvement in the international situation.
S&P also notes the "uncertainty" linked to the possible signing of an Association Agreement between Ukraine and the EU, which Brussels conditions on the release of opponent Yulia Tymoshenko.
“Signing the agreement would be positive for long-term trade but there could be negative consequences in the short and medium term related to Russia’s reaction,” said S&P, which fears Moscow’s “trade restrictions.”
Russia, which accounts for a quarter of Ukraine's exports, has warned that in the event of the creation of a free trade zone between the EU and Kiev, it should tighten its border controls for imported goods.
U.S. green light for merger Publicis-Omnicom
Publicis and Omnicom said on Friday they had received no objections from U.S. authorities to their merger, approaching the creation of the world's leading advertising agency.
The merger brings together the world’s second-largest agency, Omnicom, and the third, Publicis.
“Omnicom Group and Publicis Group announced today the expiration of the review deadline for the previously announced merger of Publicis Groupe and Omnicom, planned by the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act of 1976, as amended,” the two groups said in a statement.
They say they have also received the necessary authorizations in Canada, India and Turkey after South Africa and South Korea.
The expiry of HSR's examination period in the United States and the authorization decisions issued in the other jurisdictions meet several of the conditions necessary to carry out the transaction.
“The merger is also conditional on obtaining other regulatory authorities and the approval of the shareholders of both groups,” they added.
The Congolese army chases M23 rebels
The Congolese army (FARDC) announced Thursday that its units will be chasing M23 rebels into their bases located in the forests and mountains of North Kivu bordering Rwanda and Uganda.
The M23 appears about to be defeated after being driven out of localities in the north-eastern Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) that it had controlled since the beginning, 20 months ago, of the insurgency.
"We're going to pursue the M23 and corner it wherever it's hiding because they're about criminals," FARDC spokesman Colonel Olivier Hamuli told Reuters.
We must not let them reorganize because they have been martyring the Congolese people for too long.
It's time to bring peace.
M23 officials said they had evacuated the localities under diplomatic pressure and Bertrand Bissimwa, political leader of the rebellion, told RFI that these military setbacks would in no way change his demands for the peace talks.
The latter, according to Ugandan mediators, resumed Wednesday in Kampala between the government of Kinshasa and the M23.
Clashes are reported in the hills that dominate Bunagana, the last locality at the hands of insurgents to have fallen this week, as well as around Runyoni, a hill where the M23 rebellion began in 2012.
At their peak in November, the insurgents occupied Goma, the capital of North Kivu province, taking advantage of the flight of the government garrison and the passivity of MONUSCO peacekeepers.
The fall of Goma led the United Nations Mission in the DRC, the world's largest in terms of staff, to strengthen its mandate and to form a rapid intervention brigade composed of South African, Malawian and Tanzanian soldiers.
For its part, the FARDC General Staff has been overhauled and the army went on the offensive against the M23, changing the course of the war.
The pace of progress of government troops is now unprecedented.
"The M23 seems to be coming to an end," Congolese business expert Jason Stearns predicted on his Congo Siasa blog.
It would be historic – it would be the first time that the government of Kinshasa has succeeded in beating a major insurrection.
And it would also be the first time since 1996 that no armed group allied with Rwanda would be present in eastern DRC.
United Nations experts are accusing Rwanda, which fiercely denies it, of militarily supporting the M23, which was initially made up of former Congolese mutinated soldiers.
Britain’s foreign minister, William Hague, has invited Rwandan President Paul Kagame to exercise restraint, a Foreign Office spokesman said.
Last week, Kigali raised the possibility of military reprisals following shells in Rwandan territory.
On Wednesday, the residents of Bunagana invaded the streets of the village to salute the entrance of FARDC soldiers.
"We lived for a year with the M23 and it seemed unimaginable to us to one day be released by the army," said a resident of the community bordering Uganda.
"We've lived in terror (of M23), we're traumatized," he added.
Tests carried out by the Institut Pasteur on a patient suspected of being infected with the coronavirus have been negative, the Ministry of Health said.
“The two cases identified in May 2013 therefore remain the only two confirmed cases in France to date.”
On Tuesday, the 43-year-old patient was suspected of being affected, after returning from Saudi Arabia, a country where the disease has already killed some 50 people.
Chevron, U.S.’s second-largest oil company, on Friday reported a decline in its quarterly profit due to declining refining margins, as its oil and gas production increased while remaining down from the group’s targets.
Its net profit for the third quarter decreased to US$4.95 billion, or US$2.57 per share, up from US$5.25 billion, or US$2.69 per share, a year earlier.
Analysts surveyed by Reuters expected an average profit per share of US$2.71.
The group produced 2.59 million barrels of oil equivalent per day during the quarter, up from 2.52 million bpd produced a year earlier.
The company is targeting 2.65 million bpd for this year, with 25% output growth expected by 2017.
Most of the growth in the coming years will come from its liquefied natural gas projects in Australia.
Due to the cost of these projects, annual capital expenditures increased by seven billion dollars in two years and are expected to reach US$36.7 billion in 2013.
Proceeds from production activities declined slightly in the third quarter, while profits from downstream activities (including refining and chemical production) fell 45% to US$380 million.
This decline in refining margins affects the entire sector.
Chevron's main competitor Exxon Mobil on Thursday reported a declining net profit, despite the increase in gas and oil production.
Transportation of schoolchildren: the complaint deemed admissible
Since the beginning of the school year, the Commission scolaire de la Région-de-Sherbrooke (CSRS) has charged a fee of $150 per student (up to a maximum of $300 per family) for students benefiting from school transportation at two residences, a service that the organization offers when it is able to do so.
Prior to changes to the last budget, no financial contributions were claimed.
"There is a mediation service that is proposed by the commission and I was interested," explains Ms. Lefèvre.
According to her, the CSRS has been invited to mediation and has asked for additional time to think about it.
"It's always better to talk about it, to consult and to find solutions," says Ms. Lefèvre.
The Sherbrooke Regional School Board (SSRC) did not want to comment.
The organization only indicated that mediation is part of the process in a complaint.
Imbroglio around the closure of the Hôtel-Dieu emergencies
Faced with the slingshot carried out for several months by some of the staff and several unions, including the CGT, the Minister of Health, Marisol Touraine, had decided on July 10 to "shift the timetable for the implementation of the project, and in particular the date of closure of emergencies that will not be able to intervene on November 4".
A request officially made "so as not to run any risk on the management of emergencies in Paris at the beginning of the winter" but which was also intended not to hinder the campaign of the PS a few months from the municipalities.
Despite the ministerial instructions, the closure should still be effective on that date.
Even though at the APHP, it is preferable to use the term "transformation" or "change in continuity".
As of November 4, firefighters—who carried out about a quarter of the 40,000 annual visits to the Hôtel-Dieu’s emergency room—will all have been instructed to take around thirty serious cases a day to the emergencies of other Parisian hospitals.
This date of November 4 also corresponds to the renewal of boarding schools.
At the Hôtel-Dieu, the interns specialized in emergencies will give way Monday to five interns of general medicine.
As for the transfer of internal medicine beds, it is scheduled for the course of the month.
"The emergency reception service of the Hôtel-Dieu must close as soon as possible, and for us, it's November 4," said Loïc Capron, the president of the APHP's Medical Establishment Commission (CME), which supports the management's project.
"On November 4, there will no longer be patients brought in by firefighters, only people coming by their own means," confirms Professor Jean-Yves Fagon, the medical manager of the new Hôtel-Dieu.
"But we will continue to welcome patients in an emergency," he said, arguing the permanent presence of SMUR vehicles on site to transfer serious cases.
Senior emergency physicians will remain there, he also said.
But it is up to the ARS to decide the closure of the emergency department.
"Things are done gradually," replies Nicolas Péju, spokesman for the ARS for whom, on November 4, "there will be no change in terms of service rendered."
"The minister lied to us or we lied to her," deplores the emergency doctor Gérald Kierzek.
Denied from his position as leader of the SMUR at the beginning of July for leading the slingshot against the reorganization project, he defines himself as a "whistleblower" in the face of a "cynical" decision taken by "the medical-administrative technostructure".
“They are emptying and suffocating emergencies that were renovated less than five years ago,” he said.
For him, "if the other Parisian emergency services could absorb the surplus, there would be no problem".
However, they are regularly engorged.
There are, for example, sometimes nine hours of waiting at the Lariboisière emergencies.
At the ARS, it is argued that thirty "dispatched" patients on several sites are not likely to represent an "avalanche" in the other emergency services, whose human resources have also been strengthened.
At the ARS as at the APHP, we defend the "new hospital model" already launched since 7 October and which should continue to accommodate 30 000 to 35 000 patients per year.
By the end of the year, liberal general practitioners are also expected to participate in the implementation of an "outpatient permanence".
"Where do we go if we ask people to self-diagnosize", asks Gérald Kierzek for whom "the concept of emergency not serious" is "dangerous" and marks a "medical regression".
It's going to happen as for level 3 maternity hospitals.
People aren’t crazy, they’re going to go where there’s the best care offer.
If the minister does not take action by Monday, we will change gears, warns Christophe Prudhomme, emergency doctor and member of the CGT Santé.
We are going to be even more present as part of the municipal campaign and we are going to think about presenting a list.
In the team of Nathalie Kosciusko-Morizet, Vincent Roger, UMP advisor of Paris and elected of the 4th arrondissement, clearly announces that "even if the Parisian UMP was for the maintenance of emergencies at the Hôtel-Dieu, it would be technically and financially impossible to reopen them if we return to the responsibilities".
Anne Hidalgo, the PS candidate, again recalled Monday morning on France Inter that she had voted in favor of a moratorium so that there is no closure on November 4.
If this were the case, it would mark her clear disagreement, emphasizes Bruno Julliard, his spokesman.
Even if the restructuring of the Hôtel-Dieu has its legitimacy in substance, we cannot admit it without an acceptable pattern of postponement to the other hospitals.
Obama shuts down wiretaps for the IMF and the World Bank
Barack Obama ordered the National Security Agency (NSA) to end wiretapping on the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank as part of its intelligence activities, a U.S. official said Thursday.
This decision is part of the White House’s attempt to regain control in the NSA wiretapping case after the revelations made by former analyst Edward Snowden, a refugee in Russia.
This is the first time the IMF and the World Bank’s surveillance by the intelligence agency since the beginning of the scandal.
Asked on the subject, a U.S. administration official said: “The U.S
Expressing on condition of anonymity, this official did not specify whether such surveillance had been put in place in the past.
Another official said Barack Obama had issued an order to cease these practices over the past few weeks.
This instruction was given at about the same time as the one terminating wiretaps from UN headquarters in New York.
In this context, the Senate Intelligence Committee on Thursday approved a tightening of controls on government surveillance programs but authorized their continuation.
The commission has introduced new restrictions on data that intelligence agencies are allowed to collect and imposed a five-year limit for the retention of this information.
United Nations welcomes new goals for the fight against poverty
The United Nations will now be working on a new set of goals to replace the Millennium Development Goals (MDGs), which had been put in place 12 years ago to combat global poverty.
Australia’s diplomats have played a key role in putting forward the “Sustainable Development Goals” to replace the MDGs, which will expire in 2015, ahead of the United Nations Summit on Sustainable Development that began the previous day in Rio de Janeiro.
They were included in the final preliminary draft of the document, which will be adopted by world leaders, including Ms. Gillard, during the summit.
UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon said on the eve of the summit that it is now time to “overtake national interests.”
"I am pleased that Member States have agreed to launch and appropriate the process of setting universal Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)," he added.
These SDGs will build on progress under the Millennium Development Goals and will be an integral part of the post-2015 development framework.
I will do my utmost to fulfil the mandate entrusted to me by the Member States to achieve our vision of the Sustainable Development Goals that build on the success of the MDGs.
Security-related concern in Mozambique grows at the same time as conflicts erupt between powerful personalities
At the feet of the statue of Samora Machel, Mozambique’s founding president, pissing them off, thousands gathered in central Maputo to chant peaceful slogans during a rare public protest.
“We want peace back; we want stability,” said Vanessa de Sousa, managing director of an investment firm.
Worried about the future of her country, she exchanged her working clothes for a T-shirt marked "we want security" in Portuguese before joining the crowd amassed on the capital's Independence Square on Thursday.
For two weeks, there have been almost daily reports of conflicts between government forces and Renamo, the worst clashes since the peace agreement that was signed more than 20 years ago.
Renamo was once a famous rebellious movement, initially supported by white-governed Rhodesia and then South Africa's apartheid government as part of efforts to destabilize the country's independent government.
After a peace agreement signed in 1992, it became an opposition party.
Analysts believe that the country should not fall back into an open conflict, but recent events have shaken foreign investors and the local population.
The challenge is considerable for the growing economy since the discovery of huge reserves of offshore gas and coal deposits in the northwest could attract more than US$50 billion in investment over the next few years from companies such as Rio Tinto, Vale of Brazil, Eni of Italy and Anadarko from the United States.
Frelimo, the ruling party and the main political force since 1975, and Renamo blame each other for tensions.
Renamo states that the government is at the origin of the recent clashes and launched an attack against its members in Sofala province, a traditional Renamo stronghold, on October 17.
The assaults on the former rebels then escalated when government forces attacked the bases of the Renamo and attempted to kill Afonso Dhlakama, the group’s leader, and Renamo spokesman Fernando Mazanga, according to the Financial Times.
The government blames Renamo for unleashing conflicts, and accuses it of attacking soldiers.
President Armando Guebuza has sought to minimize concerns about instability.
Guebuza told AFP, the French news agency, on Wednesday that Dhlakama considered himself a "loser" who wanted to use "all the forces he had left to try to prove that he could impose his decisions on the government."
Frelimo and Renamo insist that they both wish to avoid war.
But concern grew after Mazanga said Renamo was abandoning the 1992 peace deal.
He told the FT that he meant that the agreement was no longer respected by the Frelimo.
“We want to resume negotiations, but with the utmost seriousness,” Mazanga said.
The previous discussions between the parties have failed to ease tensions fueled by a series of conflicts this year.
"It's two great men (Guebuza and Dhlakama) who keep arguing," said Joseph Hanlon, a lecturer at the Open University and a specialist in Mozambique.
None of them knows how to negotiate well and none of them are ready to make the necessary concessions.
Renamo, which is in favor of electoral reforms, had already said that it would boycott the municipal elections to be held in November.
The presidential and legislative elections are scheduled for next year.
Some commentators have interpreted its use of force as an attempt by a sick movement to obtain concessions and a treasure of war from the government.
Renamo's share of the vote has steadily declined since 1992, while a new party, the Democratic Movement of Mozambique (MDM), which was created by a former member of Renamo, is expected to obtain a greater number of votes in elections.
Mazanga said Guebuza – who is expected to retire at the end of his second term next year – wanted to destroy democracy in the country.
“He doesn’t want a multi-party democracy, he doesn’t want transparent elections, he doesn’t want peace because he doesn’t want to leave the presidency,” Mazanga said.
It is not known exactly what Renamo's capacity is, but it carried out disruptive flash attacks on police stations and vehicles on a major North-South highway axis.
Most clashes took place in Sofala province, which is located several hundred kilometers north of Maputo, but where Beira is located, the port from which mining companies, including Rio Tinto and Vale, export coal.
In June, Rio was no longer able to use the railway for about a week after Renamo threatened to attack the railway line.
Mr. Mazanga was falsely shy when asked if the Renamo would reiterate this threat.
Renamo wanted to “warn the international community that things aren’t going well in Mozambique,” Mazanga said.
Instability has heightened the government’s frustrations, says Fernando Lima, director of Mediacoop, an independent media company, with many people also concerned about corruption, slow development and the recent wave of kidnapping.
"People think that those responsible for the future of the country are the government and the president, and that it is up to it to find solutions to the problems," he said.
Omar Sultuane, a protester, said people only wanted stability.
"No one cares about the Renamo or the Frelimo, they just want to find peace, they want to have free access to the roads," he added.
Myths and legends should be teached to children as “lifestyle models,” says the author.
Thor’s Legend might show that “brutal force doesn’t weigh in the face of the subtle cunning,” while Arthurian legends reveal the importance of having a dream.
Saying that many myths would be "too violent, far too outrageous and in some cases, far too obscene to be taught at school", Crossley-Holland advocated a "meticulous selection" of age-appropriate works.
“I think it’s great that in America myths and folklore are already part of education,” he said.
I have been advocating in favour of this project for 20 years.
He added that the fact that authors and teachers are “openly didactic” makes children “fully pick up”, with messages “sublimated” in pleasant stories.
Crossley-Holland, who translated Beowulf from the Anglo-Saxon and wrote The Penguin Book of Norse Myths and British Folk Tales, said: “You may have good intentions, but you’d rather keep them out of sight.”
Perhaps the big difference between an adult author writing for adults and an adult author writing for children is the need to offer hope.
Not that everything has to be simplified or end well, but there is an innate sense of good and evil.
And it must be sublimated; revealed through a story rather than declared.
The known basis of what you show and don't say.
A fighter from Hamas’ military wing was killed tonight and another wounded by an Israeli tank fire in the Gaza Strip, according to medical and security sources in Gaza.
According to these sources, these activists were conducting a surveillance operation in the border area between Palestinian territory and Israel when they suffered from shelling of an Israeli armored vehicle.
NSA Spying: U.S. ‘Goes Too Far’, Admits Kerry
The United States has sometimes gone "too far" when it comes to espionage, said Secretary of State John Kerry, in Washington's first confession in the midst of controversy with Europe on the massive data collection by the National Security Agency (NSA).
After ten days of scandal, revelations and denials between the United States and its European allies, this is the first time that a U.S. government official explicitly admits of controversial practices in the NSA’s interception of communications and data in Europe.
"In some cases, I concede to you, as the president did, some of these actions have gone too far and we're going to make sure it doesn't happen in the future anymore," Kerry said at a conference in London that he participated in from Washington on Thursday, Oct. 31 via video.
In his repeated intervention, in the presence of his British counterpart William Hauge, the head of U.S. diplomacy has at length justified the practices of intelligence and information gathering by the necessary fight against terrorism and the prevention of possible attacks.
Invoking the attacks of 11 September 2001, the attacks in Madrid in March 2004 and those in London in July 2005, John Kerry assured that the U.S. authorities had since foiled numerous plans for attacks, thanks to the interception of communications and the collection of information.
"We have in fact prevented planes from falling, buildings from exploding and people being murdered, because we were able to be aware of these projects," he said.
And, John Kerry told the Europeans, "I assure you that in this process innocent people have not been deceived."
However, we are trying to gather information.
"And yes, in some cases, it went too far inappropriately," admitted the secretary of state, who had already had to comment on this international scandal during a tour last week in Paris, London and Rome.
He assured Thursday evening that President Obama was "resolved in trying to clarify and that he was conducting a review of these practices so that no one feels deceived."
But the controversy between Americans and Europeans has continued to swallow this week with new revelations in the press.
According to the Washington Post, the NSA was intercepting data from hundreds of millions of users of Google and Yahoo.
The newspaper, which cites documents obtained from former NSA consultant Edward Snowden, claims that the "MUSCULAR" program, and conducted with British NSA counterpart, the GCHQ, allows these two agencies to retrieve data from the fiber optics used by internet giants.
According to one of the documents, some 181 million items had been collected during last January alone – ranging from metadata on emails, to text elements or audio or video documents.
These interceptions would take place outside the United States.
However, Yahoo and Google have denied any connection to these practices.
For ten days, several major newspapers in France, Germany, Spain or Italy have revealed that the NSA has massively intercepted data and communications from U.S. allies and their leaders, including German Chancellor Angela Merkel.
Faced with the anger of European states and while leaks in the U.S. press claimed that the U.S. president was not aware of such wiretaps, Barack Obama refused to speak about it, invoking national security.
On the other hand, the boss of the powerful NSA, General Keith Alexander, denied him that his intelligence agency has captured tens of millions of communications from European citizens.
He even returned the bullet to the European intelligence services who allegedly seized these communications, before supplying them to the NSA.
This would concern "military operations" in countries where these NATO allies are cooperating with the United States and this would absolutely not target Europe, said General Alexander.
And the controversy spread to Asia on Friday.
Indonesia summoned Australia's ambassador, whose mission is accused of being used by the Americans as part of an extensive international espionage network that has also sparked China's ire.
Paris Saint-Germain will receive Lorient on Friday without its asselt, Sweden’s Zlatan Ibrahimovic, who is injured, the Ligue 1 club said in a statement.
PSG, leader of the championship ahead of Monaco with the difference of goals, broadcast the list of players selected for this match of the 12th day, without the 32-year-old striker.
The statement simply indicates that he is "injured", without specifying the nature of the injury.
However, he suffered from inflammation of the left knee when he returned from selection, at the beginning of the month.
Ceremony in memory of the dead crematized
The crematorium of Père Lachaise organizes, at 11 a.m., a secular ceremony, in memory of all the deceased crematized in his establishment, during the year.
Anne Hidalgo, socialist candidate for mayor of Paris must attend, and will then explain, at a press conference, his proposals in terms of funerary.
To follow the ceremony, click above.
For their own funerals, more and more French choose cremation, rather than burial: 53%, according to an Ipsos study carried out on September 6 and 7 among 1009 people, instead of 47%.
For the funeral of a loved one, it is the opposite: the French prefer burial (53% against 47%).
If they have the misfortune to lose a child, they are only 15% to choose cremation.
In his book Death in Ashes, Damien Le Guay, philosopher and vice-president of the National Ethics Committee for Funeral, insists on the "violence" of cremation, for the survivors.
There is, with cremation, "a violence done to the beloved body", which will be "reduced to a pile of ashes" in a very short time, and not after a process of decomposition, which "would accompany the phases of mourning".
There is also "symbolic violence", which stems from the "erasure of singularity and distinctive signs" reduced to the "anonymity" of ashes.
Why is accepted without difficulty in some Nordic and Protestant countries still badly experienced in France?
"Because cremation is a new fact," says Marie-Frédérique Bacqué, president of the Society of Thanatology and author of the book Approvicate Death.
It has only been tolerated since 1963 by the Catholic Church, which has limited attempts to tame it.
Approvicing cremation is for François Michaud-Nérard, director general of the Funeral Services of the City of Paris, to provide for the deceased a ceremony as dignified as that to which he would be entitled with a burial.
Ipsos' annual survey shows a very strong attachment to the organization of the ceremony.
77% of French people want one, for their loved ones, whether religious (53%), or civil (24%).
Now, 73% of people who have chosen a cremation want the organization of a ceremony.
66% of atheists or non-believers also desire it.
Crematoriums have adapted to this evolution of society.
"For about ten years, they have been trying to avoid the violence of an expectation, without doing anything, for an hour and a half, immediately followed by the remission of the ashes," observes Mr. Michaud-Nérard.
70% of establishments now offer masters of ceremonies, who proceed, in the presence of the body, to the following ritual: to welcome the assembly, to appoint the deceased, to connect him to others, to evoke him, to give meaning to his death, to organize the farewell.
This is the case at the crematorium of Père Lachaise, since 1998.
Ceremonial masters are often people who have converted.
There are also ancient Catholic priests.
It is in this context that the crematorium of Père Lachaise organizes, since 2010, the day of La Toussaint, several ceremonies of remembrance, secular and non-religious, to which it invites the families of people who have been crematized in the year.
For the second consecutive year, one of these ceremonies is broadcast enligne, to the attention of those who could not make the trip.
The crematorium allowed us to relay it.
In La Seyne, rehousing due to rats turns to controversy
At the beginning of the week, a family left their apartment on the ground floor of the Fructidor D2 building, due to the presence of rats.
Faced with the urgency of the situation, the director of the Terres du Sud Habitat office proposed, on an exceptional and provisional basis, to relocate the couple and his three children in a new T4.
However, the family refuses "for financial reasons" and the situation is blocked.
The case has taken on exaggerated proportions, says Joël Canapa, director of the office.
Rats have always been in cities.
The deratization company carries out twice crossings a year, and in addition, we intervene, at our expense, at each request of the inhabitants.
Since the end of last winter, we have carried out 64 interventions.
The neighborhood isn't abandoned, and the rats don't swear in the city.
For reasons of environmental protection and public health, deratization products are four times less effective than in the past, certainly, but for two rats, we are not going to kill a kid.
"We didn't inviate the rats at home," defends the father who left his accommodation to settle at the hotel.
We are soon penniless and we are waiting for a new accommodation, at the same rate as the old.
The Tokyo Stock Exchange ends down 0.88%, News
The Tokyo Stock Exchange ended down on Friday despite good Chinese manufacturing statistics.
The odds suffered from the decline of the dollar against the yen, prejudicial to exporting values and the warning on its annual result launched by Sony Thursday.
The Nikkei index lost 126.37 points (0.88%) to 14,201.57 and the Topix dropped 11.23 points (0.94%) to 1,183.03.
Sony has fallen by more than 11% to 1,668 yen.
A house prey to the flames in Old Quebec
A fire that broke out Friday noon in Old Quebec was quickly subdued.
More than thirty firefighters were dispatched to 9 rue Hébert.
The flames broke out in a house of three dwellings spread over four floors located behind the courtyard of the Séminaire de Québec.
All tenants were absent during the fire.
The fire was subdued an hour after the arrival of the firefighters, many of whom intervened.
As soon as our first firefighters arrived, they really saw apparent smoke.
"Then the alarms succeeded each other, because it took reinforcements to attack the building, because it's buildings glued here," explained France Loiselle, spokeswoman for Quebec City firefighters.
An investigation is underway to find the cause of this fire.
Ben Greenman: Tenth Anniversary of the New York Comedy Festival: The New Yorker
We could say that the city of New York is the place where the stand-up was born in America: almost 100 years ago, vaudeville actor Frank Fay, who held the role of master of ceremonies at the Palace Theatre, on Broadway, was beginning to tell jokes directly in front of the audience, in the tone of the conversation.
Fay’s innovation has continued over the years, until it was more recently taken over by the New York Comedy Festival.
Created and supervised by Caroline Hirsch, the founder of stand-up club Carolines, the festival celebrates its tenth anniversary this year, with more than 60 shows presented in small clubs and major theaters.
“Most headliners have emerged in the Carolinas, and have had great success, until they became too famous to play in a club,” Hirsch said.
We created this festival in order to continue to work with them.
This year’s event will feature Wanda Sykes, Kathy Griffin and Bill Maher and the presentation of “Stand Up for Heroes”, an evening of comedy and music for the benefit of veterans, organized at Madison Square Garden, where Bruce Springsteen, Jon Stewart, Roger Waters and Bill Cosby will be present.
The festival has grown, as has the world of comedy.
Many of this year’s festival-participating comedians went through non-traditional circuits, for example presenting shows on small TV channels, such as Comedy Central, FX and Spike.
Nick Kroll made his appearance on the front of the stage in a sitcom on a cable channel (“The League” on the theme of cheerfully lascive fantasy football of the FXX channel) and now has his own sketch show on Comedy Central.
Jenny Slate appeared on Saturday Night Live and was a cast of the "Parks and Recreation" series, yet she is best known for her viral video series "Marcel the Shell with Shoes On".
Kroll and Slate, along with other young actors with a singular voice (surrealist pessimist Anthony Jeselnik, the ironic defender of racial justice W. Kamau Bell), are pure products of the decentralized world of American comedy.
One of the festival’s main attraction factors this year will be an interview: David Steinberg chatting with Larry David.
Steinberg began as a stand-up comediant, but he became an accomplished film and television director, as well as an unficious comedy historian.
Between 2005 and 2007 he hosted a show on TV Land called "Sit Down Comedy with David Steinberg".
The rendezvous takes place at City Hall in downtown Manhattan.
“The city is definitely in the comedic DNA of all of Larry’s work,” Steinberg said.
He told me that, when he was here, sometimes he walked down an alley between two buildings and said to himself, if I lost all my money, maybe I'll come and live here.
Oil prices continue to drop and move closer to $96 per barrel
Oil prices continued to fall on Friday as concerns about the size of inventories offset a report showing that China’s electricity-hungry manufacturing sector is strengthening.
The price of U.S. reference crude for delivery in December fell from 14 cents to $96.24 per barrel at the end of the morning in Europe during electronic exchanges on the New York Mercantile Exchange (NYMEX).
The contract lost 39 cents on Thursday, leaving it with a decline of 5.8% for the month of October.
Abundant crude oil supply has weighed on the price in recent weeks.
The U.S. Department of Energy reported Wednesday that U.S. supplies grew 4.1 million barrels last week.
Over five weeks, supplies increased by more than 25 million barrels.
But two reports on China’s manufacturing sector arrived on Friday, which show business growth, suggest an increase in demand.
This suggests that China's economic recovery could continue to strengthen after growth rebounded to 7.8% in the third quarter, after a decline that had not been seen for two decades in the previous quarter.
Brent crude, a benchmark for international crude already used by U.S. refineries, lost 26 cents to fall to $108.58 per barrel on the Inter-Continental Exchange (ICE) in London.
Imposing the right to Abortion in Texas
A Federal Court of Appeal has reinstated in Texas certain restrictions on the right to intentional termination of pregnancy that had been blocked by a trial judge this week.
This judgment means that a law passed in July in this state and largely questioning the right to abortion will be able to come into force.
The judgment rendered by the Court of Appeal means that physicians who will practice abortions will have to have a "privilege of admission" to local hospitals.
Admission privilege is the right of a physician, by virtue of his or her status as a nursing member of a hospital, to admit a patient to a hospital or medical center in order to dispense a diagnosis or treatment.
IVG advocates argue that the contents of the law may result in an immediate closure of one-third of the state's clinics because these clinics have failed to obtain this "admission privilege" for their practitioners.
In total, nearly 22,000 women would be deprived of access to these establishments.
On Monday, a trial judge ruled on the eve of the entry into force of the law that the provisions dealing with admission privilege were unconstitutional.
But the Texas Attorney General, Republican Greg Abbott, who is running for governor, has asked the Court of Appeal to quash the trial ruling that blocked law enforcement.
A plenary hearing on this issue is scheduled for January.
The New Star has achieved a comeback, on the audience side, with 1.3 million viewers behind their TV station, or 5.6% audience share.
Was the show at the rendezvous?
Some 10,000 candidates presented themselves at the auditions of this 10th season, to face the jury: Maurane, the villain Sinclair, the perché André Manoukian and Olivier Bas.
On Twitter, viewers shared their comments.
Passed the excitement of finding their favorite program, many were disappointed.
But why so much disappointment?
No candidate seems to have really scored during this first bonus.
As every year, the first audition shows brew a wide selection of aspiring singers, for the better and sometimes for the worse.
However, on Twitter, we want show, emotion, originality, music...
Luckily, a few have found their account.
Many of them have attempted the New Star, and sometimes even went all the way.
Behind the television screens, the vocations of a successful singer are still there, season after season.
Others just have the ambition to make people laugh.
Court blocks NYPD’s control and search policy
A federal appeals court on Thursday blocked the decision of a judge demanding changes in the New York Police Department’s control and search program and divested it off the case.
The U.S. Court of Appeals (Second Circuit) said Judge Shira Scheindlin's decision was suspended until the end of the city's appeal.
The judge had ruled in August that the city had violated the Constitution following the implementation of its programme to control and question citizens.
The city appealed its findings and corrective orders, including a decision to assign a comptroller to assist the police department in altering its policy and its associated training program.
The Court of Appeal heard arguments about the request for suspension on Tuesday.
The Court of Appeal stated that the judge had to divest the record because it violated the judges' code of conduct by compromising the need for a judge to avoid any appearance of bias, in part because of a series of media interviews and public statements in which she publicly reacted to the court's criticisms.
The judge had ruled that police officers violated the civil rights of tens of thousands by wrongly targeting black and Hispanic men as part of their control and search programs.
It has appointed an external controller to oversee changes, including policy reform, training and supervision, and ordered the establishment of a pilot programme to test cameras worn on the body in neighbourhoods where the largest number of controls take place.
In August, New York City agreed to end the practice of retaining the names and addresses of those whose cases are rejected after a police check.
The hearing of the appeal lodged by the city should take place after March 14, 2014.
The tactics of control and search have been criticized by many civil rights defenders.
This system of control and excavation has existed for decades in some form, but the number of recorded controls increased dramatically under the administration of Independent Mayor Michael Bloomberg, reaching its highest historical level in 2011 with 684 3330 controls that concerned mostly black and Hispanic men.
Legal proceedings were instituted in 2004 by four men, all from minorities, and turned into a collective action.
Proponents advocating changes in the NYPD’s control and search program say they’ll end unfair practices, allow for more effective and trusted police forces, and will be able to change the way police departments use politics.
Opponents say that these changes undermine the morale of the police without lowering crime, would amount to a waste of money and would not solve the wider problem of police forces under pressure after the number of police officers has been sharply reduced over the past decade.
The judge pointed out that it did not put an end to control and search practices, which are constitutional, but that it was reforming how the NYPD implemented its controls.
Tenants have to vote, because each of them pays an average of $100 per month on the price of housing that goes into taxes to the City.
When the landlord pays $5000 in municipal taxes, he divides this amount between each dwelling.
We're forced to raise rents to help us pay new taxes.
When we vote, we send a clear message.
We need to make a difference in the city.
All the candidates told us that they'd tackle the outrageous debt.
We no longer have the means to afford fireworks at $100 000, lots of 4 to 5 million dollars in case...
Go vote is the best way to express yourself and say it's enough.
End of service for the chef of the Elysée
Entered in the 500 m2 of Elysian kitchens as committed after working in embassies, the cook climbed all the ladders to become chef, nine years ago.
He served six French presidents, from Georges Pompidou to François Hollande via Nicolas Sarkozy, including on their holiday sites, and nurtured an incalculable number of the greats of this world in a quasi-annonymity that he breaks only today, at the time of retirement, at 60.
The man with an opened face, bleached skull and intellectual glasses, born on October 24, 1953 at La Ferté-Saint-Aubin holds a pastry CAP.
Her mother was a cook herself in a castle in Sologne.
"I've gone from one castle the other!", ironically Bernard Vaussion, who knows not only the manias of the six presidents for whom he officiated, but also their wives and some of their illustrious guests.
"It's always the head of state or his companion who chooses the menus," he recalls.
"Ms. Chirac was a little more involved, she came directly to the kitchen", to the point that she had banned women in the kitchens of the Elysée!
Don't displease heads of state who showed a relative indifference to terrestrial foods, "they are all greedy", denounces Gently Bernard Vaussion.
Jacques Chirac certainly had "a slightly more developed appetite".
But with Nicolas Sarkozy, who had removed the cheese, the plates returned empty!
As for François Hollande, who restored the cheese, "it's someone who likes to eat" and "there's not much he doesn't like."
The current head of state, who regained the lost pounds before the presidential campaign, has "no demand for a specific regime".
Bernard Vaussion also remembers the pressure on his shoulders, especially during state dinners at the Elysée: it is necessary to serve "between 200 and 250 people in about an hour".
The next night is almost white.
These people wouldn't understand if there were mistakes.
The now former chef of the kitchens of the Elysée, who had a drink Tuesday evening with his team, his family and friends, leaves with a pinch in the heart "François Hollande has come to greet me".
He's said some praise.
"His human side came out," he said.
The leader will be replaced by his deputy, Guillaume Gomez, 35, who has been for seventeen years at the Elysée.
Fire in a house built in 1850 in Quebec
A historic house built in 1850 fell prey to the flames in Old Quebec Friday noon.
The four-unit building, located at 15 rue Hébert, was the subject of three alarms with the fire department.
The fire broke out at the front, but rose on all three floors of the building.
France Voiselle, spokesman for the Quebec City Fire Protection Service, said the rapid intervention of firefighters has made it possible to avoid the spread of flames to adjacent buildings, a major challenge for this sector of the city where buildings are built closely.
The burned-out house was home to four dwellings, but no tenants were on the scene at the time of the fire.
The damage was limited, even if the water caused damage.
An investigation will be conducted to determine its origin, but the sappers have dismissed the thesis of a criminal act.
Wall Street Wolf: Release of the Second Trailer
Martin Scorsese lets out some images of his next film whose title role, that of an ambitious New York broker, is embodied by Leonardo DiCaprio.
Forget that you're reasonable and get your tickets out.
Leonardo DiCaprio will blow you in his role of crazy trader.
Martin Scorsese tells us the true story of a "Long Island stock market broker, who refuses to collaborate with the authorities as part of a broad corruption case on Wall Street."
In the 80's, Jordan Belfort mounted a scam that would make him a rich man, very wealthy.
The opportunity to follow his incredible ascent, but also the vertiginous fall that will follow.
Limitless, the man chains parties, women, drugs.
Decadence and excess of all kinds are the key words of this crazy story.
The Scorsese-DiCaprio couple seems to have regained the magic that united them in Shutter Island.
The release of the new Scorsese has been delayed because of its too long assembly.
Looks like the movie finally lasts for 2h45.
In theaters on December 25, he will be able to participate narrowly in the Oscar race.
Coulson used phone hacking to verify a pipe
Former News of the World editor Andy Coulson reportedly used “phone hacking, surveillance and confrontation” to try to confirm a bogus pipe on an affair involving the then-practising Interior Minister Charles Clarke.
Prosecutor Andrew Edis QC told the Old Bailey court that News of the World heard a false rumor in May 2005 that Clarke had a relationship with Hannah Pawlby, his "pretty special adviser."
It was reported to the court that the newspaper instructed Glenn Mulcaire, a private investigator, to hack into Pawlby’s voicemail and “permanently crash in front of his door,” but Coulson also called her and left her with voice messages.
“The accusation suggests that Mr. Coulson, who is now the editor of NotW, is not the kind of man to stay in front of people’s homes hoping to surprise them, it’s the kind of man to like presenting history to people to see what they’re saying,” Edis said.
He said NotW used three ways to investigate topics: phone hacking, surveillance and confrontation.
The editor-in-chief is personally involved in the third.
It is obvious that he is aware of the second, surveillance, it cannot be otherwise.
But what about the first?
Is he aware of phone hacking?
He says otherwise, but we say, “Of course it is.”
Rumors about an affair involving Clarke were picked up first by NotW’s editorial office when a source, experiencing sexual attraction to Miss Pawlby, said she had been told, “Don’t waste your time with her, she’s with Charles.”
A band, on which were recorded pirated voice messages on his phone on at least three occasions, was seized from Mulcaire's home in August 2006.
Investigators also found seizures on the private investigator's computer about Miss Pawlby and her sier stored in a "Projects" file.
During when the detective investigated her, Miss Pawlby's grandparents received anonymous calls where they were asked for information about her, Edis added.
In the meantime, former Chief Reporter Neville Thurlbeck and former reporter James Weatherup oversaved Miss Pawlby’s every action.
In a voical message he left him on June 18, 2005, Coulson said: “I have a story we plan to publish tomorrow that I would really like to talk to Charles about.”
Edis explained that Coulson’s involvement in history has followed the same pattern as with other important men, such as former Interior Minister David Blunkett.
The jury heared Thursday that Coulson confronted Mr. Blunkett about an affair with a married woman while he himself frequented co-defendant Rebekah Brooks, who was also married at that time.
Coulson and Brooks denie conspiring with others to hack phones between October 3, 2000 and August 9, 2006.
Mulcaire, Thurlbeeck and Weatherup have admitted to doing phone hacks.
Barack Obama Receives Iraqi Prime Minister in Outbreak
The U.S. president was scheduled to receive Iraqi Prime Minister Nouri Al Maliki on Friday, November 1, 2013, seeking help from the United States to combat the strongest wave of violence in five years.
In October, Iraq experienced its deadliest month in five and a half years, according to figures released by the Iraqi authorities on Friday.
964 people died in the violence in October, including 855 civilians, 65 police officers and 44 soldiers, and 1,600 were injured.
The U.N. gives even a higher death toll with 979 dead and 1,902 injured.
This violence is increasingly deadly despite enhanced security measures and large-scale military operations launched for months by the Shiite-dominated government of Nouri Al Maliki.
The total number of deaths in October is highest since April 2008, when 1,073 people were killed.
About two years after the withdrawal of U.S. troops, the level of violence raises fears of a new conflagration as neighboring Syria is plagued by a civil war.
Bombs hit markets, mosques, weddings and funerals.
Men are shot dead in the middle of the street or even at home, and security forces are also the target of frequent attacks.
The growing discontent of the Sunni minority, in power under Saddam Hussein, who is complaining about being politically marginalized and being the target of unfair arrests, has favored this outbreak of violence.
On Friday, new violence killed four people in northern Iraq, in the aftermath of the deaths of at least 26 people in a series of attacks, including the explosion of five car bombs north of Baghdad.
Much of the violence has been blamed on the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIL), a group affiliated with Al-Qaeda (Sunni extremists), also involved in the civil war in Syria.
Nouri Al Maliki wants a "global war" against Al-Qaeda.
The violence is at the center of Prime Minister Nouri Al Maliki’s U.S
At the time, the U.S. president, elected on the promise to end military engagement in this country, had drawn an optimistic picture of the situation.
Since his arrival in Washington on Wednesday, Nouri Al Maliki has multiplied meetings with the executive, and congressional officials.
On Thursday, he advocated that the international community conduct a "third world war" against Al-Qaida.
The principle of increased security assistance to Iraq is supported by influential Republican and Democratic senators.
But the latter have also criticized Nouri Al Maliki, a Shiite, attributinging him the partial responsibility for the resumption of violence by his "sectarian and authoritarian policy".
“They have also demanded that Barack Obama make Nouri Al Maliki understand that “Iran’s pernicious influence within the Iraqi government is a serious issue in our bilateral relationship.”
Beijing accuses Uyghur group of Tiananmen attack
China’s chief of internal security on Friday accused a group of Xinjiang Uyghur separatists to be behind the car bomb attack that killed five people in Tiananmen Square in central Beijing on Monday.
The vehicle, an SUV, rushed into the mob that stood in the famous square of the Chinese capital, a symbol of the murderous repression carried out in 1989, and caught fire killing its three passengers and two passers-by.
Meng Jianzhu, a member of the Political Bureau in charge of internal security issues, accused the Islamic movement of East Turkestan of instigating this attack.
Many Uyghurs, a Turkish-speaking minority living in Xinjiang, call for eastern Turkestan China.
For the Chinese government, this movement is responsible for the frequent eruptions of violence that are taking place there driven by separatistic demands.
“The violent terrorist incident that occurred in Beijing was organized and premeditated,” Meng told Hong Kong channel Phoenix TV.
“The group held behind-the-scenes was the Islamic movement of East Turkestan,” he added, his remarks being echoed by the China News Agency.
Chinese police have identified the driver of the vehicle whose name suggests he is of Uyghur origin and said that his wife and mother were with him in the car.
The latter also contained gasoline-filled containers and a flag with Orthodox religious inscriptions.
The incident left 42 people injured.
The East Turkestan's Islamic movement is considered a terrorist organization by the United States and United Nations.
The Vatican is investigating contraception, divorce and homosexuality
The Vatican has undertaken to conduct a major survey around the world on how parishes handle sensitive records such as contraception, divorce, and same-sex couples.
The survey asks participants how priests take care of gay couples and their children, and how they treat the men and women who live together without being united by the bonds of marriage.
The survey was sent in mid-October to each National Bishops' Conference, with instructions to get as many answers as possible.
The information will be used for a major gathering on the family that Pope Francis plans to organize next year.
The news was reported Thursday by the "National Catholic Reporter", an independent Catholic newspaper of the United States.
A spokeswoman for the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops confirmed that the document was authentic and that each bishop would decide on the appropriate way to survey parishioners.
In the United Kingdom, bishops published the poll on the Internet asking Catholics to participate.
DRC: the army attacks the last stronghold of the rebels
The irreducibles of the M23, i.e. a few hundred fighters, were entrenched at nearly 2000 meters above sea level on the agricultural hills of Chanzu, Runyonyi and Mbuzi, close to Bunagana and Jomba, two localities located about 80 km north of Goma, the capital of North Kivu province.
"It hasn't stopped since this morning, fighting continues despite the night," said a resident of Jomba, who was reached by phone at around 12h30, and according to whom a girl was wounded by gunfire in the morning.
“The intensity of the clashes has diminished a bit,” he added, “it appears that the FARDC Armed Forces of the DRC have repelled the rebels a bit.”
According to this witness wishing to remain anonymous, the soldiers had "spent the night" in Jomba before rising to the forehead for a "raking operation".
There were markedly heard behind him crackling of light weapons.
From Bunagana, political stronghold and last stronghold of the rebellion fallen Wednesday, an AFP journalist could hear detonations of heavy weapons.
According to a source at the United Nations Stabilization Mission of the DRC, the fighting entered "in a final phase", the FARDC having "encircled the positions of the residual M23 to dislodge them".
Since the resumption of clashes between the March 23 Movement (M23) and the army on Friday, MONUSCO has not directly participated in the fighting, but it has provided government troops with decisive support for intelligence, observation and planning.
In the late afternoon, several dozens of soldiers, well supplied with ammunition, climbed towards the front line.
They were armed with Kalashnikov and rocket launchers.
A little earlier, on the road leading to Bunagana, a border crossing with Uganda, soldiers assisted by civilians loaded a multiple rocket launcher mounted on a brand-new FARDC truck, to ensure the succession of another craft plundering the positions of the M23 on the hills.
The Congolese president, Joseph Kabila, called on Wednesday evening once again the rebels to "wilfully demobilize" under penalty of being disarmed "by force", but has left open the Kampala process, where Kinshasa and the M23 have been talking since December without result for the moment.
On the spot, the political leaders of the M23 hope to reach an agreement.
“We’ve finished the talks, it’s up to Uganda’s mediation to organize the signing,” said Roger Lumbala, vice president of the M23 delegation, without this information being confirmed immediately on the government side.
The M23 was born from a mutiny, in April 2012, of former rebels, mainly Tutsi, integrated into the army in 2009 after a peace agreement.
The UN and Kinshasa regularly accuse Uganda and Rwanda of supporting the M23, which Kigali and Kampala refute.
North Kivu is one of DRC’s most densely populated regions, and its basement is full of coveted mineral resources.
In Bunagana, on the Ugandan side of the border, where about 5000 people had taken refuge between Monday and Wednesday according to the UN, the movement back to the DRC that started in the morning reversed at midday.
"This morning we went back to our fields, but the military told us to withdraw," Imelda Nyirankusi, surrounded by her nine children, including an infant on his back, told AFP.
We have the impression that gunshots are getting closer to the populations.
In the early evening, dozens of residents crossed the border, some with a mattress on their heads, to spend the night in Uganda.
The musical, this beast of the stage
Classic, sophisticated, popular, in English or French, sung and dared productions take off.
Following Broadway and London, Paris finally found its voice.
It is a revolution: the French musical asserts itself as a successful genre.
For a long time, from Notre-Dame de Paris to Mozart the rock opera, most of the attempts were entangled in ridicule.
And successes like Cabaret or Les Misérables carried the halo of mystery.
Jean-Luc Choplin's arrival at the Châtelet and Stage­ ­Entertainment in Mogador changed the game.
Today, these two scenes wield titles like hit hits.
The first picks up, this Christmas, its excellent My Fair Lady and announces for the following the worldwide creation of "An American in Paris".
In Mogador, "Beauty and the Beast" may well be one of the successes of the season.
On the other stages, musical performances such as "1789", "Lovers of the Bastille" and small musicals such as "Disco" or "Life and Times" put themselves in line with success and quality.
Patrick Niedo, author of the reference book "Stories of musicals" and speaker at the Théâtre du Châtelet, analyzes the reasons for the hard-conquered success.
How do French musicals evolve?
Supply has multiplied.
There are first the "musical shows" that evolve according to the producers...
Some use beautiful projections of images and a true history as in 1789, the Lovers of the Bastille.
Others make a fifteen-year leap backwards under the pretext that you can serve pretty much anything when you have Matt ­Pokora headliners.
Afterwards, we have the musicals with the sumptuous productions of the Châtelet that make us relive the golden age of Broadway and discover the universe of Stephen Sondheim, the greatest living composer.
Stage ­Entertainment popularizes Anglo-Saxon musicals of variety by adapting them in French.
The independent French producers are trying a ­pervible.
But ongoing projects such as "Rent", "The Kiss of the Spider Woman" or "The Awakening of Spring" are struggling to be financed.
Do we have enough artists capable like on Broadway of singers, playing and dancing?
In Paris, when there are eight musical shows in a season, it's a great year.
We have 200 talented artists who turn projects into projects.
In the United States, the cradle of the musical, it's very different.
Young people are trained in these professions in many schools.
There are many jobs between regional theaters, tours, Broadway, the off-Broadway...
The pool of talents is as large as the number of positions to be filled.
Why are provincial tours so successful?
With the exception of opera, the province remains the poor relative of culture in France.
Few plays go on tour and this is often boulevard theatre for adults and not for young adults.
Musical shows fill this lack.
It's these same young people who love reality TV and what it produces as ephemeral stars...
Offering beautiful (often talented) young men headlining a musical show is the assurance of a craze of these young girls and often of their entire family.
The Facebook pages of these shows are skilfully maintained by professionals who answer questions.
These shows are eagerly awaited when they arrive at a provincial Zenith.
The mayonnaise went up to Paris and tasted by the provincials.
The show is designed to enter into all the major cinemas in France in the same form as in Paris.
A seriously injured in an accident with a ghost driver in the ring of Brussels
A road accident caused a serious injury, Friday morning around 5h30, in the inner ring of Brussels near Hoeilaart, in the direction of Waterloo.
A driver's car was hit by a phantom driver, who's unharmed to her.
The ring has been closed to traffic, until 9h00, at the location of the accident, while the road is cleared and an expert determines the circumstances of the accident.
This situation, however, caused little traffic jam because the accident took place at the Heileaart exit.
