NSA revelations increase corporate paranoia over state surveillance
On a beautiful day at the end of August, a German police helicopter flew over the British Consulate in Frankfurt, Germany’s financial capital, at low altitude.
On the orders of the Federal Office for the Protection of the Constitution (BfV), the German National Intelligence Agency, its mission was to photograph the roof of the American outpost, located less than 5 km from the European Central Bank and the Bundesbank.
The German media explain that the BfV hoped to identify the presence of hearing antennas and that the operation provoked an exchange between the United States and the German Foreign Ministry in Berlin.
James Clapper, director of U.S. intelligence services, again insisted in September that the U.S. does not use its foreign intelligence capabilities to “steal the trade secrets of foreign companies on behalf of U.S. companies in order to increase international competitiveness or increase its profits.”
But since Edward Snowden, the consultant turned whistleblower, began unveiling his inexhaustible treasure trove of information on U.S. surveillance secrets, European governments and business leaders don’t know if the director is to believe on the floor.
Reports that the U.S. National Security Agency spied on Brazilian oil company Petrobras and had access to data held by U.S. providers of cloud storage services, including Google and Yahoo, have more than ever sparked corporate paranoia over state surveillance.
The revelation that German Chancellor Angela Merkel’s phone had been hacked, perhaps even for a decade, was the drop of water that overflowed the vase.
If Europe’s most powerful person can be targeted, then corporate executives are surely also potential targets.
Snowden has made transparent the intensive collaboration between U.S. intelligence services and businesses.
I believe it is conceivable that this data will be used in their mutual interest.
“Germany needs to wake up,” says Oliver Grün, president of BITMi, which represents Germany’s IT SMEs.
German companies believe that the U.S. poses a risk as big as China when it comes to industrial espionage and data theft, according to an investigation published in July by consulting firm EY.
However, in all the documents unveiled by Mr. Snowden, there is no indication to date that the U.S. has passned trade secrets from foreign companies to U.S. companies.
Politicians have expressed concern that the EU does not have sufficient computer and Internet capabilities and believe that it should strive to reduce its dependence on the United States.
Business leaders are skeptical about it.
A member of the German parliament says that we should create a German Google.
I can only close my eyes and slowly reopen them...
“It’s not the right way,” said Hasso Plattner, president of Germany’s enterprise software company, SAP.
If we wanted a strong European IT industry, then we shouldn't let it die 20 years ago.
Everything is subsidized in Germany, from coal to cars to farmers.
Anything but the IT industry.
Yet the scale and sophistication of U.S. spying agencies exposed by Snowden’s revelations has been a shock to some companies that previously thought that China posed the biggest risk in terms of surveillance.
A big change is happening in cloud computing since European officials have become aware of the fact that data stored in the United States is governed by the laws of this jurisdiction and therefore potentially vulnerable.
According to a survey conducted by the Cloud Security Alliance, a professional organization, some 10% of non-U.S. members gave up their plans to use a U.S. provider of cloud storage services after revelations about the U.S. data mining program Prism.
Jim Snabe, co-director of SAP, said: “Our customers ask us today a question they didn’t ask us a year ago: Where is my data stored and can you guarantee that it will remain physically in that jurisdiction?”
Many German leaders indicate that the latest reports are only a confirmation of what they already knew, namely that powerful states want to steal their most valuable secrets and that this data must therefore be kept at all costs.
It’s no surprising that there’s economic espionage.
There's always been.
“It’s been a recurring topic for many years and nothing has fundamentally changed as part of the current discussion,” said Kurt Bock, managing director of chemical-producing company BASF.
Americans are spying on us commercially and industrially just as we spy on them, because it is in the national interest to defend our businesses.
Business executives are generally reluctant to reveal the preventive measures they have put in place, in case this leaves the advantage to an opponent.
Large corporations have long haunted the message that it’s imprudent to pick up a free USB drive at a trade show or leave a laptop unattended in a hotel room.
Ulrich Hackenberg, a member of Audi’s board of directors, says the collection of mobile phones prior to board meetings, so they can’t be used as remote listening devices, has been a common practice for years.
The BfV of Germany advises its executives to consider using simple prepaid mobile phones while traveling abroad because of the risk of violating the integrity of smartphones.
Prepaid mobile phones are then discarded.
However, there are concerns that small and medium-sized enterprises remain vulnerable to piracy and surveillance.
In Germany, many SMEs are world leaders in their particular niche.
“SMEs often lack the experience, staff and financial resources to effectively protect their trade secrets against unauthorized access,” the BfV said in a report.
The United States alerts its own companies to economic espionage activities carried out by other countries.
The U.S. National Intelligence Estimate (NIE) in February cited France, along with Russia and Israel, culprits in the second degree of carrying out acts of hacking economic intelligence, behind China, according to The Washington Post.
A member of the board of directors of a well-known German company believed that when it comes to economic espionage, "the French are the worst."
Bernard Squarcini, former French director of domestic intelligence, DCRI, said in an interview this month: "Services know perfectly well that all countries, even if they cooperate in the fight against terrorism, spy on their allies."
Parents of a teenager from Georgia, who died in unusual circumstances, believe their son was murdered
Parents of a teenager from Georgia, whose body was found in a wrestling mat in his high school’s gym, believe their son was murdered, the family’s lawyer said Thursday.
Kendrick Johnson of Valdosta, Georgia, was found on Jan. 11 stuck in a leaning rug standing behind the bleachers in his high school gym.
Investigators from the Sheriff of Lowndes County concluded that Johnson had died under unusual circumstances, but the 17-year-old boy’s family disputed these findings.
“They’re absolutely certain that their son has been murdered,” Benjamin Crump, the lawyer representing Kenneth and Jacquelyn Johnson, told FoxNews.com.
They never thought he was dead as stated in the sheriff's conclusions.
“They think it defies all logic, the laws of physics as well as the common sense,” Crump added.
They are persuaded that these conclusions were delivered for the sole purpose of covering the person(s) responsible for the death of their son.
“They sent their son to school with a binder and they were returned to them in a mortuary bag,” he added.
U.S. Attorney Michael Moore said Thursday that he was conducting an official inquest into Johnson’s death, noting that several important questions remain unanswered.
What is the cause of death?
Is his death the result of a criminal act?
Moore spoke at a press conference Thursday afternoon.
I'll follow up on the facts, wherever they lead.
My goal is to discover the truth.
“I consider that there is a sufficient basis” to open an official investigation, he said.
Moore told reporters that the first autopsy indicated that Johnson had died by “positional asphyxiation.”
However, a second autopsy identified another cause of death, according to Moore.
"Several questions still deserve an answer or confirmation," he said.
Moore added that if he uncovers evidence to justify a civil or criminal investigation into Johnson’s death, he will ask the FBI to conduct it.
The representative of the Lowndes County Sheriff's Office was not available to give us his opinion when we contacted him on Thursday.
A South Georgia judge on Wednesday ordered authorities to hand over all CCTV recordings that investigators had examined.
Parents of the teenager said they hoped the video footage would contain clues about the circumstances of his death.
CDC publishes advice on childhood allergies for schools
On Wednesday, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention issued a series of guidelines on how to manage children’s food allergies at school.
This is the first set of guidelines that the U.S. government publishes, since the number of school-aged children with food allergies has risen sharply.
One in 20 children in the United States now has a food allergy.
The CDC found that the prevalence of food allergies in children increased by 18% between 1997 and 2007.
The guide contains information for schools on how to sensitize faculty and staff to food allergies in the child, and how to treat them in the event of an allergic reaction.
It also recommends that schools have an epinephrine stock – the EpiPen brand auto-injector being the most commonly used – to be able to react quickly in the event of life-threatening anaphylaxis.
State legislatures have recently updated regulations to make it easier for schools to have epinephrine in stock.
The report also includes a list of the typical symptoms communicated by children who have an allergic reaction.
Children can say, “I feel like something’s stinging my tongue,” “I feel like I’m having a hair on my tongue,” or “I have tingling on my tongue.”
NSA case highlights complete lack of intelligence debate
How to explain the contradictory attitude of the French government, which on the one hand is offended in public by summoning the ambassador of the United States on October 21, and on the other prohibits the overflight of the territory by the Bolivian presidential aircraft, on the basis of the rumor of the presence on board of Edward Snowden?
In my view, there are two levels of response from the French government.
When François Hollande calls Barack Obama or when Foreign Minister Laurent Fabius summons the U.S. ambassador, they are reacting to a real discovery, which is the extent of U.S. surveillance on all communications in France.
Isn't it surprising to read in the columns of Le Monde a few weeks apart on the one hand the reproduction of American diplomatic correspondence and on the other hand a condemnation of the wiretaps of the Quai d'Orsay by the NSA?
Wouldn't it be like a vague hypocrisy on your part?
The journalistic approach is not a moral positioning, but the search for the interest and relevance of information that allows each citizen to forge an opinion.
When WikiLeaks lifts the veil on U.S. diplomacy’s analysis of political or other issues around the world, we indeed believe that, in view of American power, this is an important insight.
When we describe American interception systems against French diplomacy in the United States, it is in no way to indignant about this practice, it is to describe the world as it is.
Has France benefited from information provided by the NSA regarding terrorist operations aimed at our interests?
Can we deprive ourselves of American collaboration?
The introduction of very powerful technological interception tools by the United States, but also by France, has been officially justified by the fight against terrorism.
Moreover, in this area, France and the United States in particular have put in place procedures for cooperation and the exchange of quasi-daily information and which are described on both sides as essential.
For example, the presence of Mohammed Merah in the tribal areas in Miranshah has been reported to the French thanks to the means of the NSA.
France can be led, for example, to pass on entire blocks of data on the Sahel region to American services, and, in return - as has already been quickly said - the Americans can give information to the French on other regions of the world.
So the fundamental question behind this NSA case is not so much the ability or the right of countries to acquire tools of interception, as the question of the total absence of prior debate, especially in parliaments, on the justification of such systems, the perimeter that must be theirs, and, ultimately, the issue of violations of freedoms.
What does the United States really risk? a deterioration of its image?
No matter hone denouncing them, I don't see how they can be punished.
The risk faced by Americans can be twofold.
The first is when their allies – and this has been the case recently – learn that their leaders, sometimes at the highest peak of their state, have been monitored.
This is the case of Brazil and Germany, two countries where diplomatic relations with the United States have been strained.
Another effect can be more economical: more and more European or South American companies are reluctant, in light of the revelations, to entrust their confidential data to U.S. providers subject to U.S. law, and thus to the control of the NSA.
Last element: the vast movement of revelations initiated by medics around the world, which is helping to initiate a debate on the surveillance practices of the intelligence services hitherto virtually non-existent, could push legislators, including Americans, to reconsider the powers they have given to their intelligence services.
Parents of intersex children can choose "undetermined sex"
Germany has become the first European nation to recognize a third sex for infants born with ambiguous sexual organs.
The male or female sex of newborns will no longer be rigidly attributed to them at birth.
Under the new law, parents will no longer be required to declare the sex of their child, authorizing them to enter an “undetermined” or “unspecified” sex on their birth certificate.
The purpose of the law was to remove pressure on the shoulders of parents who could make hasty decisions about sexual reassignment surgery for their infants, and to combat discrimination against intersex people.
According to the BBC, an intersex person said years later, “I’m neither a man nor a woman.”
I'll remain the patchwork that the doctors have created, bruised and scanned.
It is estimated that one in every 2,000 children born each year is neither a boy nor a girl.
They are intersex, with intersexuality being part of the group of some sixty diseases diagnosed as disorders of sexual development, a generic term for people possessing atypical chromosomes or gonads (ovaries or testicles) or abnormally developed sexual organs.
Wallis Simpson could have been intersex.
Sexual identification is still not well understood, but most specialists in the United States say that when sex can’t be determined, it’s best to use the best information available to attribute it, and then wait and track the child’s psychological and physical development before considering a surgical procedure, if any.
Dr. Jack Drescher, a psychiatrist in New York who specializes in sexual identification issues, said the new German law “seems to be a good idea.”
Intersexual children pose an ethical dilemma.
“Some people suffer from extremely serious diseases that require a surgical procedure, but this is not the case for most children,” he added.
It is possible to make a sexual assignment without an operation, and see how identity develops.
The science of how a child will develop a sexual identity is not very accurate.
No one can answer the question of why this is happening.
It's like the mystery that surrounds homosexual people.
A report submitted to the European Commission in 2011 described intersex persons as different from transgender or transgender people, since their status is not related to sex but rather to their biological constitution, which is not exclusively that of a man or exclusively that of a woman, but is generally both at the same time and not clearly defined as one or the other.
These characteristics can manifest themselves in secondary sexual characteristics, (such as muscle mass, hair, chest and stature), primary sexual characteristics (such as reproductive organs and sexual organs) or the structure of chromosomes and hormones.
The report also provided an overview of the discrimination faced by intersex and transgenders in terms of employment, as well as the extent of acts of harassment and violence and prejudiced crimes.
Non-consistent young boys of gender now have camps specifically intended for them.
Australia and Nepal are already allowing adults to enroll male, female or "third-sex" on their official documents.
In June, a 52-year-old Australian, Norrie May-Welby, became the first person in the world to have been recognized as “neutral” after winning her appeal lawsuit that allows her to keep an “unspecified” sex for life.
German passports will include a third designation next to M or F – X for intersex, according to the Ministry of the Interior.
In neighboring France, gender issues are still the subject of controversies, according to a report broadcast on France 24.
In 2011, dozens of legislators from this fundamentally Catholic country signed a petition for “gender theory” to be removed from school books.
The American site Catholic Online has also opposed German law, writing that "since the world is dragged into a new reign where gender is a choice but where sexual activity is not one, two other pillars of civilization are overthrown."
In Maryland, the mother of an infant also stated on Baby Zone that she would prefer that a sex be attributed to babies at birth.
“Parent’s role is sufficiently stressful without additional restrictions, especially if you don’t know your child’s gender,” she said on the site for young parents.
Children need stability and certainties.
Historically, children born without male or female sexual organs were called hermaphrodites, after the name of the magnificent Greek god who had a double sex.
And just a decade ago, the medical community considered the genus as a slate, which can be erased and on which one can redraw.
But from now on, there are many who challenge the ethical foundation of surgery, knowing that sexual identity is complex and that sometimes doctors can be wrong, not knowing how a child will feel his sexual reassignment when he grows up.
“In the middle of the 20th century, this was called a psychiatric emergency,” Drescher said.
When these children were born, we didn't call the psychiatrist, we called the surgeon.
The prevailing theory on how to treat children with ambiguous sex organs was launched by Dr. Johns-Hopkins University of Johns-Hopkins University, who considered gender to be malleable.
He enshrined the term “sexual identity” and argued that social and environmental signals—the way parents raise a child—interact with a child’s genes and hormones to shape it so that they are identified as male or female.
But in a 1966 case, known as “John/Joan,” his theories gave rise to controversies.
Had advised the parents of a little boy whose penis had been cut during a sloppy circumcision to make him castrate entirely, also removing his testicles, and raising him like a girl.
“Money presented this as a successful transition case, but it wasn’t,” Drescher said.
At the age of about 15, he again became a boy and married a woman.
But at the age of 38, he committed suicide.
Drescher stated that some doctors “always repeat this pattern.”
But in the 1990's, with the arrival of the Internet, those who survived these sex-change operations say "[that] they are not satisfied with the result."
This is the case of Jim Bruce, a 36-year-old writer from Montana, who was born with XY male chromosomes but ambiguous sex organs.
Doctors couldn’t really say if he had a large clitoris or a small penis and were convinced he could never have a “satisfactory life” as a man.
So soon after his birth in 1976, Bruce underwent a surgical removal of the outer organ and testicles and was raised as a girl.
He was taken female hormones at the age of 12.
“I knew I wasn’t a girl,” he told ABCNews.com.
I wasn’t happy, but it was really hard to ask questions.
At the age of 18, he underwent a vaginoplasty.
But depressed and realizing that something was wrong, he asked for his medical records.
What he discovered horrified him.
I’ve been sterilized at birth – and no one had ever told me.
Bruce was born with a sexual developmental disorder (DDS) that prevented his body from producing enough testosterone for his sexual organs to develop.
After learning the truth, he once again became a man, undergoing testosterone treatment and had his breasts removed.
The surgery made it sterile.
Today, he defends the cause of others in an organization called Interface Project, which tries to normalize the perception of intersex people.
But Anne Tamar-Mattis, executive director of the California-based nonprofit organization Advocates for Informed Choice, fears that German law will “invite labelling and stigma.”
"Many activists are concerned that German law encourages parents to make quick decisions and gives their child an "undetermined" sex," she said.
We're afraid it'll encourage interventions.
We think it would be better to attribute a male or female gender, and wait.
But we didn't see what the law was going to give, so we can only make assumptions.
Tamar-Mattis said her organization supported Australian law because “it allows adults to choose to be recognized under the name third sex.”
“Adulters should be able to make their own decisions about a legal gender,” she added.
German law concerns the allocation of sex at birth.
This is not a battle that young children should fight at that age.
When they grow up, they will be able to make decisions about their own bodies.
But Dr. Arlene Baratz, a mammography radiologist in Pittsburgh, whose daughter suffers from a sexual development disorder and who helps thousands of people in a support group, said German law will “responsibilize” parents and children.
Dr. Baratz's daughter, Katie, was born with male chromosomes, but suffers from a DDS called androgen insensitivity syndrome.
Due to the failure of its androgen receptors, Katie developed female characteristics.
She's got a vagina, but no uterus or ovaries.
Now 29 years old, Katie is married and an intern of childhood psychiatry at the University of Pennsylvania.
Although sterile, she hopes to have children through adoption services or surrogates.
“The law gives parents some latitude so that they don’t have to make hasty decisions,” Baratz said.
This gives them time to do certain tests and understand them, and a delay before entering "male" or "female".
Thus, everything is fine – you can raise your child and love him.
You have a beautiful baby and enjoy the pleasure of being a parent.
You don’t have to rush for surgery that’s irreversible.
“This puts children at the heart of the decision and alleviates the feeling of anxiety that motivates parents because they’re afraid they won’t do the right thing,” she said.
Finally, the child will decide with what sex he/she feels most comfortable with – and that’s something great.
This allows the child to make the decision for themselves.
Letta confident in the longevity of her government
The president of the Italian Council Enrico Letta considers that his government will hold until 2015 despite the strong tensions between left and right within the coalition in power in the run-up to the Senate vote to strip Silvio Berlusconi of his mandate.
