Obama's campaign considered dropping Biden for Hillary Clinton
President Barack Obama’s closest advisers have considered, in the utmost secrecy, replacing Vice President Joe Biden with Hillary Clinton on the 2012 list, according to the New York Times.
This revelation is the most significant event in Mark Halperin and John Heilemann’s highly-anticipated book of the 2012 campaign, “Double Down: Game Change 2012.”
The Times obtained a copy of the forthcoming book and recounted Thursday night that the president’s close collaborators had conducted “in-depth group work and polls at the end of 2011” to see if Biden’s ousting could help Obama with a view to his re-election as his hopes diminished.
According to Times Political Correspondent Jonathan Martin, the book offers a detailed analysis of the work of senior campaign officials and the White House, former White House General Secretary Bill Daley, aimed at measuring the effect of replacing the vice president by former Secretary of State Clinton could have on the election.
The possibility of an exchange is a secret that has been fiercely guarded within the Chicago campaign headquarters and inside the Oval Office.
Only half a dozen close advisers to the president — including Daley, former Obama campaign leader Jim Messina, and former White House advisers David Axelrod and David Plouffe — knew the change was under consideration.
“Double Down” indicates that Daley led the charge to replace Biden, despite their “close personal ties,” before finally deciding against the change when the numbers showed that having Clinton on the presidential list “doesn’t significantly improve Obama’s chances of success.”
In an interview with Martin, Daley confirmed that the administration had indeed considered replacing Biden with Clinton.
"I said I've been looking at a whole series of questions, and that was one of them," Daley told the paper.
Remember, at that time, the president wasn’t in good shape, and so we were like, “Lord, what can we do?”
While Daley called the review a “concerted,” Martin told CNN’s Anderson Cooper that the re-election campaign had invested heavily to find out if the change would benefit in voting.
“The campaigns don’t spend a lot of money on polls and focus groups unless they seriously plan to take action,” Martin told AC360.
However, it’s unclear whether Obama was aware that his team was reviewing the replacement.
Martin told CNN that he had asked Daley if his boss at the time was aware of the eventual reshuffle.
Although Daley said he didn’t think the president “was aware” of the possible change, the former secretary general admitted it was “possible” that Obama knew.
Martin added that “Double Down” did not definitively respond to the question of whether political polls had arrived on Obama’s office.
Cooper asked Martin if he seriously thought Obama didn’t know that Biden’s ousting from the presidential list was being considered.
“Maybe,” Martin replied.
Do you crave a neon ice?
A British entrepreneur created the world’s first jellyfish-based fluorescent ice.
Charlie Francis took advantage of the fluorescent properties of this marine animal to develop a luminescent snack.
He came up with the idea after reading a study on jellyfish and convinced Chinese scientists to chemically recreate bright protein.
Ice cream reacts with the tongue by increasing the pH of the protein to make it shine.
Chris explains that since ice cream begins to glow when it reacts to the heat of the mouth, the more you lick, the brighter it becomes.
Charlie, the founder of the ice company “Lick Me I’m Delicious,” said: “It’s something incredible but we’re still still in the early stages in terms of production, and 2 g of that thing costs about £200.”
The protein we use in ice reacts with the tongue at neutral pH.
So when your mouth warms the protein, the pH level rises and the ice begins to shine.
We’ve been testing it over the past few months and it seems expeditious to share it with everyone at Halloween because it’s giving an extraordinary brilliant effect.
It's probably the most expensive ice I've created because jellyfish luminescence is four times more expensive than gold.
So every ball costs me around £140.
But it tastes pretty good!
Charlie’s Bristol-based Experimental Approach company is known for its unusual ice fragrances such as beer, cheese, beef and gold leaf.
However, its next creation promises to be even more ambitious.
He added: “I would really like to develop an invisible ice cream.”
It’s intrinsically impossible due to refraction caused by ice crystals that make up ice cream, but I think I’ll find a way to do it.
Ice uses the fluorescent properties of jellyfish synthesized by Chinese scientists.
Lou Reed died during a tai chi session
Singer Lou Reed died while doing tai chi drills, his wife Laurie Anderson revealed Thursday in a letter published by the regional newspaper "East Hampton Star" intended for residents of the city of Springs, where the couple had a home.
The artist died last Sunday at the age of 71.
