Lou Reed was Master Tai Chi, a Chinese martial art.
The singer’s wife also explains that she had promised her husband, the week before his death, to take him out of the hospital and take him to their home in Springs (Long Island).
Lou and I've spent a lot of time here in recent years.
And even though we're city dwellers, it's our spiritual home.
“Lou was a prince and a fighter, and I know that his songs about the pain and beauty of the world will convey to many people the tremendous joie de vivre he had,” she added.
Lou Reed underwent a liver graft last May.
Halloween 2013 : In numbers
When I was a kid, Halloween was magical.
My sister and I were allowed to eat candies, go to bed late and dress up to go to the neighborhood.
Today, I've become more punger.
For the past two years, I haven't distributed a single candy and I certainly won't do it more this year.
But the stats show that I'm the scabby sheep when we talk about Halloween.
The majority of Americans – 158 million of them actually – will celebrate Halloween this year, spending a total of US$6.9 billion in candy, disguises and decorations, according to the National Retail Federation.
What I look forward to every Halloween party is the trends.
Costumes are expected to represent 1.2 billion dollars out of the 6.9 billion spent, according to the NRF.
This year, inanimate sexy objects are all the rage.
Women no longer need to be sexy professionals; they can also be sexy foods like pizzas, burgers and carrots.
As for men, I expect to see many zombies, thanks to the TV series The Walking Dead, and I bet that Daft Punk's spacemen will be among our Instagram photos this year.
According to Google, the most sought-after disguises are zombies, Batman, pirates and witches.
I don't suppose there's any harm in staying traditional.
We disguised our dogs last year, and to my astonishment they weren't alone.
In fact, Americans are going to spend US$330 million on animal costumes this year, according to the NRF.
That's a lot of curious dogs disguised as hotdogs.
When it comes to candies, you don't glaze.
U.S. is going to spend US$1.9 billion on candy this year, Nielsen said.
That’s about 272 million kg of Hershey bars, lollipops, Milk Duds, Twizzlers and Clark bars.
That’s great news for the 41 million children who go on candy hunting in their neighborhoods, according to the U.S. Department of Commerce.
In fact, we’re going to buy and, don’t tell stories, consume 41 million kg of chocolate during Halloween.
Corn candies are the only thing you don't want to eat; and yet almost 16 million kg are sold for Halloween, according to the National Confectioners Association.
That’s about 9 billion grains of maize.
It's a mystery that I haven't managed to solve yet.
There's nothing more representative of Halloween than haunted houses.
They have the best names, such as “Terror Behind the Walls”, “Howl-O-Scream” and “The House of Shock”.
In fact, there are 1,200 haunted houses officially recognized in the U.S., which generate about US$500 million in recipes, according to the America Haunts, and this includes these great photos of you pissing on you that your friends put on Facebook and that you can’t remove and on which a guy you really like leaves a “nice head” comment.
Finally, let's talk about pumpkins.
Charlie Brown introduced us to La Grosse Citrouille when we were kids, and carving a lantern-shaped pumpkin, it's like decorating a Christmas tree - it's something we've been doing since we're little.
Luckily for us, the “baby pumpkin trend” didn’t start until last year thanks to Pinterest, so most of us carved these pumpkins and didn’t have to sit in it.
This year, Americans will spend about US$106 million on pumpkins, according to the U.S. Census Bureau.
Lantern pumpkins that gently wither on the porch of your house probably came from Illinois, where 542 million pumpkins were cultivated this year.
If you’re looking to get noticed more, call Tim and Susan Mathisdon in Napa, California, and try carving their 922-kg pumpkin.
Royal Bank of Scotland will in-house create a bad bank structure with 38 billion pounds (45 billion euros) of riskiest assets, a measure designed to relax its relations with London and accelerate its reprivatisation.
The bank wants to reduce by 55% to 70% the proportion of questionable assets in its balance sheet in the next two years and hopes to have completely cleaned it within three years.
RBS also said it would include a provision for depreciation of additional bad loans of four to 4.5 billion pounds over the quarter, a writing related to the constitution of the cantonment structure.
RBS has also made it clear that this internal restructuring would release 10 to 11 billion pounds of capital, strengthening its ability to lend.
The bank and London stressed that the "bad bank" would make it possible to draw a link on the past, the government having in particular been accused of interfering in the management of RBS.
“We can now move forward, worry about the future and 90% of the assets that make up a really good bank and build a big bank for customers and for the United Kingdom,” McEwan told reporters.
Land Rover announces sponsorship of a series of rallies
The interior features bucket seats and 6-point harness belts, as well as an Intercom system.
Options include improved brakes, access to Bowler Works mechanics, logistical support, and vehicle warehousing between events.
Drew Bowler, chief executive of Bowler Motorsport, said: “Rally fans who join Bowler have changed.”
Not all of them are experienced, some are looking for excitement and adventure and realistic access to international events.
We are really happy to offer such an approach in partnership with Land Rover and the MSA, and we are convinced that it will be a new way to try different forms of rallies in the UK and internationally, and to prepare for the rigors and realities of raid rallies.
We’ve had a lot of fun developing the Defender Challenge vehicle – the championship is going to be really exciting.”
In addition, the Defender Challenge will offer a training and testing day in February and the opportunity to take part in events in the deserts of North Africa and the Middle East.
Firefighters were called to rescue a lost puppy after getting stuck 15 m above the ground on an unstable cornice in a quarry
Ruby, a cocker spaniel, had escaped after an unraveled road accident
She was spotted three days later by a person who was walking her dog stuck in the quarry
Firefighters rappelled along the cliff to retrieve the dog and save her from certain death
A puppy had the chance to escape the worst after firefighters were called to recover it safely while it was perched 15 m above the ground on the unstable protrusion of a cliff.
Ruby, a 9-month-old cocker spawner, escaped after a traffic accident on Sunday afternoon and survived for three days alone before being rescued in a career on Wednesday.
Its owners, 25-year-old Scott Alderson and 20-year-old girlfriend Becky Hall went to Flappit's career in Denholme, West Yorkshire, to find Ruby and thanked the West Yorkshire Fire and Rescue Service.
They had desperately searched for their lost dog and appealed to social media sites after she disappeared into the quarrier following an unravelled accident.
Around 2:15 p.m. on Wednesday, a sharp-sighted-sighted person who was walking his dog spotted Ruby on a cornice in the quarry, stranded 15 m above the ground.
A technical rescue team at Cleckheaton's firefighter's barracks rushed to the scene and descended as a reminder to rescue Ruby using an animal stretcher to transport it to the top of the cliff.
Technical relief specialist Andy Clayton said: “She was in a precarious situation.”
It was in the middle of the cliff, 15 m from the top and 15 m from the ground.
She didn’t move at all during the rescue – she was frozen.
But she's fine now.
She ate cookies afterwards.
It was a very unusual call.
It's incredible that someone noticed the dog.
Technical relief specialist Peter Lau said: “Ruby was very lucky to get by unscathed.”
She could've been seriously injured or worse.
Ruby was taken to the veterinarian for control; his overall condition was satisfactory, apart from exhaustion and dehydration.
Miss Hall, from Halifax in West Yorkshire, said: “I was terrified observing help.”
I didn't think she was up there.
It was amazing to have her in her arms again.
The veterinarian said that if she'd been too exhausted and fainted, she probably fell.
Firefighters were amazing.
What they did was really brave.
We're so grateful to them, each of them was absolutely great.
Mr Alderson of Keighley added: “We were afraid that she would fall but she didn’t move.”
Firefighters were great.
I couldn't believe she was up there.
Mick Jagger says he never flirted with Katy Perry when she was 18.
During an interview on an Australian radio show this week, the pop star said she was a chorister on Jagger's song "Old Habits Die Hard" in 2004.
Perry said he had dinner with the rock veteran and explained, "he dredged me when I was 18."
She added, “That was a long time ago, and he was very kind.”
In a statement on Thursday, a representative of Jagger, 70, said he "categorically denies making advances to Katy Perry."
"Maybe she confuses him with someone else."
Perry was one of the singers to make an appearance on this year’s Rolling Stones tour.
His new album “Prism” landed at the top of the charts this week.
Beijing implicates Turkmen Islamic movement
“The backstage support for the attack came from the East Turkestan Islamic Movement based in Central and Western Asia,” the head of China’s security agencies told a Hong Kong television channel, according to a video posted online Thursday night on a website.
Meng Jianzhu, who is a member of the Political Bureau of the Chinese Communist Party (CPC), spoke in Tashkent during an official visit to Uzbekistan.
This is the first time a Chinese official designates a particular organization after Monday's attack.
According to Chinese police, three Uyghurs from the same family in the predominantly Muslim region of Xinjiang, bordering several Central Asian countries, precipitated their car loaded with gas cans against the entrance to the Forbidden City in Beijing, in a suicide attack that left two dead and 40 wounded.
The driver of the car, his wife and his mother perished in the car fire.
The Etim, which declares to fight for the independence of East Turkestan, a former name of Chinese Xinjiang, was classified by the UN in 2002 among the organizations affiliated with al-Qaeda.
This movement is often designated by the Chinese authorities as responsible for sporadic unrest in Xinjiang, but its real influence is questioned by several experts.
George Kerevan : The break-up of Europe gives the choice to the Scots
Another day, another worrying story of independence.
This time, we are told that an independent Scotland would be required to join the European Free Movement Area in order to join the EU.
And now we hear stories of passport control in Berwick and a barbed wire border along Hadrian's Wall.
If it is true that Strathclyde's diary indicated the possible economic benefits of greater freedom of movement with the rest of Europe, this was not included in the headlines (and this was expected).
Nor has anyone said that EU Member States spend much of their time circumventing their formal rules when it suits them.
Since Scotland is not part of the Schengen area at the moment, a continuous non-compliance with the rules would be a slim concession that Brussels could offer in return for everything it wants from the Scots.
It's a story that's not one.
And a story so old that it's now fixed: I heard for the first time the bobbard that "independence means passport control" more than 40 years ago.
However, we have forgotten an interesting point in this interpretation of an old dusty tale.
Why should an independent Scotland actually follow Europe's instructions?
Why exchange the yoke of London for that of Brussels, especially now?
Here is the real news concerning Europe: the great post-war plan to unify Europe is definitely at a standstill.
With the euro crisis, Project Europe is officially dead.
Across Europe, parties that oppose the EU or campaign for the abandonment of the euro as a common motto are gaining ground.
Even in Germany, the Eurosceptic Alternative for Germany party, founded only this year and coming out of nowhere, gathered almost five million votes in the September federal elections and took the Liberal Democratic Party (equivalent to our Liberal Democrats) out of the Bundestag.
There has always been national opposition to the plan to create a federal Europe.
However, the current economic crisis marked a turning point.
The austerity imposed by Berlin and the European Central Bank, coupled with the straitjacket on national economies through accession to the single currency, has led many people to believe that Project Europe has gone too far.
The euro crisis has little to do with national governments with excessive budget deficits – this was only true for Greece.
Instead, the European system has placed favourable exchange rates on its members for German exporters – something German politicians want to maintain.
Without the possibility of a devaluation of the national currency, the countries of southern Europe end up with a problem of intrinsic productivity vis-à-vis Germany.
The only recourse is to make dark cuts in wages and public spending – encouraged by Berlin.
Beyond today's fiscal and monetary problems, there is a deeper unease related to productivity in Europe.
As a result of the “green” energy policies imposed by Brussels – which are a reason to subsidise French and German companies in the energy sector at consumer cost – the European industry pays twice as much for electricity and four times more for gas than the United States.
This is a prohibitive disadvantage in terms of costs, as we have already seen in Grangemouth.
Not all wage freezes in the world will prevent the European petrochemical industry from being penalized by cheap American shale gas.
As a result, the revolt grows, particularly in France, one of the great leaders of the EU.
After the war, the French political elite saw the EU as a way to control Germany and put Paris on an equal footing with Washington.
But Berlin no longer need Paris as a passport of its political legitimacy and imposed its own economic policy in Europe, letting the French economy struggling.
Result: the far-right party of Marine Le Pen, the Anti-European National Front, has just won a crucial by-election, returning the socialists to power in third place.
The National Front is now the most popular party in France with 24% of the vote – an opportune warning for the British Labour Party which reminds it that it cannot start from the principle that a split of the right will automatically favor the left.
What is Marine Le Pen doing with her new popularity with the French white working class?
She wants to use next year’s European elections to create a single anti-currency and anti-European bloc in the European Parliament.
If the anti-European parties look good in these elections, which is very possible, such a bloc could control the European Parliament for the first time.
This is what I think: very soon, anti-European and anti-currency sentiment unique in Europe will unify and eventually kill the euro.
The EU will not disappear, but it will return to something that will look more like the "Europe of (sovereign) nations" privileged by General de Gaulle.
Germany and some of its satellite economies could retain the euro, but France and southern Europe will regain their own currency.
I expect the United Kingdom to distance itself from this project, and I hope it will do soft eyes in the United States.
However, Washington's growing interest in the Pacific suggests that Britain will be left behind in the Atlantic.
What about Scotland in all of this?
We can decide to be an area of small England (essentially).
Or we can defend our own economic interests, which includes sendinging to walk Berlin and Brussels.
I believe that Scotland could accommodate a freer European agreement provided that we keep our own motto.
Co-operation with other like-minded countries would be easier in a non-federal Europe.
If not, we should imitate Norway and maintain our economic independence.
Scotland’s SNP government is – and that’s worth noting – the most successful anti-austerity political movement in Europe, as it won a spectacular majority in 2011 based on its opposition to the proposed (and implemented) dark cuts by Labour Chancellor Alistair Darling and the ensuing Conservative/Liberal-Democratic coalition.
It would now be ridiculous for Scotland to vote for independence if it is to accept the austerity imposed by Berlin and Brussels.
Early Puberty : Aging earlier
Studies show that African-American and Hispanic girls tend to reach puberty age earlier than white girls.
Physical changes do not mean that puberty is imminent
There is no evidence that hormones or other chemicals are responsible
Experts believe that the obesity epidemic could be a trigger for early puberty
The trend towards early puberty is not as pronounced in boys
Former CNN correspondent Pat Etheridge is a journalist specializing in children's family and health issues.
Should a mother be worried if her daughter’s breasts and pubic hair begin to grow at age 7 or 8?
At this week’s American Academy of Pediatrics annual conference in Orlando, Florida, child endocrinologist Dr. Paul Kaplowitz explained that these early physical changes are quite common among young Americans and represent a new standard.
“I spend a lot of time reassuring parents – in general, it doesn’t indicate a rapid shift towards full puberty,” Kaplowitz said.
The obvious signs of development, such as boob budding, hair under the arms and in the pubic area and body odor, appear earlier in girls.
But there has been only a slight advance in the age of the first period in the last four decades.
In the United States, the average age is 12-and-a-half, when it was 12-quarters in 1970.
“Once the chest has begun to develop, it takes at least two or three years for the first period to appear,” said Kaplowitz, also the author of “Early Puberty in Girls: The Essential Guide to Coping with This Common Problem.”
Time is the most accurate test to know how puberty will evolve.
There is a debate about what constitutes the actual onset of puberty, but it is considered "early" when the development of the chest is accompanied by sudden growth before the age of 8.
In most cases, development slows down or stops – it’s something a pediatrician can follow closely.
Faster evolution can justify tests performed by an endocrinologist to rule out serious problems such as tumors or cysts.
There are treatments to delay the appearance of the first periods and prevent other consequences: premature aging of the bones that can hinder growth and a small size in adulthood.
Drug and hormonal treatment recommendations are based on the child's age, developmental rate, growth rate and emotional maturity.
Psychosocial aspects are important too.
Kaplowitz is cautious with drugs but acknowledges that “controlling puberty can alleviate certain behavioral problems and the sensation for young girls to be different from others.”
The other big problem is understandable: Parents simply don’t want their very young girls to have their rules.
“They’re afraid of the risk of pregnancy or even how they’re going to handle hygiene issues,” Kaplowitz said.
"It's been a shock," recalls a woman whose daughter had her first period at 10.
Even though there were signs and we talked about the rules, she wasn’t prepared emotionally.
She came back from school frightened and upset to be first among her friends.
There are many theories brought to the public’s knowledge about the causes of early puberty.
Still, there is no consistent theory that the hormones contained in milk or other foods, the chemicals present in the environment or sexual messages conveyed by the media are responsible.
Boys, like girls, reach their puberty earlier.
Kaplowitz claims that the most credible hypothesis is that of the obesity epidemic.
He participated in a study conducted on girls aged 6 to 9 years that links the percentage of fat mass to trigger puberty.
Other findings support this conclusion, but there are many other influential factors.
In this country, African-American and Hispanic girls tend to reach puberty age earlier than white girls.
There are various explanations.
Globally, the mechanisms of early puberty appear to be influenced by everything from economic conditions to climate to genes.
Another problem: Although boys have hair on their faces and in the younger pubic area, the trend towards genuine early puberty is not as pronounced as in girls.
Other physicians participating in the PAA conference emphasized the complexity of the topic.
The appearance of acne and pubic hair is common, even in infants and toddlers.
“We have to be careful about how we identify the real appearance of puberty,” said Dr. Lawrence Silverman, a child endocrinologist working at Goryeb Children’s Hospital in Morristown, New Jersey.
Parents should not hesitate to ask their pediatrician for advice on how to speak with their child.
"This may mean having a conversation earlier than expected," Kaplowitz explained.
If you remain calm, your child will generally react well.
Girls with early puberty need to be reassured that, even when it happens earlier than expected, the process is part of life.
The Folk Tribute of the Coen Brothers
"Inside Llewyn Davis" of the Coen brothers, Grand Prize of the last Cannes Film Festival, is a nostalgic and funny film about the Greenwich village of 1961 and the folk music that was just beginning to resonate there, before the arrival of Bob Dylan.
Joel and Ethan Coen, whose filmography has just been the subject of a retrospective at the Cinémathèque française, had won the Palme d'Or in Cannes in 1991 for Barton Fink.
Failing to hire Dylan or the trio Peter, Paul and Mary, Joel and Ethan Coen set their sights on the rising star of American cinema, Oscar Isaac, 33, but also on the planetary pop star Justin Timberlake.
In this humorous film where the music is a central character and the songs performed live, Oscar Isaac reveals himself an accomplished musician and folk singer, while Timberlake, his friend in the film, leaves pop for folk music with contagious jubilation.
As for Carey Mulligan, she left Daisy Mulligan’s rich attire in “Gatsby the Beautiful” for less shiny clothes, a soft voice when she sings, but the swarm delivered to the machine gun when she speaks.
"Inside Llewyn Davis" tells the tribulations of a folk singer who can't pierce and gets angry with the whole earth.
He receded replacements in the studios.
Without housing, he makes the tour of the sofas of his friends who still want to receive him.
An Air of Documentary
"His relationship with success is tortured, that's what interested us: a mixture of bad luck, a guy who is not there at the right time, who is not careerist but honest, with self-destructive behavior," Ethan Coen, crowned with his brother Joel of several Oscars and awards in Cannes.
If Llewyn Davis did not exist, the two filmmakers fans of folk music built their story on real personalities of the time such as folk musician Dave Von Ronk.
The idea was to show the moment before the advent of Greenwich village, this neighborhood of New York called to become "the epicenter of the rise of folk music that would generate international stars" as the journalist Elijah Wald, friend of Van Ronk, writes.
The film then takes on the appearance of a documentary, the dusty music label invaded by 33T unsold inhabited by an unpayable old secretary, the musical choices of the studios of the time and the cafes where the singers performed.
The magic of the Coen brothers still operates by integrating a funny character who crosses the film constantly: a magnificent red cat with a crisp gaze, fortune companion of Llewyn Davis.
Asked how he composed his character, actor and singer Justin Timberlake recalled having "greaked up in Tennessee, bathed by blues and country."
“My first music lessons were given to me by my grandfather who taught me how to play the guitar,” he added.
For Justin Timberlake, if work is necessary to "be regarded as good", "coincidence can also launch someone's career", and "increasingly today".
Like Llewyn Davis who refuses any compromise on his music, the pop star explains that in a career, "the most important thing is not to get stuck by everything that can prevent us from expressing ourselves."
Gérard de Villiers, the author of "SAS", died.
He'll never know the word retirement.
Phenomenon of the French edition, Gérard de Villiers, died Thursday at the age of 83, had just published his 200th SAS, "Revenge of the Kremlin".
In February, the New York Times dedicated it as “the author of spy novels who knew too much.”
He had just spent ten days in Afghanistan, theater of the 198 and 199th installments of the famous series of espionage novels.
Diminished physically by a very serious heart attack in December 2010, he traveled during this trip with a walker.
And prior to Afghanistan, he had also returned to Libya, Russia, Lebanon and Mali.
With four SAS published per year, Gérard de Villiers, born on December 8, 1929 in Paris, ensured to ignore the exact number of books sold since 1965 and the publication of "SAS in Istanbul", the first of the series, almost half a century ago.
"Undoubtedly between 120 and 150 million all countries combined," he said last March.
His Burmese cat on his knees, in the living room of his huge apartment of a building on Avenue Foch a stone's throw from the Arc de Triomphe, the mischievous eye under his white hair, he cited some of the languages in which the adventures of SAS were translated: Italian, German, Russian, Greek, Japanese or Korean.
"Without counting pirate editions," he added, designating a stack of books on the coffee table between bronzes or ivories brought back from the 130 countries surveyed to situate his novels.
For the price of a pack of cigarettes, the reader is entitled to the traditional cover with the photo of a young woman with an advantageous chest, wearing a gun or an assault rifle.
Inside, his serenissive highness (SAS) Prince Malko Linge, an Austrian disangered aristocrat and CIA contract agent to pay reparations for the family castle, embarks on the pursuit of all the wicked of the earth, communists of the 70's and 80's, then jihadists from the 90's.
With each book, the same recipe: a large dose of geopolitics and exoticism, a few scenes of hard sex, a zest of violence and torture.
"I've never pretended to be a literary author," explained Gérard de Villiers.
I see myself as a storyteller who writes to distract people I don't send messages to.
He worked "like the great pre-war reporters, of the Albert London type, who went there and returned with real and lengthy investigations."
The father of SAS said "to make a kind of geopolitical soap opera".
"I am constantly my files (Afghanistan, Syria, ...) before leaving," added Gérard de Villiers.
On the spot, I meet journalists, including those of AFP, diplomats, people of the services that I have known for some for twenty or thirty years.
As a result, many of its SAS are often premonitory: thus, a month before the attack of a command center of the Syrian regime having killed several senior officials, he had told the story in "The Way of Damascus".
In "Benghazi Fools", he was the first to reveal the existence of a secret CIA command center in this city, the cradle of the Libyan revolt.
In 1980, he staged the assassination of Egyptian President Anwar El-Sadat in "Caira Conspiracy", a year before the attack.
In October 2012, in "Panique à Bamako", he recounted the columns of 4X4 jihadists based on the Malian capital.
“I’m not guessing,” defended Gérard de Villiers, “I simply make assumptions from countries that I know well and, from time to time, some of my hypotheses come true.
After these trips to frequently troubled areas, he settled for a month behind his 1976 daisy typewriter IBM "whose parts have been changed".
300 pages later, he wrote the word "FIN" and corrected each page to the pen.
On the walls of his desk hang AK-47 assault rifles, erotic photos and photos of the author with African warlords.
Regularily pinned for machismo by feminist leagues and for racism by human rights organizations, Gérard de Villiers dismissed these accusations in two sentences.
Some women are sexual objects in my books but others are beautiful, intelligent and courageous women.
I am always well received in Africa where I have many readers.
Gérard de Villiers said: "Malko Linge, like all heroes, has no age."
He won't die and he won't retire.
No more than me.
Planers quarrel about the width of the seats while large orders are at stake
The dispute rages between the major aeronautical manufacturers about the width of the tourist class seats on long-haul flights, paving the way for a bitter confrontation at the Dubai aeronautical show which takes place of months.
The conflict concerns the width of the seats offered on long-haul flights to economy-class passengers – who aren’t always most courted by airlines, but whose allocated space is essential to increase efficiencies in the latest aircraft presented by Airbus SAS and Boeing Co.
This week, Airbus called on the aviation industry to set up a siege-size standard of at least 18 inches (45.72 cm) in the economic classes, but its great U.S. rival Boeing says it should be up to airlines to decide.
The dispute erupted as planners seek to sell even larger versions of their twin-engine long-haul aircraft, eventually hoping for a record orders at the event that will take place from November 17 to 21.
The way in which the seats will be arranged at the rear of the aircraft – in particular, there will be 9 or 10 front seats – is essential for the economic performance of the “mini-jumbo” segment.
Boeing states that its refurbished “777X” will be able to accommodate 406 people in economy class in seats more than 17 inches wide, and will be configured with 10 seats per row.
Airbus indicates that the competing version of its A350 will carry 350 people in 18-inch seals configured in rows of 9.
Aeronautical giants often exchange blows on technical issues by advertising in the professional press.
Today, Airbus appeals directly to the public ahead of the Dubai aeronautical show, where the 777X is expected to take over its competitors with more than 100 orders.
We were able to realize recently what could be the beginning of a new advertising war with the presentation to the financiers of a photo showing three people against each other at the restaurant, entitled “Would you accept that?”.
"Boeing offers long-haul flights in tighter seats than in regional turboprop aircraft," said John Leahy, Airbus Chief Commercial Officer.
As eating habits change, people grow, but seats on airplanes haven’t changed dramatically.
Between the early 1970s, when Boeing's jumbo 747 defined modern long-haul travel, and the turn of the century, the average American's weight from 40 to 49 years increased by 10%, according to data from the U.S. Department of Health.
The 21st-century average American's waist circumference is 39.7 inches, according to U.S. health statistics.
Airbus states that its rival clings to a siege concept that dates from the 1950's, when the circumference of the aircraft freshly dubbed "jet set" was narrower.
Airbus adds that it has commissioned a study that indicates that an extra inch at seat width would improve the quality of sleep by 53%.
Boeing disputes Airbus figures with respect to seat width and says it’s not up to automakers to decide how airlines balance the price of tickets and equipment.
He also says that the study shows that cabin experience does not only depend on the width of the seats.
“It’s really about providing flexibility to airlines and allowing them to do what they think they have to do to carry out their operations,” said Kent Craver, in charge of passenger satisfaction at Boeing.
They don't want us to tell them what will make them profitable.
They know their business better than anyone.
For passengers, it is more a question of having some freedom of movement, but for suppliers, this is increasingly an issue that could affect their results.
Behind the dispute is a race to control aircraft for an estimated amount of at least US$700 billion at market price in the coming decades, enough to tip the balance of U.S. and European exports.
As Reuters first reported in July, the configuration of the sieges is exactly what fuels the battle between the latest aircraft.
Airbus and Boeing both claim a per-siege yield in their latest models of long-haul twin-engines 20% higher than the market leader in this segment, the 365-seat Boeing 777-300ER.
The performance announced by Boeing depends in part on the comparison between the 777X configured with 10 seats per row and an original 777 model configured in rows of 9.
The gain in terms of unit costs is low compared to the aircraft configured with 10 seats per row currently in service.
“Boeing is doing this in order to be able to fit more seats and make its aircraft more competitive compared to our products,” said Kevin Keniston, director of Confort Passenger at European aircraft manufacturer Airbus.
On the other hand, analysts say that cabins configured with 10 seats per row in the existing 777s suggest that many passengers vote in favor of a denser configuration, which can go hand in hand with cheaper rates.
“An 18-inch seating width would be perfect for passengers, but the reality is that, from a commercial point of view, Airbus’ proposal is motivated by the threat of the 777,” said Cabin Interior Expert Mary Kirby, founder and editor-in-chief of Runway Girl Network.
Airbus and Boeing don’t provide seats, but offers a catalogue of suppliers from which airlines must choose.
Airplane sellers who travel around the world even take with them a meter to verify competing configurations.
While bragging about offering comfortable aircraft, all manufacturers also offer aircraft with high-density configurations to low-cost airlines and for regional travel.
Airbus offers an A350 configured with 10 seats per row but says it hasn't sold it yet.
Until recently, Airbus stressed the need for greater customization of cabins by offering wider seats on some of its aircraft.
Without the support of the one-in-thirst-stanging manufacturer of large modern aircraft, experts say it is unlikely that its call to the aeronautical industry to set up a size standard for seats is successful, but it could make us forget the 777X wave of sales.
Oracle shareholders irritated by Ellison's salary
A majority of Oracle's shareholders voted Thursday against the compensation proposal for founder and chief executive Larry Ellison, in light of the financial performance of his group.
The vote is not constraining but tarnishes the image of Larry Ellison, the world's third fortune just surrounded by the victory of his boat at the America's Cup.
The billionaire still owns 25% of the capital of the software group he co-founded 40 years ago.
Last year, Ellison had suffered a negative vote on his remuneration.
Oracle’s boss renounced a US$1.2 million bonus for fiscal 2013 ended May due to the group’s poor performance, which missed its growth targets, but it permeated approximately US$77 million related to stock options.
His fixed salary is only a symbolic dollar.
As a whole in fiscal 2013, Oracle's net profit rose by 3.5% while the stock price jumped by 27.5%, outperforming the S&P-500 index which took 24% at the same time.
The new anti-nicotine vaccine could suppress the pleasure of smoking
Scientists have developed an anti-nicotine vaccine that could eliminate the pleasure of smoking a cigarette.
Only one dose of the vaccine has been able to protect mice for life from nicotine addiction.
Further tests are needed prior to start human trials, but Professor Ronald Crystal of Weill Cornell Medical College in New York said the first signs were encouraging.
“We really hope that this kind of vaccination strategy can help millions of smokers who have tried to quit, exhausting all the methods available on the market today, but discover that their addiction is strong enough to overcome these different approaches,” Cornell said.
The new vaccine contains a harmless virus that has been modified to carry the genetic information needed to design anti-nicotine antibodies.
The virus selectively infects liver cells, which then begins to produce a steady flow of antibodies.
Antibodies track down all the nicotine molecules present in the bloodstream and neutralize them before they reach the brain, which prevents the smoker from having his dose of nicotine.
During the tests, the vaccinated mice who were then given nicotine continued their activity normally.
But mice that hadn’t been vaccinated “were appeased,” the researchers said, a sign that nicotine had reached their brains.
The experiments are described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
Precedent tobacco vaccines have been shown to be ineffective because they contained antibodies.
Injections had to be repeated so frequently that antibody levels remained high that they were too expensive and impractical.
But the cost of the new vaccine should be much lower because it turns liver cells into antibody factories.
Professor Crystal said that if the future vaccine for human use was completely safe, it could be given to children before they were tempted to try a cigarette, which would prevent any nicotine addiction.
But it is more likely to be used by smokers who want to quit smoking.
“They’ll know that if they start smoking again, they won’t have any fun because of the anti-nicotine vaccine, and it can help them lose their bad habit,” he added.
British scientists said the results were interesting but reported that much more in-depth research was needed.
Pakistan's Taliban leader killed by a drone
The leader of the Pakistani Taliban movement, Hakimullah Mehsud, was killed in a U.S. drone strike on Friday in Pakistan, security services said.
Hakimullah Mehsud's death has been announced several times in the past.
However, intelligence, military and activist officials said this time that he had lost his life in the strike in the North Waziristan region.
"We can confirm that Hakimullah Mehsud was killed in a drone fire," a senior security official said.
The day before, Pakistani Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif, on a visit to London, had informed the British government that discussions had been initiated with Tehrik e Taliban Pakistan (TTP), the Pakistani Taliban movement.
Tripodi denies being influenced by Obeid
Former New South Walnes Labour minister Joe Tripodi will be the subject of an investigation by the state's anti-corruption regulator.
Former New South Wales minister Joe Tripodi has denied modifying the business leasing policy at the request of his political mentor Eddie Obeid, who had hidden interests in three properties located in the government-controlled area.
On Friday, the Independent Commission Against Corruption (ICAC) expanded its investigation into whether Mr. Obeid had pressured several state ministers to renew his leases in Circular Quay, where the Obeid own two restaurants and a café, after their expiration in August 2005, without a call for tenders.
It is now investigating allegations that Mr. Tripodi was aware of Mr. Obeid's secret interest in these properties, following the testimony of Mr. Tripodi's former deputy secretary general, Lynne Ashpole on Thursday.
During the years of discussions that began in 2005, the government lobbied for leases to be tendered.
Tenants were against and also wanted longer durations.
In 2009, Circular Quay’s corporate leases, which related to the Obeid approximately $2.5 million per year, were renewed without a tender.
Mr Tripodi, who served as Minister of Ports from February 2006 to November 2009, was in favour of calls for tenders.
But he denied having changed his policy at the request of Mr. Obeid, who, Mr. Tripodi acknowledged, demanded a change in government leasing policy.
The transcript of telephone conversations presented to ICAC showed calls made in August and September 2007 between Mr. Obeid, Mr. Tripodi and Steve Dunn, a senior official who had arrived at the Ministry of Ports after working under Mr. Obeid at the Department of Fisheries.
"Was the topic discussed during these telephone conversations the development of trade lease policy?" asked Assistant Commissioner Anthony Whealy to Mr. Tripodi.
“No,” Mr. Tripodi replied.
I don't remember what we talked about, but it wasn't.
Certainly not between me and Mr. Obeid.
Israeli warplanes target Syria, says official
Israeli warplanes hit a target in the Syrian port city of Latakia on Thursday night, a military official told Fox News.
The official didn’t specify the target, but said there was at least one.
The Associated Press reports that the target was Russian-made SA-125 missiles.
At least twice this year, Israel carried out airstrikes against missile shipments in Syria.
Foreign workers in possession of a 457 visa may have to pass an “authenticity” test
The government is currently exploring the possibility of setting up an “authenticity” test for foreign workers in possession of a 457 visa while it plans to expand crackdowns.
The test, if adopted, would be applied according to a criterion designed to prevent 457 visas from being used for unskilled positions or as a diverted way to bring family and friends to Australia.
A government working paper was unveiled today as Labour MP Maxine McKew denounced the government’s speech on foreign workers, saying it could offend Australia’s neighbors.
“Smashing statements about ‘foreigners returning to the end” and “jobs for Australians first” are an unpleasant return at the time when unions were calling for a protected labour market,” she told the Australian India Institute today.
Historically, this meaned that the work of whites had to be protected – and I wouldn’t be surprised that some countries in the region see it as an echo of this historical artifact.
The working document mentions the 12 measures that had previously been contemplated by former Immigration Minister Chris Bowen.
Immigration Minister Brendan O'Connor, who was in Sri Lanka to meet with senior officials to discuss human trafficking, implemented five of the recommended changes, with the remainder under review.
If the "authenticity" criterion was adopted, a visa applicant could be checked to know "if the job offer is authentic in the event that the candidate is a relationship or a personal partner of the owner or person concerned within the company sponsoring it".
Companies could also be required to report the number of 457 visa holders after some of them, having intended to sponsor a small number of workers, actually employed hundreds.
Meanwhile, a 35-year-old Sri Lankese asylum seeker reportedly died of a heart attack after arriving on a boat carrying asylum seekers to Christmas Island this week.
The 9-year-old man's panicked son traveled to Australia with him and found some comfort since his father's death with an adult cousin who was also on board.
The man was urgently transferred by the Australian authorities to the hospital on Christmas Island, where he died.
A study aimed at increasing the benefits of the HS2 railway project for Scotland has been launched by the British Government.
HS2 Ltd’s work suggests that high-speed services to Scotland and the north of England will kick off Phase 1 in 2026.
Transport Minister Kramer said the project would “bring the United Kingdom together.”
The Scottish Minister of Transport, Keith Brown, said he was "excited" at working with the British government on the project.
Phase 1 will focus on a new high-speed rail line between London and the West Midlands.
When Phase 2 is completed, routes will serve Manchester and Leeds.
In June, the government increased the estimated cost of constructing the high-speed link between London and the north of England, from £32.7 billion to £42.6 billion.
The British government, which is in talks with Transport Scotland, has asked HS2 Ltd to study the strengthening of railway capacity and the improvement of travel times for northern England and Scotland.
This must include the possibility of travel times between Glasgow and Edinburgh of up to three hours.
Baroness Kramer said: “Our goal for HS2 is a truly national network that will bring the United Kingdom and its cities closer together.”
We are pushing the HS2 project because the benefits it has are enormous.
Without it, we are faced with an issue of the capacity of our railway network.
But it’s also about developing connectivity between Britain’s 18 major cities, including Glasgow and Edinburgh, so that links between them are better thanks to HS2.
British Secretary of State for Scotland, Alistair Carmichael, added: “Today’s announcement is good news for Scotland.”
For the Scottish government, Keith Brown called on Mr. Carmichael to "unequivocally" support Scotland's inclusion in the HS2 network.
Brown said: “The high-speed train has the potential to offer considerable economic benefits to Scotland, but it also brings the economic weight of Scotland into the overall high-speed rail network project across Britain.
“We are therefore excited to work in partnership with the UK government to explore options to bring the high-speed train network to Scotland, creating an advantage for all and completing the Glasgow-Edinburg line that the Scottish Government is already planning.
"I look forward to reading the review report with British ministers next year and we will then decide together on the next steps."
The rules on the use of electronic devices on board remain in force in Australia for the time being
Australian air passengers will have to continue to turn off their tablets and smartphones during take-off and landing despite the U.S
The U.S. Federal Aviation Administration has leaved U.S. carriers the door open for a change in their procedures so that passengers can read e-books, watch videos, or play games on their devices during critical flight phases, provided they are in “aeroplane” mode.
Passengers can already do this for most of the flight, but many people find it painful not to be able to access their e-books during take-off and landing.
Australian carriers are reviewing the decision requiring U.S. carriers to do important work to meet the requirements, but indicated that they do not immediately intend to change their procedures.
The Civil Aviation Safety Authority also stated that it was reviewing the announcement but pointed out that restrictions on the use of electronic devices during critical flight phases were still in effect in Australia.
“CASA does not have specific regulations governing the use of electronic devices on an airplane,” she said.
The issue is subject to regulations requiring aircraft operators to ensure the maintenance of safety on an ongoing basis and for passengers to comply with the safety instructions given by crew members.
Virgin, who has already spoken to CASA about its intention to expand the use of its onboard entertainment system using Wi-Fi technology, was open to change, but said it would follow the regulator.
“We’d welcome a CASA revision that would allow the use of electronic devices because we truly believe it would improve the customer experience now that we have (the onboard entertainment system using Wi-Fi technology) on our aircraft,” a spokesperson said.
Qantas said she was following the current rules at the moment.
"According to our current policy, electronic devices cannot be used during take-off and landing, and we do not immediately intend to change this," she said.
FAA's decision applies to U.S. airlines.
However, we are still interested in the regulatory developments that could benefit passengers, and we will look closely at FAA’s decision and why.
For U.S. carriers, the impact of the decision will vary from one airline to another and will depend on the age of their fleet.
Carriers will be required to demonstrate that their aircraft can tolerate radio interference from electronic devices, as well as overhaul manuals, training equipment, carry-to-hand baggage programs and passenger instructions.
“As soon as an airline has verified the tolerance of its fleet, it can allow passengers to use light portable electronic devices, such as tablets, e-book readers and smartphones at all altitudes.”
In rare cases of low visibility, the crew will ask passengers to turn off their aircraft during landing.
The group also recommended that heavier aircraft be stored securely under the seats or in the upper compartments during take-off and landing.
Pia: at least four wounded in violent fight
The small town of Pia experienced an unusual excess of fever on Thursday night.
Three gendarmerie vehicles, two ambulances and a gathering of about thirty people according to the testimonies collected...
On this Halloween day, the evening was hectic on Pia's side where a fight involving several people erupted on the side of the Post Office.
At least four minor injuries are reported.
Alerted, the gendarmes went to the scene to separate the belligerents and ensure that the wounded were taken care of.
Singapore's pawnshops take advantage of middle classes forced to tighten their belts
At a pawnshop from Bendemeeer Mall in Singapore, Janani Amirthalinga lays down a bracelet, a ring and a pair of gold earrings to pay for her daughters' tuition.
“My husband and I have just bought a house, so all my money has gone inside,” says Amirthalinga.
“Although she earns $3,000 from Singapore ($2,400) per month as an administrator and her husband also works, the monthly family income is not enough, she says.
In fact, demand is such in parts of Southeast Asia – where household indebtedness is increasing – that ValueMax, where it made its transaction, this week became the thirst-largest pawnpall company to be listed on the Singapore Stock Exchange.
Paying off jewelry isn’t just a quick way to get cash – $1,300 from Singapore in the case of Ms. Amirthalinga – but it’s almost as cheap as unsecured bank loans.
In general, Singapore’s pawnshops charge an effective annual interest rate of 17%, just above the 15.4% offered by United Overseas Bank, a local funder with a branch in the same shopping centre.
However, pawnbrokers have the advantage of not applying for a solvency check or proof of salary, and can unblock loans faster than banks.
As a result, millions of people across the region are turning to pawnshops as families feel the pressure of rising the cost of living and over-indebtedness from households and consumers.
After five years of strong growth since the global financial crisis, and cheap credit fueled by too lax monetary policy in advanced economies, low- and middle-income families are turning to pawnshops to bridge the difference as their economies slow down.
This week, rating agency Standard & Poor’s cited growing household indebtedness, stemming mainly from rising mortgages, as a risk factor for the solvency of Asian banks.
She said Malaysia, Thailand, and Singapore had the highest household indebtedness/gross domestic product ratio in Asia.
Malaysia tops the list with 80% of GDP, compared to 60 per cent in 2008.
Economists are also concerned about Thailand’s high level of consumer indebtedness, which this week emerged from a technical recession.
On Thursday, data showed a continued weakness in exports and a slowdown in consumer demand.
“The balance sheet is that with rising costs, people in the middle or bottom [of the wage ladder] are looking to supplement their income as they can,” says Song Seng Wun, an economist at CIMB, a Malaysian bank.
Historically high gold prices over the past two years have meant that people have rushed to pawn their personal belongings to touch the cash value of their jewelry.
In Singapore, about 70% of items pawned in the city’s 200 pawnshops are in gold.
People say, “The price of gold is high, let’s pawn Grandmother’s gold chain and we’ll pick it up next month.”
In Thailand, EasyMoney’s largest pawnshop operator has seen a 20% increase in the number of customers using its stores in recent months.
The increase is such in the pawnshop sector that ValueMax, operator of the store in Bendemeer and 15 other stores like this in Singapore, plans to expand not only in neighboring Malaysia – where it already has four stores – but also outside of Asia, says Yeah Lee Ching, its managing director.
The company will finance them using 60% of Singapore’s US$66 million raised this week when it went to the Singapore Stock Exchange.
While some reduced-rate lenders have faced criticism for the exorbitant interest rates they practice, Ms. Yeah indicates that the pledge not only offers lower rates than other lenders, but it doesn’t directly increase debt.
“Customers mortgage property they already own, and monetizing their personal property doesn’t increase household debt,” she says.
The pledge is becoming increasingly socially accepted as a means of obtaining short-term guaranteed funding.
The people who go to pawnbrokers are not just those who have financial problems either.
Well-to-do people from Singapore also go to ValueMax pawnshops to pawn gold bars or Rolex watches, which can account for up to 60% of their purchase price in cash.
We see clients from all walks of life.
"It can be affluent people who need to borrow in the short term for business activities or investments, or small businesses that have cash needs to pass a difficult course," adds Ms. Yeah.
Sometimes they just need money very quickly.
Pastor accused of child pornography in Nova Scotia
A pastor who took care of young people in the Halifax area of Nova Scotia is accused of child pornography.
Aaron Hudgins, 30, was arrested Friday morning following searches at his home and at the office of the National Research Council where he works.
Timberlay's Baptist church, where the pastor officiated, claims to be deeply saddened by this news.
In a statement, church officials said Hudgins resigned from his post.
The pastor has been conditionally released.
In particular, it must not communicate with people under the age of 18 and do not have access to the Internet.
He will appear in provincial court in Halifax in December.
He goes in a Nazi uniform to the supermarket, before being chased
A man dressed in a Nazi uniform barred with an armband with a swastika was asked to leave an English supermarket, with customers complaining to the management of the establishment who called the police, the store said Friday.
"We received several complaints from customers, so we asked him to leave the store," a spokeswoman for Asda said.
The store called the police to the rescue, but "by the time it arrives, he had already left without esclander," she added.
I was waiting in line when I saw a woman who seemed upset.
People were speechless.
“You don’t go out on the street like this unless you want to attract attention,” a 60-year-old client, Rosina Rusin, told Cambridge News.
This incident occurred on Thursday, Halloween Day, where it is customary to disguise yourself as a monster, but it was difficult to know if it was a hoax.
A man of Cambridge claimed responsibility for this act on his Twitter account, where he posted footage of Adolf Hitler.
“I’ve been wearing an SS black armband twice a week for three years at Asda,” Dutton said, explaining suffering from “mental problems.”
$325 million-dole rescue plan for Tasmania’s healthcare system
The federal government maintains that the $325-million-dollar rescue plan for the wobbling health care system comes with strict conditions to ensure that the state government does not waste the funds.
Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek has announced that the Commonwealth is taking “urgent measures” to end a crisis caused by an ageing of the island population, the high rate of chronic diseases and the constraints of the system.
Funding, planned over 4 years, was decided after government consultations with independent Tasmanian MP Andrew Wilkie.
“The government has proposed an emergency rescue plan that we believe will address the unique challenges facing the state,” Plibersek said today.
The $325M plan includes a $31 million campaign for non-emergency surgeries.
An additional 2,600 operations, especially in the field of orthopaedic surgery and cataract surgery, will help catch up.
Funds are also provided for walk-in clinics in Hobart and Launceston, better external care, training of medical executives, mental health services and the deployment of electronic medical records systems in local hospitals.
“These investments respond to the idea that, according to front-line clinicians, it’s the best way to treat Tasmania’s health care system,” Plibersek said.
The minister stated that the Government of Tasmania would be confronted with a strict regime of preparation of reports and accountability.
The government will have to maintain current funding levels in order to receive money from the Commonwealth and prepare monthly reports on areas where it spends additional funds.
A commission composed of three persons will be set up to ensure that the State provides the services in the most effective and efficient way possible.
Wilkie said today that the US$325 million won’t matter “unless they’re followed by a real reform to strengthen Tasmania’s public health system.”
Nevertheless, he commended the government for responding to his request for urgent assistance to the Prime Minister in early May.
"I hope that the federal aid plan will go a long way towards removing Tasmania's public health system from the blacklist," said Mr. Wilkie.
According to the state government, these additional non-urgent procedures will largely reverse the recent cuts.
But health opposition critic Peter Dutton believes today’s announcement is a “fortune solution.”
“The reason we’re here is that the state’s Labour government has removed US$430 million out of its health care system,” he said.
We can't have a state government that snatches almost half a billion dollars and the Commonwealth puts $300 million, and pretends it's a good day.
Mr. Dutton visited Ms. Plibersek to ensure that no dollar in the rescue plan will be spent on additional bureaucracies.
Guillaume Nicloux’s adaptation of Denis Diderot’s novel prides itself on a remarkable artistic direction and a meticulous re-enactment of the period, but it is also more laborious than it should be.
Taking place in France in the 1760's, it tells the dark story of Suzanne, a young aristocrat sent to a convent by her family.
When she rebels, she faces the boundless cruelty of a deliberately sadistic Higher Mother and becomes an object of erotic fascination for another.
The film never pours into lubricity or sensationalism, and that's the problem.
The conscientious solemnity of storytelling could also become penance for the audience.
Syria has destroyed its capacity to produce chemical weapons
Syria has destroyed equipment critical to the production of chemical weapons and ammunition filled with toxic gas, the international chemical weapons watchdog said on Thursday, as violent clashes raged in the north of the country, near one of the sites where toxic agents are supposed to be stockpiled.
A group of Syrian militants also said on Thursday that more than 120 000 people have been killed since the beginning of the civil war in the country nearly three years ago.
The announcement of the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) arrived one day in advance of the deadline
1st november set by the Hague-based organization ordering Damascus to destroy or “make unusable” all chemical weapons production units and machinery for mixing toxic gases and filling ammunition.
Completion of what is essentially the initial destruction phase is an important milestone in the ambitious timetable for the destruction of chemical weapons in Damascus by mid-2014.
Thanks to the destruction of equipment, Syria will no longer be able to produce new chemical weapons.
However, Damascus has yet to start the destruction of existing weapons and reserves.
It is believed that the country has about 1 000 tonnes of chemical products and weapons, including mustard gas and the sarin nerve agent.
The announcement came as fighting raged Thursday in the city of Safira, which experts say is home to a chemical weapons production unit as well as storage sites, the British-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights reported.
The group of activists, which is monitoring the evolution of the human’s toll thanks to a network of militants based in Syria, said on Thursday that 120,296 people had died.
Among them, it is estimated that there were 61,067 civilians, including 6,365 children.
On the government side, it is estimated that 29,954 members of the armed forces of President Bashar al-Assad were killed, of which 18 678 were fighters of the pro-government forces and 187 of Lebanese Hezbollah militants.
Among the dead, it is also estimated that 2 202 were deserters and some 5 375 opposition fighters, many of whom were foreigners.
On 25 July, the United Nations estimated that 100,000 people had died in the conflict since March 2011.
It has not updated this figure since.
Some 2 million people had to flee the country because of the conflict.
Assad's troops are fighting against the rebels, many of whom are linked to groups of the Al-Qaeda network, in Safira for weeks.
The Observatory said on Thursday there were losses on both sides, but gave no details.
The fighting underscored the importance of the dangers faced by chemical weapons inspectors as they have to complete their mission to rid Syria of its toxic arsenal in the midst of an ongoing civil war, within tight deadlines.
An OPCW statement, working closely with the UN, said its team was “now pleased to have controlled – and seen destroyed – all declared critical production and machinery for mixing and filling.”
She added that "no other inspection activities are planned at this time."
Earlier in the week, inspectors said they had completed the first stage of verification work, visiting 21 of the 23 sites declared by Damascus.
They were unable to visit the last two sites due to concerns about their safety, inspectors said.
On Thursday, the OPCW said the two sites were, according to Syria, “abandoned and... that elements of the chemical weapons program they contained had been moved to other declared sites, which have been inspected.”
It was unclear at first glance whether the Safira unit was one of two sites that the OPCW inspectors were unable to visit.
Syria submitted a plan for the total destruction of its chemical weapons that was approved the following month by the OPCW Executive Committee.
"I salute the moral strength and courage you have all shown as part of this organization's most difficult mission ever," Ahmet Uzumcu's CEO said in a commentary published by the OPCW.
Now in its third year, the civil war mainly pits Sunni Muslim rebels against Assad’s government and its security forces, which are backed by members of the Alawite sect, a branch of Shiite Islam.
On the other hand, the director of the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights said there had been a strong explosion on Wednesday at an air defence base in Syria’s coastal province of Latakia.
The cause of the explosion remains unknown, he added.
Anger caused by sentencing against one of Bali's bombers
Survivors and relatives of the 202 people killed in the 2002 Bali attacks reacted angrily to the sentencince against the last conspirators to be brought to justice, saying that Umar Patek should be shot by a firing squad.
Patek, who spent almost 10 years on the run as one of the most sought-after men in Southeast Asia, was yesterday sentenced to 20 years in prison for his role in the manufacture of explosive devices used in the attacks.
He could be released after 15 years on parole.
The 45-year-old man has been convicted of mass killings for attacks on two nightclubs in Kuta’s popular tourist district, which killed 202 people, including 88 Australians, and injured many.
He has also been convicted of many other counts in relation to terrorism, including a wave of attacks on churches throughout Indonesia on Christmas Eve in 2000.
Prosecutors have requested a life sentence, yet they could have asked that the man, described as “Demolition Man” for his reputation as an expert in bomb making, be sentenced to death.
The decision revived painful memories for June Corteen, a mother of Perth who lost her 39-year-old twins, Jane and Jenny, in the destruction by Patek and other conspirators nearly 10 years ago.
On the verge of tears, she said Patek should have been sentenced to death.
“I really think he should’ve followed the same path as the other men.
"He should be dragged in front of the firing squad," Corteen told the AAP.
I have to live every day without having other grandkids or seeing my daughters.
The Sari Club was completely destroyed when a huge bomb, loaded into a van parked outside, exploded just after 11 p.m. on October 12, 2002.
Peter Hughes was at Paddy's Bar when a kamikaze blew a backpack loaded with explosives just 20 seconds earlier.
He sank into a coma the aftermath of the attack and remained unconscious for a month, and he "died" three times while under respiratory assistance.
Hugues said Patek should have heard the same fate as the other three members of Jemaah Islamiyah’s terrorist cell responsible for the carnage – Amrozi, Mukhlas and Imam Samudra – who were executed 4 years ago.
In fact, this man should have been sentenced to death before others.
Keep him alive, why, there's no reason to keep him alive.
Spending 20 years in prison after killing 202 people and injuring hundreds more isn’t much.
Patek is the last one of Bali's bombers to be brought to justice.
He had managed to avoid being captured for almost 10 years but was finally arrested in January 2011 in the Pakistani city of Abbottabad, where U.S. forces will kill former al-Qaeda leader Osama bin Laden less than four months later.
During the trial, an FBI agent said intelligence reports had revealed that Patek was in Pakistan to meet bin Laden in an attempt to re-establish links between terrorist groups in Southeast Asia and Al Qaeda.
“He didn’t surrender,” Corteen added.
Until recently, he mocked the grief he may have caused to others.
The verdict was delivered on the eve of the 10th anniversary of the attacks which will be celebrated later this year and marked by ceremonies in Bali and Australia.
“There will be a lot of crying this year,” Corteen said.
However, Patek can appeal his conviction.
FAA: Air passengers can now use gadgets on airplanes (but not make a call with their mobile phone)
Airline passengers will be able to use their door-to-door electronic devices to read, work, play games, watch movies, and listen to music — but not speak on their mobile phones — under the much-anticipated new guidelines released Thursday by the Federal Aviation Administration.
But passengers shouldn’t expect immediate changes.
The speed with which the change will vary from one airline to another, FAA Administrator Michael Huerta said at a news conference.
Airlines will have to demonstrate to the FAA that their aircraft comply with the new guidelines and that they have updated crew training manuals and aircraft storage rules for compliance.
The FAA said it has already received plans from some airlines to expand the use of electronic devices on their aircraft.
Delta and JetBlue are among the companies that have already submitted plans.
“Depending on the plan, we could approve an expanded use of electronic devices very soon,” the FAA said in a statement.
Currently, passengers have to turn off their smartphones, tablets and other devices as soon as the aircraft doors are closed.
They are not supposed to turn them back on until the plane reaches an altitude of 10 000 feet and the commander gives his green light.
Passengers are expected to turn off their aircraft during the descent of the aircraft prior to landing and do not turn them back on until the aircraft has landed.
Under the new guidelines, airlines whose aeroplanes are properly protected from electronic interference will be able to allow passengers to use their aircraft during take-offs, landings and ground rolling, the FAA said.
Most new airliners and other aircraft that have been modified so passengers can use Wi-Fi at higher altitudes, should meet the criteria.
Laura Glading, president of the Association of Professional Flight Attendants, said she was pleased with these changes.
“Once the new policy is safely implemented – and we’re going to work closely with carriers for that – it’s going to benefit everyone,” Glading said in a statement.
We’re frankly tired of feeling like “room supervisors” when it comes to this issue.
But it will still be forbidden to connect to the internet to surf, exchange emails and SMS or download data below 10 000 feet, the agency said.
Passengers will have to put their smartphones, tablets and other devices in airplane mode.
So still no Words With Friends, the Scrabble-style online game that actor Alec Baldwin played on his smartphone in 2011 when he was dumped from an American Airlines plane after refusing to turn off his device while the device was parked at the door.
And heavier devices like laptops will still have to be stored because they could injure someone if they flew across the cabin.
Cell phones on board will also continue to be banned.
The regulatory power over mobile phones belongs to the Federal Communications Commission and not to the FAA.
The FAA may lift the ban on certain electronic devices during take-off and landing
Last month, Mark Rosenker of the National Transportation Safety Board, national transportation safety expert on CBS News, said mobile phones were still considered to pose a risk.
“Mobile phones are really a problem, not only because they could eventually create interference with navigational instruments, but because we know, according to the FCC, that they could disrupt mobile phone relay antennas if they’re used on board,” Rosenker said.
An industrial advisory committee established by the FAA to examine the issue recommended last month that the government allow for wider use of personal electronic devices.
Pressure on the FAA has increased in recent years to ease restrictions on their use.
Critics such as Senator Claire McCaskill, D-Mo., argue that there is no valid security reason for these prohibitions.
Restrictions are also becoming increasingly difficult to enforce because the use of devices has become ubiquitous.
Some studies indicate that at least one-third of passengers forget to turn off their devices, or deliberately do so.
The FAA began to limit the use of electronic devices in 1996 following reports on the presence of interference with navigation and communication instruments when passengers were carrying FM radios, then's high-tech gadget.
The new airliners depend much more on electrical systems than previous generations of aircraft, but they are also designed to withstand electronic and FAA-approved interference.
Airlines have allowed their passengers to use Wi-Fi at cruising altitudes for several years.
Aircraft modified to accommodate Wi-Fi systems are also more resistant to interference.
The vast majority of airliners are expected to allow wider use of electronic devices under the new guidelines, Huerta said.
Today's electronic devices generally emit radio transmissions of much lower power than previous generations of devices.
E-book readers, for example, emit only minimal transmissions when turning a page.
But transmissions are stronger when devices download or send data.
Amazon.com is among those lobbying for a relaxation of restrictions on the use of electronic devices by passengers.
In 2011, the company’s executives loaded an airliner with Kindle e-book readers and flew it to see if there were any problems, but there were none.
This has inspired FAA Advisory Committee members mixed feelings about whether or not the use of the devices poses a risk.
Douglas Kidd of the National Association of Airline Passengers said he believed interference from the aircraft was real, even if the risk was minimal.
Other members of the committee stated that there are only anecdotal reports from pilots indicating that aircraft can interfere with an airplane’s systems, and that most of them are very old.
However, the Committee recommended that the FAA allow pilots to ask passengers to turn off their aircraft during instrument landings under low visibility conditions.
A group in the travel industry welcomed the changes, calling for common sense arrangements for technology-equipped travellers.
“We are pleased that the FAA recognizes that an enjoyable passenger experience is not incompatible with safety,” said Roger Dow, President and Director of the U.S. Travel Association.
A helitrailed bird from a platform in the North Sea has been released into the wild
A bird, helitrailed after being found depleted at an oil platform in the North Sea, has been released into the wild.
The water rail was brought aboard a helicopter to Aberdeen last month before it was set back by the Scottish SPCA at its Alloa rescue centre.
The director of the center, Colin Seddon, said: “This rattle of water was probably a wintering migrator who came from northern Europe and found himself caught in the midst of strong winds in the North Sea.”
It seems that the depleted bird has managed to find refuge on the oil platform.
He added: “He was unable to fly again, so we were contacted to help.”
The water rattle was healthy when it was released.
Is the European elite ready to do business with Britain?
The Business for Britain campaign was launched in April with the promise of bringing businesses together and defining what the creators of wealth and jobs want to see changed in our relationship with the EU.
To do this, we commissioned Britain’s widestriest and most comprehensive survey and asked them what they thought of Great Britain, businesses and the EU.
YouGov surveyed more than 1 000 business leaders, broadly representative of all sizes of companies, sectors and regions of Great Britain.
The findings of the investigation will be a surprise to many.
We have discovered that the vast majority of companies are now looking to export outside of Europe, focusing on countries that are modernizing and developing while EU states are stagnating.
They want the government to prioritize new trade ties with countries like China, India and Brazil rather than getting bogged down in the lengthy and ardust process of reforming European institutional arcana.
When asked their opinion on specific policy issues – ranging from the regulation of monopolies to product laws – the majority of business leaders believe that controlling these key competences should return to the hands of Westminster.
The Single Market has caused general dissatisfaction, with companies saying that the cost of the Brussels regulation is disproportionate to the advantages of being part of the European trade area – 40% of the large companies, traditionally the most pro-European, even agree.
Finally, and what is even more telling, our survey among entrepreneurs has revealed that a large majority wants Britain to pursue initiatives in favor of a change in the treaty and the development of a relationship with the EU based on trade and not politics.
This conclusion, found in all large groups and companies of all sizes, shows that the corporate world insists on a “significant change” that empowers the UK.
The stakes are high – succeeding in changing the treaty and better representing Britain’s interests would make it possible to see a 16% shift in votes in favour of maintaining the country in the EU in a referendum.
The Prime Minister should not doubt for a moment: this survey shows that British companies support this plan to renegotiate the terms of Britain's accession to the EU.
It also shows that companies hope renegotiation will be a significant change in the current balance of power in favour of a return to the hands of the UK.
It is possible, and increasingly necessary, to better represent the interests of British companies, since the eurozone is embarking on the path of a closer economic and fiscal union.
The priority must be jobs and growth in Britain and, as the findings of our survey show, for businesses this means once again focusing on trade and a fundamental change in Brussels' regulatory approach.
The United States in the colors of Halloween
In the United States, President Obama did not derogate from the Halloween tradition.
Along with his wife last night, he handed out treats to hundreds of children invited to the White House gardens in Washington.
Last year's Halloween festivities on the east coast of the United States were cancelled due to Hurricane Sandy.
This year, partygoers have caught up.
In New York, thousands of stunted people participated in the "Greenwich Village" parade.
“Everyone is over-excited,” says one of the participants, Andrea.
I was finally able to put on the suit I had planned last year.
We're having fun like crazy, Rhonda adds.
The people are happy.
It's a good kid, it's cool.
We really need this.
Halloween is a pagan festival celebrated on the eve of All Saints’ Day, mainly in Anglo-Saxon countries.
Alexei Miller of Gazprom declares that the Bulgarian gas pipeline inaugurates a new era of gas
The start of construction of the South Stream pipeline in Bulgaria marks the launch of one of Europe’s largest energy projects, the Gazprom director said.
"A historic event took place today: Construction began in the Bulgarian part of the South Stream pipeline, Europe's large-scale large-scale project," Alexei Miller, Gazprom's chief executive, said in a statement Thursday.
This project is a key part of energy security across the European continent.
South Stream is expected to diversify Russia's export routes to Europe.
A contractual dispute between Gazprom and its counterpart in Ukraine, a country through which most of the Russian gas destined for Europe transits, increases the level of risk compared to conventional pathways.
Miller added that the direct junction to Bulgaria, a member of the European Union, means that the geopolitical risks associated with transit countries are eliminated “forever”.
Bulgarian consumers will receive South Stream gas at a reduced rate as soon as the entire project is put into operation in 2015.
Gazprom said construction is expected to start in other countries downstream by the end of the year.
The pipeline was designed for an annual capacity of 63 billion cubic meters of natural gas.
Pitcairn wants to create the world’s largest marine reserve
Pitcairn Island wants to create the world's largest marine reserve, the daily newspaper Les Nouvelles de Tahiti announced on Thursday, taking information from Radio Australia.
This island, to the east of the Gambier archipelago (French Polynesia), is the last British territory of the South Pacific, halfway between New Zealand and Chile.
This territory measures 47 km2, if there are three other small islands nearby.
It has been British since its occupancy in 1790 by the Bounty mutinies, an episode in the history of the English navy that gave rise to three Hollywood adaptations.
A few dozen inhabitants, mostly descendants of Bounty mutinies, still inhabit Pitcairn, and their incomes depend to 95% of London's generosity.
The Council of the island voted unanimously in favor of the creation of a marine reserve around the small archipelago, i.e. 836,000 km2 which correspond to its exclusive economic zone.
A deputy mayor of the island went to London to ask Britain to validate this request.
Mike Warren, the mayor of Pitcairn, believes that the creation of such a protected area would be a first step towards more financial autonomy for his island.
The project must be accepted by the English governor of Pitcairn, residing in New Zealand, and the British government.
The creation of such a marine reserve would allow the United Kingdom to sanctuarize these 836,000 km2, provided, however, that it has the means to monitor it, which is not the case.
There is no airport in Pitcairn, which makes it impossible to base one or more aircraft dedicated to the surveillance of the area, and there is no port where to place military surveillance vessels.
As for France, it must already carry out the very expensive aerial and maritime surveillance of the five million km2 of the EEZ of French Polynesia, adjacent to Pitcairn.
The FAA eases restrictions on the use of electronic gadgets on airplanes – but it will still be forbidden to make calls on mobile phones.
Fighter jets are attacking a stockpile of Russian missiles in the port city of Latakia, an official said.
This would apparently be the continuation of Israel’s campaign to prevent the proliferation of weapons in the Middle East.
