The police say she's in possession of a video that appears to show Mayor Rob Ford smoking crack.
Even close allies hide things – and look from every angle to find out what hasn’t been said.
The Vatican wants to know how Catholic parishes around the world handle sensitive issues such as contraception, divorce and same-sex couples.
Life imprisonment for former Chinese vice-governor
A former deputy governor of China’s northeast China’s Jilin province was sentenced to life in prison for corruption on Friday.
Excluded from the Communist Party in July 2012, Tian Xueren was accused of touching more than 19 million yuans of bribes, official Chinese media reported.
Between 1995 and 2001, the Deputy Governor, who was also the president of Jilin Bank, a public institution, helped businesses and managers obtain contracts, loans or promotions in exchange for money or gifts, said Beijing’s first intermediate court on its microblog.
President Xi Jinping, who took office last March, made the fight against corruption a national priority, believing that the phenomenon posed a threat to the very existence of the Communist Party.
If the head of state promised that justice would be as intractable with the powerful "tigers" as with the "flies" - junior officials - only a handful of high-ranking officials have been convicted, including former executives of the oil giant Petrochina.
The most spectacular recent case was that of former CPC leader in Chongqing, Bo Xilai, sentenced to life in September for corruption and abuse of power after targeting the highest positions within the state.
But the government has in no way demonstrated the intention of reforming the anti-corruption system by creating, for example, a body independent of the party.
Two YMCA employees accused of sexual assault following allegations made against Jonathan Lord at a Royal Commission hearing.
Two YMCA employees from New South Wales were charged with sexual assault on children following allegations made against Caringbah educator Jonathan Lord in 2011 at a Royal Child Sexual Abuse Commission hearing.
However, in its opening statement before the Commission, the YMCA stated that it “has never had to deal with a problem of sexual assault on children within its organization.”
Managing Director Phillip Hare has been questioned about two cases: a YMCA employee has been charged with child pornography offences and a physical education monitor at YMCA Caringbah Hall has been convicted of child sexual assault in the centre where he worked in 1991.
Mr. Hare told Gail Furness, counsel to the Board, that he was aware of the first case but that he was not aware of the latter.
He also admitted that the YMCA opening statement was inaccurate, pointing out, “External YMCA controls have been carried out and have indicated that child safety is a top priority for the organization.”
It has been shown before the Commission that the YMCA has been informed that it has received theselvest misappreaces in four institutions during a quality audit by the Department of Education and Communities conducted in August this year.
Mr. Hare, who started working at the YMCA at the age of 21, admitted that staff management was a failure with Lord's recruitment and had failed to verify that employees knew they were required to report any violations of the child safety policy.
Earlier in the year, Lord was convicted of sexually assaulting of twelve boys during the two years he worked at the YMCA.
He was sentenced to a minimum of 6 years in prison.
But Hare rejected the idea that the YMCA would have a cultural problem preventing staff from reporting Lord’s breaches of child safety.
Staffiers testified that they had observed certain failures, such as the fact that Lord stayed alone with the children, kept them privately, made them sit on his lap, told them that he loved them and let them play with his mobile phone.
Danielle Ockwell, who was supervised by Lord and had asked for training in child protection because she was concerned about her behaviour, indicated that she found YMCA Caringbah, Jacqui Barnat, who mentored Lord "very intimidating and difficult to approach most of the time".
The Director General stated that he was rejecting staff testimony that they were reluctant to report these breaches to their supervisor.
Instead, he stated that the friendly relationships they had with Lord clouded their judgment, so they didn’t report his actions.
Mr. Hare stated that he had expressed his views before the YMCA Council in New South Wales saying that for the organization, the lesson learned from the "Jonaton Lord incident" should not concern "reporting" by the staff, and the council agreed with him.
Mr. Hare said that the decision to have staff sign confidentiality agreements shortly after the allegations were raised was made by YMCA's Director-General for Children's Services Liam Whitley.
He stated that this decision was intended to avoid any contamination of the evidence but that it was an "excess of zeal" and that it had been poorly implemented.
The YMCA of New South Wales was not a safe organization for children at the time when Jonathan Lord was employed there, i.e
He indicated that there were "serious problems" in the recruitment, control, initiation, training and supervision of staff.
The hearing was adjourned until December 20.
Tony Blair said he would take the opportunity to become Prime Minister again, but acknowledges that his return is unlikely.
In an interview last night marking the 5th anniversary of his departure at the end of his mandate, the 59-year-old expressed his opinion on various national policies.
Since his departure in June 2007 after a decade at the head of the country, Mr. Blair has largely avoided discussing British politics, limiting his comments to foreign affairs and his role as Special Envoy of the Quartet for the Middle East Peace Process.
When asked if he would like to return to the post of Prime Minister, Mr. Blair would have replied, according to the Evening Standard in London: “Yes, of course, but it can probably happen, so...”
When many equestrian experts gathered in Cardiff to compete against each other and win the Horse of the Year trophy, they knew the competition would be tough.
But no one was expecting to see Fenton Kirkland, a three-year-old.
Not yet at school and only a few months after making his first weapons, the toddler and pony Shetland Toffee trotted during the three towers with great ease and won the first prize, leaving behind the other 30 adult competitors.
The two inseparables – which are the same size – were hailed for the presentation, attitude and style during the annual competition held at Sunnybank Equestrian Centre in Rudry near Cardiff.
Presenting himself against men and women in elegant melons hats, he wore his flat cap a little on the side casually to parade his 2-year-old Toffee pony around the track.
Fenton was greeted by the judges for his uncommon natural dressage qualities for a child of his age.
And Toffee received very good grades for his presentation and personality.
Fenton had Toffee as a gift for his 3rd birthday last March and has been training every day every day.
His 30-year-old mother Donna said: “Fenton and Toffee make an excellent duo.”
They presented themselves against and against all, but the twelled the gold cup and the rosette.
It was only the second time he had competed with Toffee and we were crazy with joy that he won.
On the trail, people who didn’t know him thought he was so phenomenal that they wanted to be photographed with him.
This young boy, from the village of Nantyglo, near Ebbw Vale, in South Wales, follows in the footsteps of his aunt Sharon Howells, who has participated in equestrian competitions for more than 10 years.
Ms Howells said: “There was electricity in the air and everyone encouraged and applauded it.”
He traveled the entire length of the track running and even though he seemed to be very small, he did a wonderful job.
Fenton is crazy about animals – he loves horses, tractors and the rural world and has two chickens he takes care of.
From the way he started, he’ll surely participate soon in the Horse of the Year demonstration – and I’m sure he’ll do a great performance.
A spokesman for the annual equestrian protest said: “Fenton is only 3 years old but he knows how to drive his pony.”
They're a good team.
The judges noticed that Fenton and Toffee presented well and the way they marched on the track.
They are looking for an effective teamwork between the pony and the trainer – Fenton and Toffee have been the best on the track.
I'm sure his beautiful clothes helped Fenton, he looked really great.
A Chinese newspaper that issued an appeal “will be overhauled”
A Chinese newspaper that issued a first-page call for the release of a journalist accused of defamation is going to be overhauled, a press regulator said.
The Guangzhou-based New Express issued a rare public appeal for the release of journalist Chen Yongzhou.
But Mr. Chen later admitted on television that he had accepted bribes to fabricate stories about a semi-public society.
Now the New Express will undergo a “comprehensive reshuffle,” the regulator said.
The "reshuffle" order emanates from the Guangdon administration for the Press and Editing, Radio, Film and Television.
A preliminary investigation has shown that the New Express, belonging to the Yangcheng Evening News group, published several erroneous articles about the listed company Zoomlion between September 2012 and August 2013.
"The writing of New Express was disorganized," the regulator said in a statement.
He said he had decided "to impose an administrative penalty on Chen Yongzhou by removing his press card".
He also “ordered the Yangcheng Evening News group to undertake a complete reshuffle of New Express, and recommended that it conduct an investigation of those responsible at New Express and immediately overhaul the newspaper’s management team.”
Chen wrote several articles for the New Express about the alleged financial irregularities of a construction machinery manufacturer called Zoomlion.
Following his arrest, his diary twice published an appeal on the front page for his release, writing that he supported his journalism.
But Mr. Chen then appeared on a public television channel where he admitted to posting false stories for money.
"In this case, I hurt Zoomlion and the media industry and its ability to gain public trust," he told CCTV.
I did it mainly because I was chasing money and fame.
I realized my mistake.
Following Mr. Chen's apologies, New Express published his apologies on the front page, writing that he had not properly verified his articles.
Several prominent suspects have recently made televised confessions.
Specialists say people are systematically forced to confess, despite a change in the law that was passed earlier in the year prohibiting authorities from forcing anyone to incriminate themselves.
Aircraft carrier Charles-de-Gaulle "unavailability"
In Toulon, the aircraft carrier is flush.
The leak of radioactive vapour detected on one of the two nuclear boiler rooms of Charles de Gaulle, in mid-October and while the boat was at sea, was "safe for sailors", but is not without consequences for the Navy.
The Royale has just confirmed to us the "unavailability" of the ship until "mid-November", "the time to take the corrective measures that are necessary" on the reactor.
For its part, DCNS, a specialist in the naval defense, assures that its teams and those of Areva, dispatched to the Var for the occasion, "currently intervene on the aircraft carrier".
It is for them, among other things, to change a pump on the failing boiler room.
“Everything is being done so that Charles-de-Gaulle can make its planned deployment at the end of the year 2013,” DCNS said.
And the National Navy to certify that "it does not call into question the program of activity of the boat" with nuclear propulsion.
Recall that aircraft carrier Charles-de-Gaulle leaves an intermediate maintenance period of six months.
He sailed from Toulon in mid-October for training.
This, in particular, with a view to qualifying the young fighter pilots.
A "little damage", circumscribed at the containment space of the reactor, had then occurred on the flagship of the French fleet.
According to the National Navy, the crew had not been exposed to possible radioactive contamination.
For more than a year, everywhere, I have been able to see a strong discontent on the part of the people I meet: monstrous reassessment of their properties, deluge of taxes, cost of permits of all kinds, administrative costs to never end anymore.
In short, siphonage without limits of our pockets.
Millions of squandered dollars could be saved in order to allow taxation that is very respectful of those who pay.
Any money forcibly taken from taxpayers reduces their freedom of consumption and contributes to their impoverishment.
In Shawinigan, despite the closure of all major companies, we do not hesitate about the sumptuous expenses and the maintenance of lame ducks: deficiency cultural center, high subsidy to the Cité de l'Énergie, etc.
If these facilities aren't cost-effective, sell them to the private company or demolish them.
It is also heartbreaking to note the expropriation of several properties in order to accommodate a major industry that has never come.
However, I give an honourable mention to the transformation of the old Wabasso into a kind of industrial incubator, but at what cost to tax payers?
It is not up to the public levels to invest in such businesses, but to the private sector based on consumer demand.
Citizens were pleased to learn that Fishing Lakes and Batteries will continue to supply the city with drinking water.
As a result of the departure of all industries (large water users) and the great decline of the population, the use of water has decreased significantly throughout the territory.
Without saying to waste it, the city will never run out of water and the strict regulations regarding its use could be greatly softened.
In short, it’s very easy to achieve great things with the money of others.
I'd be embarrassed to defend such a record.
With a gross debt of more than 200 million dollars, this city is no longer able to buy a pen without going through a borrowing settlement.
We've been run for more than 40 years by ties with beautiful diplomas for the most and I don't believe that a sheep breeder does worse.
Discovery of a drug tunnel between the United States and Mexico with its own railway
One of the most sophisticated tunnels for drug trafficking between the U.S. and Mexico, with its own lighting, ventilation and electric rail systems, has been discovered.
U.S. authorities have described the 4-foot-by-3 tunnel as one of the most sophisticated secret passages they’ve ever seen.
The tunnel, zigzagging over a length equivalent to nearly six long football fields, connects warehouses near Tijuana, Mexico, to San Diego, United States.
The area is full of indefinable warehouses, making it easy to conceal trucks loaded with drugs.
The tunnel was closed before the drugs came out going unnoticed, authorities said.
Authorities seized 8.5 tonnes of marijuana and 148 kg of cocaine after the tunnel was uncovered, according to court records.
Three men, according to the authorities, worked as drivers, were charged with possession of marijuana and cocaine with intention to sell.
They face a prison sentence ranging from 10 years to life imprisonment if they are sentenced.
In Nogales, Arizona, traffickers operate vast underground drainage channels.
Tunnel is the eighth major passage discovered in San Diego since 2006.
Some of the largest tunnels were discovered following the marijuana harvest in central Mexico in October, posing a challenge for drug cartels that must quickly deliver their products to consumers.
In 2010, the authorities found a passage of about 700 yards equipped with railway tracks that departed from the kitchen of a house in Tijuana and went up to two warehouses in San Diego.
British police issue extradition warrant against Assange
Britain’s police today issued an extradition warrant against WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange, who took refuge at Ecuador’s embassy in London and sought political asylum.
Scotland Yard said it issued a "remission notice" against the 40-year-old Australian, asking him to appear at a police station, adding that if he did not, he would be likely to be arrested.
Assange could be extradited to Sweden as a result of sexual crime allegations, after exhausting all possible avenues under British law after the Supreme Court invalidated his appeal against his extradition earlier this month.
Fearinging that Stockholm would allow its transfer to the United States, it took refuge at the Embassy of Ecuador in London on June 19, asking the South American country for political asylum.
Scotland Yard has "issued a notice against a 40-year-old man who asks him to appear at a police station, on the date and time of his choice," a spokesman said.
He continues to violate the conditions of his release on bail.
The Embassy declined to comment on the issuance of the notice by the police.
Assange fears being extradited from Sweden to the U.S. where he could be accused of espionage, after unveiling more than 250,000 U.S. diplomatic cables on the WikiLeaks site battling secret information.
How biometrics will invade our lives
No more passwords to access your smartphone or pay your purchases?
American, French or Japanese researchers predict a future where we will be recognized by the biometric sensors of phones and computers.
Bernard Didier, deputy managing director of Morpho, even believes that "the century will be biometric".
Only biometrics will make it possible to ensure the identity of a person carrying out transactions in a universe as transversal and transnational as the Internet.
Organizations dedicated to the protection of privacy confirm this craze for biometrics, but are concerned about it.
Just in 2011 and in France, the Commission nationale de l'Informatique et des Libertés authorized 774 systems for the recognition of fingerprints, the shape of the hand or the venous network of the hand in companies, administrations, canteens...
“Everyone will soon be able to be identified, anywhere and at any time,” said Justin Brookman, head of consumer privacy at the CDT (Center for Democracy and Technology) in Washington.
The reasons for such optimism, in some, and pessimism, in others?
Biometrics will be based on new techniques, which will make it possible to provide new services to citizens and consumers.
Today, in France, the most used biometric data are fingerprints, the contour of the hand and the venous network of the palm or fingers.
However, each of these techniques has constraints.
"For example, some people who work concrete have very damaged fingers and their fingerprints are illegible," says Philippe Robin, technical director of the identity field at Thales Communications & Security.
And the collection as well as the verification of the venous network and the contour of the hand presuppose a voluntary and precise gesture of people.
It is at the same time a security - we can not capture this data without their knowledge - and a disadvantage: the procedure sometimes has to be repeated and therefore takes time.
As a result, research has been conducted for about twenty years on other approaches, such as recognition of the face or iris (the colored part of the eye).
Thanks to the increased accuracy of the sensors and the computing capacity of the computers necessary for data analysis, these techniques become operational.
The accuracy of facial recognition has multiplied tenfold over the past five years, says Cyrille Bataller, director of Accenture’s R&D laboratories in Europe.
With our help, the United Kingdom and Holland deployed automatic border crossings using facial recognition.
It is now possible to identify a moving face or iris.
"Today, we're researching voice recognition or how to walk using sound sensors, but you need a silent environment," said Sridhar Iyengar, director of security research at the Intel Labs.
A unique and unfalsifiable marker, DNA also raises hopes and concerns.
"In the current state of knowledge, it can be considered as the ultimate biometric data," confirms Sophie Vulliet-Tavernier, director of studies, innovation and foresight at the CNIL.
But DNA analysis is still very long and very expensive.
NEC offers the judicial police a suitcase costing 90,000 euros to analyze DNA samples at the scene of a crime and in one hour.
The uses of biometrics are divided into two groups: identification (recognizing one person among others) and authentication (ensuring that the person is the one he claims to be).
Until now, the identification concerned the issuance of identity documents: it is necessary to confront the biometric elements (imprints, photos, iris) with the information that the State has on all citizens already registered.
This ensures that a person does not try to impersonate someone else.
This principle of comparison may have other applications.
NEC offers VIP recognition at the entrance to a hotel or store.
"We are recovering images from surveillance cameras and comparing them to the photos of celebrities who circulate freely on the Internet," says Dany Nassif, head of business development at NEC France for biometric identification solutions.
Other use: take a picture of a quidam that queues in a store, track its progress through facial recognition and calculate the waiting time.
Authentication initially concerned physical accesses (borders, protected premises, canteens, etc.) or logics (opening of a computer).
More recently, there has been the attendance control.
"A biometric pointer avoids the phenomenon of the friend who points for another," justifies Cyrille Bataller, of Accenture.
But, increasingly, authentication will also concern transactions, in particular those carried out from devices connected to the Internet.
In Japan, it is already possible to withdraw money from some distributors by introducing his card and putting his hand on a biometric reader: this gesture replaces the striking of the secret code.
A similar technique is tested in Villeneuve-d'Ascq and Angouleme by the company Natural Security in partnership with banks and retailers: at the time of paying in store with his credit card, the customer does not type his code, but introduces his finger into a reader that scans the veins.
The experiment is scheduled to last six months.
If it is conclusive, biometric readers could soon arrive at French traders.
Ways to reassure users
There are three ways to make biometrics attractive to the general public.
The first thing is to explain that it can save time.
"If, thanks to biometrics, a customer stays at the checkout thirty seconds less, it's appreciable," explains the director of a large area.
The second is to offer personalized services: in a few years, a voice server will be able to recognize your voice and offer tailor-made options.
And, finally, reassure by highlighting the precautions taken to protect the databases.
"Fingerprints are stored on a first server; people's identities are in a second base: the correlation between the two numbers is encrypted and stored in a highly secured box, which crashes if you try to move it," says Philippe Robin of Thales.
However, for lack of extensive studies, it is impossible to know if these speeches really convince users.
Privacy specialists continue to worry.
"The progress of facial recognition, the proliferation of surveillance cameras and the huge amount of photos available on Facebook, Flickr or Picasa, makes me fear the worst: widespread surveillance," predicts Andrew Patrick of the Office of the Privacy Commissioner in Canada.
A driver travelling at 210 km/h with a hot drink between the legs was inflicted an almond of £1,000
A motorist was fined £1,000 for driving at 210 km/h with a hot drink placed in balance between his legs.
Andrew Howie, 35, of Tiptree in Essex, was spotted driving his Mercedes Benz on Braintree on May 27.
When the police arrested him, they discovered the take-away drink between his legs.
At the Colchester Court of First Instance, Howie admitted the offence of conduct without the requisite prudence and attention.
The seven points withdrawn on his licence led to a 6-month licence withdrawal.
Howie has also been ordered to pay a £90 fee and a £100 victim surcharge.
A tax on foreign real estate owners to burst the London bubble
The Consolidated Consolidated Capital Earnings Tax Measure but is awaiting a final determination by Mr. Osborne who, in Budget 2012, entered a stamp duty of 7% for properties costing more than £2 million and an annual fee for purchasers who choose to hold property under a corporation rather than on an individual basis.
Stamp duty already applies to residential properties in the boroughs of Westminster and Kensington & Chelsea, and represents 708 M£ in fiscal year 2012/13, which exceeds the total amount of Northern Ireland, Wales, Scotland, North-East, North-West and Yorkshire and Humber regions combined.
"Following an increase in stamp duty on high-value properties and the introduction of anti-avoidance law in this area, it is very difficult to argue that high-value properties are understood independently of the effects of the obsolete housing tax system."
“But this change could make foreign investors reluctant to buy properties in London or current homeowners reluctant to sell,” he added.
Prestigious real estate – the 5 to 10 percent of the highest prices in the real estate market – in the thriving sector southwest of London stretching from Fulham to Wimbledon, rose by a recorcular percentage of 11.8% over the past year.
Prices in central London show steady year-on-year growth of 5.6% but have been eclipsed by a growing "domestic market", with the south-west of the city, the north (7.4%) and the east (6.5%) recovering, according to Savills studies.
Scientists have just brought to light how a dog’s tail movements are related to its mood.
Previous studies had found that happy dogs moved their tails further to the right (from the dog’s point of view), while nervous dogs stir more to the left.
But now scientists say dogs can detect and respond to these subtle differences.
Professor Georgio Vallortigara, a neuroscientist at the University of Trent, said: “Everyone knows that, in humans, the left side and the right side of the brain are differently involved in stimuli that arouse positive or negative emotions.”
Here we tried to see what was happening to other species.
He added that just as in humans, in dogs the right side of the brain was responsible for movement towards the left and vice versa, and that the two hemispheres played a different role in emotions.
To learn more about how dogs react to the messy tail movements of other dogs, the researchers tracked animals watching movies where other dogs appeared.
They measured the heart rate of the animals and analysed their behavior.
Surely it won't be long before we figure out why their tail sometimes goes on one side and sometimes on the other.
Professor Vallortigara explained: “We presented to dogs films featuring other dogs – either a naturalistic version or a silhouette to avoid any other confusing factors – and we were able to tamper with the movement of the tail and present it as going further to the left or right.”
When the animals saw a dog without expression wagging the tail to the right (from the point of view of the dog wagging the tail), they remained perfectly relaxed.
But when they spotted a deflect tail mainly to the left (again from the point of view of the dog wagging the tail), their heart rate accelerated and they looked anxious.
Professor Vallortigara said that he did not think the dogs were deliberately communicating with each other through these movements.
Rather, he thinks that dogs know from experience the movements they should or shouldn't worry about.
He added: “If you repeatedly encounter other dogs and if you notice that, most of the time, the movement of their tail towards one side is associated with a friendlier attitude and the right side leads to a less friendly attitude, you will react according to this experience.”
The researchers indicate that the findings could allow homeowners, veterinarians and trainers to better understand the emotions of their animals.
Canine behavioral expert John Bradshaw, a visiting professor at the School of Veterinary Sciences at the University of Bristol, clarified that this was not the first study to examine the importance of the left and right in the canine species.
Last year, a team at Lincoln University discovered that the dogs turned their heads to the left when they were looking at an aggressive dog and to the right when it was a cheerful dog.
And in another scientific study conducted by the University of Victoria in Canada, he said: “Dogs went more easily towards a robot dog when its tail was moving to the left rather than the right, instead of becoming anxious – the opposite of what appears in the Italian study.”
He indicated that the differences may come from the fact that the dogs that participated in the different studies did not fully consider the animals of the movies or the robot dogs as dogs.
A study on how dogs respond to real dogs could be helpful, he explained.
“Although many elements, found in different mammals, indicate that both sides of the brain are used for different purposes, many details still need to be clarified – and dogs aren’t an exception.”
However, given the ease with which their behaviors can be recorded, it surely won't be long before we figure out why their tails sometimes move on one side and sometimes on the other.
Arctic Monkeys Postpones Concert in Glasgow Due to Alex Turner's Illness
Rock band Arctic Monkeys postponed a concert in Glasgow after its singer was diagnosed with laryngitis.
Sheffield’s band was scheduled to perform Friday at The Hydro’s Downtown Concert Hall.
However, his laryngitis forced singer Alex Turner to reschedule the show.
The announcement was made after the band was forced to similarly postpone a concert at Birmingham’s LG Arena on Thursday.
In a statement on their official website, the Arctic Monkeys said: “Following the decision to postpone the concert at LG Arena in Birmingham tonight and after seeking medical advice, the Arctic Monkeys must also postpone the show to Glasgow Hydro on Friday, November 1st.”
“Alex Turner suffers from laryngitis and regrets not being able to perform in concert.”
The show at the LG Arena in Birmingham will take place on 20 November and at the Glasgow Hydro will take place on 21 November.
All tickets remain valid for these concerts.
We would like to apologise to all ticket holders for any inconvenience caused.
Thank you for contacting customer service at the ticket office where you bought your tickets for any further assistance.
Pope Francis will appoint his first cardinals in February
Pope Francis will appoint new cardinals of the Catholic Church for the first time on February 22, the Vatican announced Thursday.
The cardinals are the highest-ranking churchmen in the Catholic Church behind the pope, and those who elise him, so Francis will appoint his first group of men who will ultimately choose his successor.
There are currently 201 cardinals.
However, as soon as a cardinal reaches the age of 80, he can no longer participate in the election of a pope – so this amounts to a group of 120 “cardinal voters”.
In a statement announcing the news, Father Federico Lombardi, a Vatican spokesman, said that a meeting bringing together all existing cardinals would be held before the ceremony during which bishops will be elevated to the dignity of cardinal, known as the consistory.
“Pope Francis has decided to communicate his decision to convene the February consistory in advance in order to facilitate the planning of other meetings requiring the participation of cardinals from different parts of the world,” Lombardi said.
Jack Valero of the Catholic Voices said that by February, the number of cardinal voters will likely have decreased.
He stated that a pope generally appointed as many cardinals as it was necessary for the number of cardinal voters to reach 120 and as many cardinals over the age of 80 as he wanted.
The consistory of next year will be important because it will be the first since the election of Pope Francis in March of this year, Valero said.
For the moment, there is a kind of tendency to favour Europe and in particular Italy.
“It will be interesting to see if the new pope will appoint cardinals from the rest of the world to restore balance,” he added.
40% of Roman Catholics live in South America, but a small number of cardinals represent them.
The cardinals will also be the first to be chosen since Francis formed the Council of Cardinals, a group of eight cardinals from around the world tasked with examining ways to reform the church.
Previouslow, the pope decided by himself.
“From now on, Francis has chosen these eight cardinals to help him,” Valero explained.
He added that it was "completely possible" that Francis sought advice from the cardinals.
But we’ve never experienced such a situation – it’s totally new.
Valero indicated that the popes generally raised bishops representing vast communities to the rank of cardinal, but that Francis was “full of surprises – so we don’t know who he will name.”
GM recalls some of its new pickups in the U.S. to fix seatbacks
General Motors Co recalls nearly 19,000 of its all-new 2014 Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra pickups to fix a problem with manually reclining records, according to a statement Friday by the U.S. agency responsible for vehicle safety regulations.
On some pickups, the front seats could have a defect in the inclination mechanism.
As a result, records do not meet federal vehicle safety standards for head restraints.
“If the vehicle is hit from the rear, it may be that the head restraint does not properly protect passengers, increasing the risk of injury,” according to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA).
The recalled models were built between August 1 and September 10.
The roll-out of GM’s pickups began in June and represents the largest vehicle launch for the first U.S. automaker since its bankruptcy and restructuring in 2009.
GM informed pickup owners of the defect in the first half of October.
NHTSA was unable to review the newsletter to homeowners due to a 16-day shutdown of government operations, slowing vehicle sales growth in October.
Sales of pickups Silverado and Sierra, which have been redesigned for the 2014 model, increased by about 20% in the first 10 months of the year, GM said Friday.
In October, GM sold 42,660 Silverado pickups and 16,503 Sierra pickups.
GM’s stock price climbed 1.4% to $37.47 at the New York Stock Exchange on Friday afternoon.
A worker dies buried under concrete blocks
A construction worker died buried under dozens of concrete blocks Thursday morning in Montreal.
The tragedy occurred around 11 a.m., on rue Marquette, very close to the intersection of rue Beaubien, in the borough of Rosemont-La Petite-Patrie.
In all likelihood, work done on the foundations had been carried out there for some time.
An adjoining foundation wall had just been erected over the last few days.
The accident occurred at the time when the victim had just arrived at the construction site in order to recover his tools.
“I was driving and saw the wall collapse with a big cloud of smoke,” said Sylvain Jean, who lives near the construction site.
I landed and went to remove the big bricks that covered him.
You could only see part of your back, it's so miserable.
According to the authorities, the victim is an elderly man in his 50s who worked for a formwork company.
Resuscitation maneuvers were tempted by the emergency services, but without success.
The man didn't survive his serious injuries.
An inspector of the Occupational Health and Safety Commission was dispatched to the scene to investigate the circumstances surrounding this tragedy.
Obama's Voter's Cry of Desperation
Twice I voted for President Obama, believing in the possibility of change
He says that Obama has made commendable efforts foiled by the obstructionism of the Republican Party
Obstructionism does not excuse the difficulties of the Obamacare site, nor the drone attacks
The memoir of Obama’s 2008 campaign is a sad reminder of what might have happened
Nathaniel P. Morris is a 2nd year medical student at the Harvard Faculty of Medicine.
I’m reading a book that’s beautifully sad these days.
It's a book that, I thought, would encourage me during the gloom of the 2nd year of medicine and give me back hope.
It’s called “The Audacity to Win” and it’s the memoirs of Barack Obama’s 2008 presidential campaign.
When I finished the medical reports of my patients in the evening and going to bed, this book brings me back to the days when politicians inspired millions of people and when speeches could take your breath away.
The election ended with an overwhelming victory and the presenters of the news newspapers took the time to reflect on the historical nature of the moment.
My comrades cried with joy and my parents kept all the newspapers they found.
A young team of visionaries advanced towards the White House and the nation was ready for change.
During Obama’s transition period before taking office in 2008, he had 82% favorable opinions.
And then I close the book.
Going back to the present is a difficult awakening, it's like getting out of a dream.
It’s hard to remember those days full of optimism, they’re like distant memories, a sad reminder of the opportunities of the past.
The change really happened in the years following the first time I voted.
It had nothing to do with what I had imagined.
I credit Obama with great accomplishments, from passing the Affordable Care Act to the departure of Iraq from our military, to the end of the "seen nor known" and the assassination of Osama bin Laden.
Moreover, I believe that partisan obstructionism has destroyed too many efforts to advance our nation in the areas of immigration reforms, public health care and the closure of Guantanamo, among other things.
But having repeatedly defended the Obama administration against my peers and colleagues, I can no longer provide explanations.
I'm on the verge of political despair.
Republican obstruction can't explain the wiretapping of foreign leaders, or the attacks of innocent foreign children by drones.
It cannot explain that the National Security Agency collects data on the privacy of Americans, or the indictment of whistleblowers who reveal the government’s misdeeds.
It cannot justify the assassination of Anwar al-Awlaki, a U.S. citizen, without trial, or the escape of public funding and the exceedance of authorized expenses during presidential campaigns.
It cannot justify the findings of a report saying that the White House’s efforts to silence the media are “the most aggressive... since the Nixon administration.”
And, more recently, it cannot apologise for the impossibility of designing a simple website for more than three years after the Affordable Care Act has been ratified.
I don't know if that's what I expected.
If, at the age of 18, I was supposed to understand that governance could contradict the political campaigns that precede it.
Of course, the electoral function is not foreseeable, because the opposition political party and the events that occur by chance, such as the Newton massacre, influence our public conversation.
However, all of the examples I have listed above appear to be largely the choice of administration.
That's what bothers me the most.
I voted for Obama again in 2012, but not because I was excited by his candidacy.
Mitt Romney presented a confusing and unrefined alternative that did not seem to determine his policies or positions.
I felt that Obama, elected for a second term by being freed from the pressure of future elections, would keep the promises we had heard about for so long.
However, while the index of favorable opinions has plunged below 45% this week, it is even more difficult to return to the year 2008 through this book.
It makes me desire the many promises that have flown away.
This week, I read the part of the book that describes how Obama suffered huge losses in Clinton’s favor in the Pennsylvania Primaries.
At a post-campaign meeting, he told his team that they had to get back on track and remain loyal to their cause.
“I want us to refocus on our talent,” he said.
We need to remember who we are.
We've been 5 years later, Mr. President, and I don't agree with you anymore.
The points of view expressed in this commentary are only those of Nathaniel Morris.
Friday, around 3 p.m., a violent collision between a motorcycle and a car occurred rue de Retinne in Fléron.
The biker, Jonathan, 26, of Fléron, didn't wear a helmet.
Despite the rapid rescue intervention, he died in this head-on collision.
Clive Palmer claims that MP Tony Abbott has a conflict of interest over the parental leave system.
Billionaire MP Clive Palmer argues that Prime Minister Tony Abbott has a conflict of interest over the parental leave system because his daughters could be pregnant and take advantage of this system.
The mining tycoon, who contests the payment of a $6 million carbon tax, has argued this argument to try to rule out issues regarding a possible conflict.
The Palmer United Party could control up to four votes in the Senate, which could be crucial when deciding whether carbon and mining taxes should be abolished.
But Mr Palmer claimed that only ministers could have a conflict of interest and indicated that Abbott's daughters were prepared to personally benefit from these policies.
"He'll have a significant conflict of interest when it comes to paying parental leave because if one of her daughters is pregnant, he'll have a vested interest in whether or not she gets leave," Palmer said.
Two months after his election, the electoral commission officially stated that Mr. Palmer won the seat of Fairfax’s Sunshine Coast by 53 votes, after recount.
Mr. Palmer put forward the idea of reshuffling the draft system to speed up the process.
Tony Abbott, Frances and Bridget's daughters.
Should this election be decided two months after the vote is over?
“We need a better system,” he said.
Why couldn't we have a system in which you can enter, enter your contact information in a computer, vote immediately and have the result at 6h30 the next morning?
Palmer also criticized the use of paper pencils to mark the ballots.
Is it to be able to erase the result if someone disagrees?
Nowadays, having a pencil of paper seems extraordinary.
The Electoral Commission has considered possible options for e-voting and has recently published a joint concept paper with New Zealand.
Palmer, 59, said his political project included an international airport for the Sunshine Coast and that he would take his new duties "very seriously."
The exercise of public functions is a matter of public service.
“We’ren’t looking for a reward, except the reward of history because we have to serve the community at a critical time,” he added.
A team of biologists led by the Wildlife Conservation Society in New York has identified a new humpback dolphin off Australia’s northern coast.
Thibaut Bouveroux, head of scientific mission at the Observatory for the Conservation and Study of Marine Animals and Environments (OCEAMM) returns for L'Express on this discovery and explains the long process of identification of new species.
Is the discovery of this new humpback dolphin astonishing you?
This is good news, but I’m not particularly surprised.
If in the past, species have been described and identified on a morphological, anatomical and geographical basis, today the development of new tools such as genetics allow us to improve our knowledge of species classification sciences.
Moreover, it is not impossible that these advances in genetics and molecular biology will call into question the classification or belonging of a species to a genus, a family, or even an order.
At the beginning of the 19th century, morphologists classified animals on the basis of morphological differences between species.
Today, some of these rankings are being questioned due to advances in genetics.
In the same way, two very morphologically close species have been distinguished through genetics.
This is the case of the new species which has just been identified.
Ten years ago, the scientific community recognized two species belonging to this sub-family: Pacific humpback dolphins and Atlantic humpback whales.
Recent analyses have made it possible to distinguish four species.
Why is this an important discovery as stated by the Wildlife Conservation Society?
From a conservation point of view, it is essential to know these genetic differences, in order to protect the species and therefore its genetic variability.
If this population had long been known by the researchers, they did not know that it was part of a new species, present only there.
Therefore, if a problem of mass mortality caused by a viral epidemic or accidental capture arises in the region, we could deal with the extinction of a species, rather than a local disappearance of a population of a more widespread species on Earth.
This loss of genetic heritage would be much more problematic.
A species is considered threatened or endangered based on the number of existing individuals on Earth, i.e
With respect to Sousa chinensis, from which the population of the new discovered species originated, its stock has automatically become weaker, making it more vulnerable.
Why is it rare to discover new species of marine mammals?
Marine mammals are located at the top of the food chain.
They live in homogeneous, open habitats, in which there are many genetic exchanges between populations and individuals.
This mixing of genes limits the creation of new species.
Conversely, more closed ecosystems promote genetic isolation, which can eventually lead to the creation of new species.
It should be noted that the marine environment is the less well-known environment.
We'd rather send robots to Mars than know what's living in the Mariana Trench, just 11 kilometers from the surface of the sea.
More money is spent on the search for life on other planets than on marine research, and it is high time for this to change.
RBS Suspends Two Traders in the Foreign Exchange Market
Royal Bank of Scotland has suspended two traders from its foreign exchange market division according to two sources familiar with the matter, which again shows that the regulators’ global investigation into suspicions of currency market manipulation is beginning to bear fruit.
Some of the world’s largest banks, including UBS, Barclays, Deutsche Bank and RBS, have confirmed that they are cooperating with regulators as part of the world’s largest financial market surveys, where US$5.3 trillion changes hands every day.
The two traders are the first RBS employees to be suspended as part of expanding the investigation that echoes the Libor interbank rate manipulation scandal.
The bank, which declined to comment on the suspensions, confirmed this month that it had received requests for information from regulators.
“The ongoing investigation into this case continues and we are fully cooperating with the FCA and the other regulators,” said a representative of the bank two weeks ago.
Last month, sources close to the matter said RBS had forwarded records of emails and instant messages, sent to and by a former trader to the British regulator, the Financial Conduct Authority.
This trader, Richard Usher, left RBS in 2010 and is said to have been suspended from his position as European Head of Spot Trading for currencies at JPMorgan.
Rohan Ramchandani, Europe’s head of spot exchange trading at Citi, was on leave this week, while Matt Gardiner, senior currency trader at Barclays and UBS, was suspended by Standard Chartered this week.
None of these traders have been the subject of any charges.
Usher’s file included instant messages sent to and by bankers working at Barclays and Citigroup, sources close to the matter added.
UBS explained this week that it filed a lawsuit against some of its employees after Swiss regulator Finma said it was investigating suspicions of foreign exchange market manipulation in a number of Swiss banks.
Worldwide, at least six authorities – the European Commission, Finma, the Swiss Competition Authority Weko, the FCA, the U.S. Department of Justice and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority – are examining allegations made about bankers having agreed to manipulate rates in the foreign exchange market.
HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse have also launched internal investigations or received requests for information from regulators, sources familiar with the matter said.
Banks are peeling out instant messages and emails sent for years to find cases of wrongdoing.
The news regarding these surveys has shaken traders in a sector that has been one of the largest growth engines of investment bank trading units in recent years, but has been challenged this year due to low currency volatility that limits opportunities for speculators.
Some bankers have tried to downplay the business by saying it’s almost impossible to manipulate the vast highly liquid foreign exchange market, but senior traders explain that this isn’t necessarily true.
A senior trader said that despite the high volume of day-to-day foreign exchange transactions, the fragmentation of liquidity between trade management platforms, and the increasingly frequent use by banks of their own in-house platforms “explains that it is possible to start having an impact on the market at little cost.”
The news came on the same day Credit Suisse announced that it had dismissed a trader working in its traded funds office the London Stock Exchange this week after it caused a $6 million loss at the end of last year.
The bank promptly briefed the competent authorities and cooperated with regulators.
"We are convinced that the trader acted alone and that the case was brought under control," said a representative of Credit Suisse.
The investigation into Mayor Rob Ford has been botched, argues a lawyer
After the police confirmed that they had got their hands on a copy of the video that would show Rob Ford smoking crack, Clayton Ruby claimed to have never seen an investigation "so botched".
Ruby said he told The Canadian Press that the police had "ignored or downplayed" the evidence against the mayor.
On Thursday, police arrested Rob Ford's casual friend and driver Alexander Lisi, 35, and accused him of extortion in relation to the video.
Alexander Lisi, previously accused of drug trafficking, was in frequent contact with the mayor.
Police said they had also observed the man issuing packages to Rob Ford, according to new court documents.
Ruby described as "inexplicable" the fact that the police have never searched Rob Ford's vehicle or his home, or wiretapped his phone, arguing that Police Chief Bill Blair knowingly chose not to act against the mayor.
Under the law, the police, having witnessed suspicious transactions by hand, could very well have intercepted Rob Ford’s vehicle, arrested the mayor, and carried out a search – even without a warrant.
She could also have requested an instant search warrant, if deemed necessary.
The policer did not comment, but his spokesman Mark Pugash described Ruby’s remarks as “obvious and hopeless attempt” to argue with the media in a case that does not concern him.
In a press conference on Thursday, Blair said there was nothing in this video that could constitute "reasonable motives" that could lead to the laying of a criminal charge against the mayor.
European stock markets down at mid-session, except London
With the exception of London, Europe's major stock exchanges are trading down on Friday at mid-session, weighed down by some news from disappointing companies, while Wall-Street is expected upwards.
Moreover, investors remain cautious because they believe that the U.S. Federal Reserve could unravel its policy of quantitative easing earlier than expected.
Conversely, after the announcement of inflation at 0.7% for the euro area as a whole, the idea spreads among market participants that the European Central Bank (ECB) could relax its monetary policy.
Wall Street’s futures point to an increase in U.S. equities, after two consecutive downturn sessions.
Renault (-4.63%) recorded the strongest decline of the CAC 40, weighed downed by its partner Nissan Motor's earnings warning on Friday.
Royal Bank of Scotland (-6.26%) marks the worst performance of the EuroFirst300, after having also published declining results this morning and announced the internal creation of a bad backing structure bringing together 38 billion pounds of riskiest assets.
For its part, Vodafone (+2.45%) remains at the forefront of the rises of the EuroFirst300 in response to a press report that AT&T is studying a possible OPA.
In the foreign exchange market, speculation on the evolution of the ECB's monetary policy is well underway, as evidenced by John Hardy, strategist at Saxo Bank.
The ECB’s unique mandate has always focused on inflation, so Mario Draghi and his team have more reason to act at next week’s meeting.
We are anticipating a strong downturn in the euro.
In this context, the euro continues its decline against the US currency and reached a two-weether loss at US$1.3517 in session.
These same conjectures, on the other hand, support the eurozone bond market.
Supreme Court Validates Obama’s Health System Reform Act
Great victory for the Obama administration: The U.S. Supreme Court said today that Obama’s health system reform law was constitutional.
By 5 votes to 4, the judges validated the individual mandate of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act – which requires citizens to take out health insurance by 2014, only to have to pay a fine – and said it was constitutional under the government’s tax power.
Supreme Court President John Roberts has rallied to the four most liberal members of the Court, while Justices Scalia, Thomas, Alito and Kennedy expressed their disagreement.
The Court also validated the other sections of the 2-700-page law and also considered that the provision of the Health System Reform Act, which stipulates that it is necessary to lower the eligibility threshold to Medicaid under penalty of losing all federal funding of the program, was coercive in accordance with the constitution.
Proceedings to block the law were initiated by 26 states and the National Federation of Independent Business.
All the major Republican candidates in these presidential elections during the 2012 primaries, including nomineed candidate Mitt Romney, fiercely opposed the law.
Vibrant tribute to veterans in Menton
In the heart of the Trabuquet cemetery, civil, religious and military authorities, as well as many patriotic associations, paid tribute this Friday afternoon to the soldiers and deceased of all wars.
Wreaths have been deposited in two of the three military squares of the cemetery by the deputy mayor Jean-Claude Guibal and various personalities.
Hanging between Palestinians and Israelis on the Gaza border
Four Hamas fighters were killed and five Israeli soldiers wounded in the evening of a violent clash on the Gaza-Israel border, the most serious incident in Palestinian territory in a year.
The only power plant in the Gaza Strip stopped operating Friday, November 1st, after a depletion of its fuel stockpiles, the Palestinian Enclave Energy Authority said.
A local commander of the Ezzedine Al-Qassam Brigades, Khaled Abu Bakr, and another cadre of Hamas' armed wing Rabieh Barikeh, were killed by tank shelling during an IDF incursion east of Khan Younes, in the southern Gaza Strip, according to local medical sources.
Two other local officials from the Al-Qassam Brigades, Mohammed al-Qassas and Mohammed Daoud, were killed when an Israeli helicopter opened fire in the same area.
Their bodies were discovered later.
According to Palestinian security sources, the four fighters conducted a surveillance operation in the border area between the Palestinian enclave and Israel.
An attack on a tunnel dug by the Palestinians
According to the same sources and witnesses, an Israeli tank and an armoured bulldozer made a hundred-and-a-hundred-meter incursion into the territory before withdrawing afterwards.
The clash lasted half an hour, according to witnesses.
In a statement, a Hamas spokesman, Sami Abu Zouhri, paid tribute to the four "heroes" and claimed that Israeli soldiers had died during the confrontation.
Hamas congratulates the heroes of Al-Qassam who fell defending the territory during an incursion by the Zionist occupier in Khan Younes.
Several enemies were killed and wounded during the operation.
“Hamas assures that Gaza will be hell for the occupier,” the spokesman threatened.
For its part, the Israeli army indicated that the target of its operation was initially a section of a large tunnel dug into Israeli territory from the Palestinian enclave, discovered on October 7 and intended, according to the army, for "terrorist activities".
Hamas claimed responsibility for the use of tunnels to combat Israel, noting that the goal was to abduct Israeli soldiers to exchange them for Palestinian prisoners.
The operation was intended to prevent future terrorist attacks using this tunnel, an Israeli military statement said.
During the operation, Hamas unleashed an explosive device targeting IDF forces and wounded 5 soldiers.
Israeli Attack in Northern Syria
"This mission was imperative because of the risk of using the terrorist tunnel for attacks against Israeli civilians," military spokesman Peter Lerner said.
In addition, Israel struck an air military base in north-western Syria, targeting a shipment of missiles destined for the Lebanese Shiite Hezbollah movement, the Al-Arabiya satellite channel reported Thursday.
A U.S. official confirmed an "Israeli strike" but did not give details about the target.
“In the past, the targets have been missiles transferred to Hezbollah,” he added.
Israeli government officials have refused to confirm any information regarding such an attack.
Citing "exclusive sources" that it did not name, the Saudi-powered chain said that "the bombardment targeted a shipment of surface-to-air missiles that was destined for Hezbollah in Lebanon", in reference to the powerful Lebanese Shiite movement that is fighting rebels alongside Syrian forces.
Earlier, a Syrian NGO, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights, had reported several explosions heard Wednesday at dawn at an air defense base in Sonar Jablé, near Latakia, on the Syrian coast.
This organization based on a network of activists and medical sources had not been able to identify the origin of the explosions.
Back in black and white for Marie Chouinard, who presents at Danse Danse her two new group choreographies: the first inspired by the inks and poems of Henri Michaux, the second infused by Satie's famous gymnopedias piano solos.
An evening that allows you to see all the prism of Chouinard, from art to tics and ink black to white.
A white screen serves as a background wall.
On the carpet, also white, in the immense space that is the Théâtre Maisonneuve, advances, in profile, fine like a line and dressed in black, a body.
On the screen, an ink by Henri Michaux is projected.
The body, simple stroke, takes its shape.
And this shape will be that of the whole room: a string of physical imitations of the drawings.
The dancers, entirely dressed in black, only their hands and faces uncovered, wait on either side of the playground, rush when the next graph appears, embodies it, comes out running.
This black-and-white game by Henri Michaux is made in relays of solos, in groups in unison (a mass that makes solo), in large group of twelve solos where each performs his sign.
We think of "Gloire of the morning", where, in solo, Chouinard performed a series of choreographic tasks written on cartels, posed online at the forefront.
It's the same principle, giant PowerPoint version.
Here the music is loud, percussion and electric guitars.
The energy is high, the pace is always fast.
There will be broken when Carol Prieur takes refuge under the dance rug, to deliver, microphone in hand, without sobriety or restraint, a part of Michaux's poem, monochord and monotonous in the screaming, and continue while dancing.
And the music to leave, and the sequence to resume.
This sound and energetic discharge, long, if possible, ends up deafening and numbing.
As the regularity of the visual rhythm also does.
There are a few beautiful flashes - the creation of images has always been a strength at Chouinard - such as this hair that ruffles or these black fabrics that lengthen the lines.
However, the choreographic approach lacks composition.
We have the impression that the choreographer, instead of going to meet the work of Michaux, imposed herself, with her tics.
As a result, the bodies seem flat in front of the drawings.
Michaux's inks, such as Gestalt's forms, open a wider and giboyous imaginary than dancing, more porous.
Only the end, in negative, with its dancers chopped by a strobe light and the white signs in a suddenly black theatre, touches the magic.
The public loved it, the welcome was warm.
Gymnopedias, after intermission, is richer.
The scene is partly draped with gray, the dancers and a piano are covered with fabrics, like furniture in an abandoned house.
At the piano, dancers take turns playing the scores.
Their clumsiness in music, touching, gives a real fragility.
On stage, pass the couples, in love duos, very sensual, even erotic.
Group work, small transition solos, moving sets, choirs, writing is less binary.
Buffoons appear, also familiar to Chouinard, sporting the red nose, in very polarized male-female parades.
Loves are carnivorous, fleeing or players.
The gestures, laughters, and cries beautifully translate the archaic, the grace, and the ridiculousness of the bodies into coitus, the beings that blend together.
But the play takes its real flight after salvation.
A clown comes back on the scene when the audience leaves.
There, everything becomes permissible: the fourth wall falls, the explored universes are superimposed, the dancers cabotine, descend among the spectators, glove, smoke, short-circuit, play the codes of applause, stretch the sauce in a cleverly calculated disorder that remains sensual.
Joy and madness transpire, and it's Chouinard's art of contaminating the audience.
Google, Samsung and Hauwei are facing lawsuits regarding Nortel patents
The group, which owns thousands of former Nortel patents, began an avalanche of patent lawsuits Thursday against mobile phone manufacturers, including Google, the company it capped at the patent auction that took place after Nortel’s bankruptcy.
Rockstar, the consortium that acquired Nortel patents for US$4.5 billion, filed a lawsuit against Samsung Electronics Co Ltd, HTC Corp, Huawei and four other companies for patent infringement in the U.S. Assize Court in Texas.
Rockstar is jointly owned by Apple, Microsoft, Blackberry, Ericsson and Sony.
Google is accused of violating seven patents.
Patents contain technologies that make it possible to display advertising related to internet search terms, said the conclusions of the lawsuit, which is Google’s core business.
It has not been possible to reach the representatives of Samsung, Huawei, HTC and Rockstar.
Samsung, Huawei and HTC are making phones that run under Google’s Android operating system, which delivers fierce competition to Apple and Microsoft mobile products.
In 2011, Google made an initial $900 million offer for Nortel’s patents.
Google has increased its offer several times, to finally offer 4.4 billion dollars.
After losing Nortel patents to Rockstar, Google later acquired Motorola Molobility for an amount of US$12.5 billion, a market partly based on Motorola’s patent library.
“Although having failed in its attempt to acquire the patents at issue during the auctions, Google has infringed and continues to violate these patents,” the trial’s findings said.
Rockstar claims greater damages against Google because it claims that Google’s patent infringement is stubborn, according to the plaintiff.
Explosion of a house in Gesves: a defective water heater at the origin of the explosion
The grandma would have lit a cigarette during a gas leak.
The explosion of a home in Gesves, which left two seriously injured Friday morning, a 52-year-old grandmother and her 5-year-old grandson, is due to a gas leakage from a faulty water heater, said Friday evening the substitute of the King's prosecutor.
The expert dispatched to the site by the prosecution assures that the origin of the explosion is purely accidental.
The grandmother turned on a cigarette when there was a leak and an accumulation of gas.
The prognosis of the two victims is still committed.
The grandma was projected by the explosion and is badly burned.
The little boy, who was staying with his grandfather and companion, was by his side and was burned lighter.
"The two victims were intubated and conditioned long on the spot before being taken to the CHU of Liège, she by helicopter, him by ambulance, "said the mayor José Paulet, descended on the scene.
Traumatized, the grandfather was upstairs at the time of the explosion.
Healthy and safe, he was able to reach the ground floor by the stairs remained intact while the back wall of the house was totally destroyed.
The grandfather and tenant of the neighboring house, weakened by the explosion, were relocated by the president of the CPAS.
Firefighters from Andenne and Namur and the Arches police intervened.
Crisnée civil protection has stabilized both buildings.
Egypt calls on the first freely elected president to take an oath
Mohamed Morsi has taken the oath of inauguration, but it is unlikely that his day of triumph marks the end of political conflicts in Egypt.
Islamist Mohamed Morsi promised the emergence of a “new Egypt” when he tossed the oath of inauguration to the presidency, thus becoming the first freely elected president and succeeding Hosni Mubarak who was ousted from power 16 months earlier.
During his inauguration before the High Constitutional Court, Morsi also became Egypt's first freely elected Islamist president and Egypt's fifth head of state since the overthrow of the monarchy 60 years ago.
He sworen before the 18 judges in black court robe whose building on the edge of the Nile was built to resemble an Egyptian temple.
“We aspire to better tomorrows, a new Egypt and a second republic,” President Morsi said during the solemn ceremony broadcast live on public television.
“Today, the Egyptian people laid the foundations for a new life – absolute freedom, true democracy and stability,” said Morsi, a 60-year-old U.S.-based Muslim Brotherhood engineer, a fundamentalist group considered for more than 84 years since its formation as an illegal and harshly repressed organization by successive governments.
Hundreds of soldiers and police were guarding the building when Mr. Morsi arrived shortly after 11 a.m. local time, followed by a small convoy.
Only a few hundred supporters had gathered before the Court to acclaim the new president and, standing out from the presidential splendor of the Mubarak years, traffic was only briefly interrupted to allow the passage of his convoy in the busy streets connecting the city center to the southern suburbs.
Considered the uncharismatic “screen wheel” of the Muslim Brotherhood, his personal prestige has strengthened considerably since his victory and his speech of Friday during which he tried to present himself as the candidate not only of the Islamists but of all those who want to finish the work begun during the 2011 uprising against authoritarian President Mubarak.
“Egypt is today a civil, national, constitutional and modern state,” said Mohamed Morsi, in a blue suit and red tie, addressing the judges present in the room decorated with woodwork where he swore.
Later, Mr. Morsi went to Cairo's University where he gave his inaugural speech.
He was officially greeted by a military band that played the national anthem as he put himself at attention.
Marshal Hussein Tantawi attended the ceremony.
Upon his arrival, he was greeted by the song "The people and the army, hand in hand..." sung by thousands of people gathered in the university's large amphitheater.
Founded in 1908 and a former bastion of secular education, the University of Cairo later became a stronghold of Islamist student groups in the 1970s.
Morsi made a symbolic oath Friday in Tahrir Square, from which began the uprising that ended Mubarak's authoritarian regime last year, and vowed to recover the presidential powers whose military council, which replaced the ousted former president, dispossessed him.
But by agreeing to officially take an oath before the Court, rather than before Parliament as it is customary, he bows to the will of the army by showing that the struggle for power is not over.
Morsi’s speech in Tahrir Square was accompanied by spectacular populist gestures.
The shutdown in the U.S. has slowed the rise in the automotive market
The 16-day shutdown of most federal administrations weighed on growth in U.S. car sales in October, the automakers released on Friday, with five of the top six performing below expectations.
Detroit’s thread-standing automakers posted double-digit growth compared to October 2012, but only General Motors did better than expected.
Analysts expected an average of 15.4 million sales at an annualized and seasonally adjusted pace, but the market stood at 15.3 million according to Adam Jonas, an analyst at Morgan Stanley.
In a note to the bank's customers, he believes that this slight brake is explained in the first place by "the government 'shutdown' and its impact on consumer sentiment during the first half of the month".
However, GM reported an increase of nearly 16% of its sales last month to 226 402 vehicles, compared to 211 563 expected by the consensus.
The group's four brands have seen their commercial performance improve compared to October 2012.
Ford, which continues to benefit, among other things, from the success of its pick-ups, has seen its sales increase by 14% year-on-year to 191,985 vehicles, while the consensus awaited 194 301.
For its part, Chrysler, controlled by Fiat announced an 11% increase in sales to 140 083 vehicles, compared to 143,536 expected.
Toutoya and Nissan also show sales below the consensus, despite year-on-year increases of 8.8% and 14.2% respectively.
On the New York Stock Exchange, the GM share gained 1.4% at 16:35 GMT while Ford yielded 1.1%.
Renault lead in the stock market by Nissan's warning
Renault on Friday recorded the largest decline in the SBF 120 index in Paris after its partner Nissan Motor announced that it had declined by nearly 20% of its annual net profit forecast, traders report.
Nissan, which had to carry out such a revision, also announced a reshuffle of its management.
Several traders have reported that the Renault share was affected this morning by the "warning".
“It’s clearly Nissan’s profit warning,” says a trading manager of a Parisian broker.
As part of the Renault-Nissan alliance, Renault owns 43.4% of the capital of Nissan and the Japanese manufacturer 15% of the French, according to data from Nissan’s website.
Greece: Two dead in shooting near neo-Nazi party premises
Two people were killed and another was seriously injured Friday night by shots fired by two people who were aboard a motorcycle passing in front of a neo-Nazi party local Golden Aube in the westerly suburbs of Athens.
The police were currently unable to provide information on the identity of the victims and their possible political affiliation.
Some Greek media reported that members of Golden Aube had argued that the victims were guards of their premises.
The injured person was immediately hospitalized, according to the same source.
Anti-terrorist service police rushed to the central avenue of the Neo Iraklio suburb, where the incident took place and cordoned off the neighborhood.
The incident comes a few weeks after the indictment of sive deputies of Golden Dawn, including the leader and founder of the party, for participation in "a criminal organization" as part of an offensive against this party launched after the death of an anti-fascist musician by one of its members.
Scott Brown's appeal dismissed
Scott Brown, the captain of Celtic Glasgow, has had his appeal rejected and will be well suspended for his club's next two Champions League matches against Ajax and AC Milan.
Expelled against Barça folding a blow to Neymar, the Scottish midfielder saw his suspension extended to three games by UEFA.
In a statement, his club said he was "very disappointed", saying that his appeal was entirely justified.
Nearly 50 000 households are deprived of electricity throughout the province, shortly afternoon Friday, due to strong winds affecting many regions along the St. Lawrence River.
The sectors most affected are the Laurentians with 15 042 subscribers immersed in the dark, the Montérégie with 13 464 and the Outaouais with 8642.
The greater Montreal area is also experiencing its share of breakdowns with nearly 7000 households deprived of power in the metropolis and in Laval.
Did you think the travel agencies belonged to the past because of the Internet?
Flight Centre seems to be reversing the trend.
The company has revised its net profit forecasts upwards and expects record results thanks to vacationers from Australia and the United Kingdom.
The traveling agency now expects to make an underlying profit over the entire year between 325 and 340 million dollars before taxes, compared to a previous forecast of 305 to 315 million dollars.
If the current forecast is met, it will represent 12 to 17% growth compared to the real profit of US$ 290.4 million in 2011/12.
Managing Director Graham Turner said that Flip Centre made 8% profits in the first half of the year and that the second half had started in force, especially with regard to non-business travel in Australia and the United Kingdom.
“Since the beginning of the year, the 10 countries in which we operate are profitable, and many of them are on track to achieve record results throughout the year before deducting interest and taxes,” he said.
This applies to Australia and the United Kingdom, which are generally the countries that generate the biggest profits.
In Australia, the leisure travel sector rebounded in the second half of the year, making it possible to compensate for a slow-declining domestic business travel market.
Similarly in the United Kingdom, Flight Centre's leisure travel sector performed well, while business customers spent less.
Its subsidiary in the U.S. recovered its losses in the first half when seasonal demand is lower and was expected to derive profit over the entire fiscal year for the third consecutive time.
Flight Centre shares grew 3 cents to $38.20 yesterday.
Unknowns shoot a hotel near the pyramids of Cairo
Encagouled strangers opened fire this Friday on a hotel near the pyramids of Cairo in Egypt without causing casualties, as a result of an apparent quarrel with dismissed employees.
The attackers fled, said the spokesman of the Ministry of the Interior, police general Abdel Latif.
This attack comes as Egypt receives almost no tourists since the army impeached Islamist President Mohamed Morsi in early July and repressed the protests of his supporters in blood.
Schools are encouraged to focus more on mathematics, spelling and grammar
As part of the courses in English literature, students will have to study at least one play by Shakespeare, a 19th-century novel, a romantic poem, and a work of contemporary British fiction from 1914 to the present day.
The review will also introduce "new texts" to encourage a more diverse reading.
A course combining English literature and language will be abolished.
Starting in 2015, students will have to pass an independent language GCSE, and will be strongly encouraged to choose a separate qualification in English literature.
The Ministry of Education is due to publish its new English and math programs tomorrow – the raw subjects to undergo a radical review.
It will make changes in other core matters next year.
In addition, Ofqual, the Regulating Bureau of Examinations, will unveil a reorganization of the GCSE structure, with a new scoring system and less courses.
Speaking during the summer, Michael Gove, Minister of Education, said there was a “broad consensus on the need to reform our system of reviews to restore public confidence,” stressing that GCSEs would be “more challenging, more ambitious and more rigorous.”
Studies show that English schools spend less time on math (116 hours a year, or 3 hours a week during the school year) than most other countries.
In comparison, Australian schools offer an average of 143 hours per year, and students in Singapore do about 138 hours.
While there is no formal obligation to devote more hours of schedule to math, sources close to the coalition government have indicated that in-depth math GCSE – as well as the greater emphasis on subjects in international rankings – would likely encourage schools to provide more hours of classes.
The programme will place greater emphasis on "real-world problems", including financial mathematics.
This judgment means that a piece of legislation passed in July in this state and largely challenging the right to abortion will be able to come into force.
An octogenarian greets children with a rifle at Le Muy for Halloween
Minors aged 9 and 13 were doing door-to-door to claim candy on Halloween night.
One of the residents of the Muy district visited by the children opened the door holding his shotgun.
The 86-year-old man explained to the gendarmes that he was afraid.
His gun was seized.
Ex-Gestapo chief buried in Jewish cemetery
Müller, who disappeared at the end of the Second World Wire without ever finding his trail, was actually buried in a mass grave of a Jewish cemetery in Berlin, says the leader of the German Resistance Memorial, Professor Johannes Tuchel, in Bild.
Müller did not survive the end of the war.
"His body was buried in 1945 in a mass grave in the Jewish Cemetery in Berlin-Mitte," he told the large-distured daily based on archival documents.
This revelation, 68 years after the end of Adolf Hitler's Nazi regime, would respond to one of the great enigmas of the Post-War period.
The German secret services, the BND, claimed that during the summer of 1949, Müller was in Karlovy Vary in Czechoslovakia, according to a document obtained by Bild.
The secret services were completely wrong.
"From August 1945, Müller's body was found in a temporary tomb near the former Ministry of Reich Aviation by a commando," Tuchel said.
He was wearing "a general's uniform."
"In the left inner pocket were notably his states of service with a photo," he continues.
Bild also publishes a document from the district town hall of Berlin’s Mitte district indicating that he was buried in the neighborhood’s Jewish cemetery.
The President of the Central Council of the Jews of Germany, Dieter Graumann, said he was shocked by this revelation.
That one of the most brutal Nazi sadists is buried in a Jewish cemetery, it's enormity of bad taste.
"We trample the memory of the victims with his feet," he tweeted.
Heinrich Müller is one of the important figures of the Third Reich who have never been captured.
He participated in the Wannsee conference in January 1942, which decided on the "final solution" and had, in particular, under his command, Adolf Eichmann, responsible for the "logistics" of the extermination of the Jews, sentenced to death and executed in Israel in 1962.
Pamela Anderson cuts her iconic blonde curls and adopts a spectacular boy's cup.
