He walks through the mud, the jungle and crosses rivers to offer free medical assistance
Dr. Georges Bwelle provides free health care in villages in Cameroon
Dr. Bwelle and his team spend almost all of their weekends seeing hundreds of patients
There are not many doctors in this West African country, barely 1 per 5 000 inhabitants.
Vote here or through your mobile device
Dr. Georges Bwelle is one of 10 CNN Heroes of the Year 2013.
You can vote for him, or one of the other 10 heroes, to elect "CNN Hero of the Year."
This person will receive $250 000 to continue their extraordinary work.
For 21 years Georges Bwelle watched his sick father alternate periods of consciousness and unconsciousness and went to hospitals that were not equipped to help him.
Jamef Bwelle was injured in a car accident in 1981 near Yaoundé, the capital of Cameroon.
At first, he had only one broken arm, but he contracted an infection that spread to his brain, creating a hematoma whose after-effects he was going to carry the rest of his life.
"There were no neurosurgers in Cameroon," Bwelle said.
We'd've taken him abroad if we'd had enough money.
Instead, Dr. Bwelle spent years accompanying his father to crowded clinics and hospitals, so that he would get any treatment he could benefit from.
"It's not easy," Bwelle said.
You can leave the house at 5 a.m
There's a lot of patients.
People can die because they're waiting too long.
The situation hasn't changed much since Dr. Bwelle's father died in 2002.
In Cameroon, there are only 1 doctor per 5 000 inhabitants, according to the World Health Organization.
By way of comparison, the ratio in the United States is 1 doctor per 413 inhabitants.
And even if they could see a doctor, many Cameroonians would not be able to pay the consultation.
Two out of five people in the country live below the poverty line, and almost three-quarters of the country's health costs are in the private sector.
"The only problem they have is poverty," Dr. Bwelle said.
Because of poverty, they can’t take advantage of life.
After seeing his father and so many of his compatriots suffer, Dr. Bwelle was determined to try to change the course.
Dr. Georges Bwelle and his team of volunteers have practiced more than 700 surgical procedures free of charge over the past year.
He became a doctor himself, working as a vascular surgeon at Yaoundé Central Hospital.
And he created a non-profit humanitarian association, ASCOVIME, which travels to rural areas during weekends to provide free health care.
Since 2008, he and his group of volunteers have helped nearly 32 000 people.
Almost every Friday, with about thirty people, they pile up in vans after fixing the medical equipment on the roof and passing through difficult grounds to get to the villages that need help the most.
Luck doesn’t always follow them.
They had many times to push the vehicles to cross rivers or muddy tracks.
But when they arrive, they are greeted as heroes: the community organizes a party, sings and dances and offers them the best accommodations available.
In these villages, free health care is a good reason to party, and Dr. Bwelle – with his big smile and overflowing energy – is more than happy to join the festivities.
The next morning, the team begins to receive hundreds of patients.
“We receive 500 people on every visit,” Bwelle said.
They sometimes live 60 km from the village, and they come on foot.
In this homeless hospital, a variety of health care is provided.
Many people are receiving treatment for malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, parasites and sexually transmitted diseases.
Others may receive crutches, a collected pair of glasses, or a free birth certificate – a document needed to go to school, but many poor families simply can’t afford to buy it.
In the evening, the team practices simple surgical acts under local anesthesia.
The operations are usually carried out in a school, a town hall or a house; after the intervention, the patient gets up and goes to the recovery room to leave his place to the next person.
Thanks to the lighting of the generator set, the operating room and sterilization of the equipment, Dr. Bwelle and his volunteers work until the early hours of Sunday morning.
It's an exhausting rhythm, but the musicians of the villages generally help the team to remain motivated.
“They hit drums all night to keep us awake so that we can continue to work,” Bwelle said.
On Sundays, the team returns to the city, tired but proud of the work it has done.
The group, made up of Cameroonian physicians and medical students, has performed 700 surgical procedures free of charge over the past year, and it knows that their presence can make all the difference for those they help.
A man explained that the gratuitous operation he had undergone to treat a hernia would allow him to work again.
“It’s going to change my future and my family’s future,” he said.
In addition to supporting this itinerant hospital and working as a surgeon at the hospital, Dr. Bwelle also works at night in private medical offices around Yaoundé.
It is this second job, he explains, which finances at 60% its association; the rest is covered by private donations.
"I don't know when he's asleep," said Katie O'Malley, a second-year medical student at Drexel University in Philadelphia and a volunteer on Dr. Bwelle's group.
He's still in the hospital or trying to find money for his association so he can run these campaigns.
For medical students and nursing students like Katie O'Malley, who come from the United States and Europe to join Mr. Bwelle in his missions, this is a field learning opportunity they don't have at home.
"Were able to participate in operations to help sponge the blood or pass the instruments to Dr. Bwelle," O'Malley explained.
It’s not something you have the opportunity to do in America when you’re a second-year medical student.
Voluntary students generally pay for their trip to Cameroon, and often arrive with collected medical equipment.
But once in Yaoundé, their pension, transportation and teaching are taken care of by Dr. Bwelle.
"He's a hero, there's no doubt," said Katie O'Malley.
He gives his life for this association, and his desire to help the Cameroonian people is not about to go out.
For Dr. Bwelle, the relatively constant workload is not a difficulty.
Helping others to live happily by keeping the promise he made to his father is something that brings him immense joy.
“I’m so happy when I’m doing this work,” Bwelle added.
And I'll think of my father.
I hope he sees what I'm doing.
Making people laughter and relieving pain are the reasons that drive me to do this.
Visit ASCOVIME to see how to help.
The Two France by Pierre Nora
Perplexed, academics have long struggled to define and situate Pierre Nora.
Teacher in amphitheaters and classrooms?
Undoubtedly, but highlighting its weakness for crossroads, Sciences-Po, Les Hautes Etudes.
Referee of the works of others in his office on Gaston-Gallimard Street?
Indeed, but ignoring the hidden work and underground writing of the publisher.
Heir of the armchair occupied Fontenelle at the French Academy?
Admittedly again, but knowing that such a coronation cannot replace the true holy oil, that of the thesis, which happened.
They gladly recognized in him a great awakener, animator and unifier of their works, but pointing out that he showed little inclination to produce it himself.
In this uncertain image the monument of "Places of memory", to which he attached his name, also contributed.
The wicked languages may have seen a gigantic flea market, presented by an intelligent commentator, with a sharp glance and weight of erudition, but incurably dilettant.
The two books published by Nora almost two years ago have already established that the one who passed for not writing had written a lot.
The book here should definitely do him justice.
However, we find, once again, the temptation of ubiquity, the intellectual vagrancy, the jubilation put to jump as with joined feet from one subject to another.
But we quickly understand that the disparate objects collected in this book with such a beautiful title bring the fleaker back to one and the same passion: that of discovering the constitutive knot of the French identity.
Why tragic calls for national unity?
Identity: It's a period concern.
Recently, we were collectively invited to give a definition of it.
But in the peremptory spirit of our leaders at the time, French identity was a timeless essence.
It was only necessary to deploy the accidents, and the case, Jacobinism obliges, was entrusted to the prefects and sub-prefects, authorized interpreters.
The one that Pierre Nora hunts is quite different.
He does not pose as an heir of an eternal frankness, he refuses the definition.
An analyst worried about familiar strangeness, he wanders in a forest of symbols, stopped at every step by an object in the shape of a rebus, tormented by irritating questions.
Including it, which is born of the enterprise itself: why, in a country so permanently established within its borders, so formerly framed and solidly masonry, we hear these dramatic calls for unity?
They are so pressing, suggests Pierre Nora, only to conjure the disorder born of the discords of French history.
Franks and Gauls, Armagnacs and Burgundians, Catholics and Protestants: the forces of division, here, are very old.
And the most emblematic is obviously the one that breaks the national history in two.
Starting from the earthquake of the Revolution, the French have two stories and they have two nations, one monarchical, the other revolutionary.
The second wanted the death of the first, but without succeeding in denying it; anxious on the contrary to find its sacredness and all the more eager for unity and indivisibility that it had cut off the head to the one who was the obvious and mighty incarnation.
And, from there, an invincibly binary nation, divided between left and right, secularism and Catholicism, adoration and hatred of revolution.
To make feel this singularity of the French identity, nothing beats the four steps out of Hexagona, so that the American getaway of Pierre Nora could well be the heart of his book.
For America and France both made a revolution, drafted a declaration of rights, wanted to found a new society.
But American cousinage only better underlines what is unique to us.
There, men who had left their Ancien Regime in England and no longer had to care.
Here, men cluttered with an ancient indigenous regime, and all the more radical.
There a consensual revolution when here she sowed drama and confides it.
There founding fathers always honored, and here revolutionary ancestors whose models are difficult to make, and who have also killed each other.
There is an institutional constency, and here a cascade of constitutions, like so many mistakes to catch up and attempts to resume.
France who is leaving, France is coming
However, there has been a time when the French believed they could sew the torn dress from their history, and overcome the curse of the number two.
At this Third Republic, a central and creative moment, Pierre Nora showed a lot of interest and even tenderness: saluting those who then worked to repair the revolutionary fracture, teaching schoolchildren all that in ancient France obscurely prepared modern France and by offering them a unified version of their history.
However, this peaceful identity had only for a while.
Here it is again in debate, jostled by immigration of a new type, threatened by the rise of demanding minorities, absorbed in the European space.
The book therefore offers both the fascinating portrait of France that is leaving and the circumspective sketch of France that is coming.
With, as a bonus, the portrait of the historian, who should reserve some surprises.
It shows that the shelf was a cassanier.
The man of multiple curiosities turned, slightly obsessive, around the same idea.
The edge prunder stood in the center of the center.
And the one who had deposed, more than half a century ago, a subject on the idea of a nation, but had evaded the initiatory exercise of the thesis, confides to us, cum grano salis, that he will have ended up doing it.
Admittedly in a less steep, more exploded and more subtle form.
Don’t make a mistake about it, just as constraining.
Because much more than the imposed figure of the university curriculum, this thesis says the inner necessity of a life.
A former hostage in Lebanon: "Return is quite complex to manage"
Abducted in Beirut on March 8, 1986 with three members of his "Antenne 2" team filming a Hezbollah demonstration, journalist Jean-Louis Normandin was released nearly 21 months later, on November 27, 1987.
Retired since 2008, he participated in 2004 in the creation of the association for the defense of hostages "Hostages of the world", which he has presided for several years.
Its main objective: to obtain the possibility for the hostages to lodge a complaint and bring their hostage-takers before the International Criminal Court.
Detained for more than 1 000 days, the four hostages of Aqmi (Al-Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb) Daniel Larribe, Thierry Dol, Pierre Legrand and Marc Féret were released on Tuesday.
After medical examinations at the Val-de-Grâce military hospital on Wednesday afternoon, they have since been able to find their families.
And try, little by little, to resume the course of their lives.
"Le Nouvel Observateur" questioned Jean-Louis Normandin, 62 years old, former big reporter, retired since 2008, president of the association "Otages du monde".
From the evening of your release, you were on the 20h plateau of "Antenne 2".
What are your memories of your liberation today?
On site in particular, in Beirut.
When I'm released, I find myself in the trunk of a car where I meet - without seeing it because it's dark - Roger Auque.
He says "we're released", but I'm not sure about it, and tells me that we can still be killed.
We're very excited, but very tense too.
It's war, and those who drive us are also very tense.
They're laying us on the edge of a sidewalk.
There's Syrian soldiers.
We then arrive on the site of the Hotel Summerland where the press is present.
There are French people taking us to the French embassy.
It's the time of the first phone calls for parents, family, friends, the press...
I remember taking a bath for an hour.
And having dinner in a t-shirt at the French Embassy.
I also remember a short night chatting with Roger and Marchiani.
The tension is still there because we don't have the means to evacuate to Larnaca airport in Cyprus.
Arrived there by helicopter, we take a private plane that takes us back to France via Corfou and Solenzara, where Pasqua embarks.
How did your arrival on French soil work?
We're landing in Orly.
Chirac, Prime Minister, is here.
That's kind of brutal.
There's an incredible joster.
And a lot of media.
When I left there were three television channels.
I'll come back and there's plenty.
The descent from the staircase of the plane, the reunion with my son and my parents, my friends, all this is extremely strong in emotion.
Between the airport and at my home, many motorcycles follow our car that Roussin drives.
Bikers shoot each other to take pictures and follow me home where I have to do a little of the police to prevent some from rising.
There is tremendous media pressure.
Doesn't stop, we're well placed to find out...
But the state of emotion and fatigue is such that it is difficult to see clearly.
We're coming out of a hole and we're suddenly in the media.
It's a little complicated, a little violent, quite complex to manage.
But we've done the hardest.
The same evening, I'm at 8pm, the next day I'll be on TV too.
Then begins a kind of rehabilitation to life, very gently, for one or two months that are a bit like a vacation.
How did medical examinations and DGSE debriefing go?
I received a first medical examination in Corsica.
The rest was done in Val-de-Grâce the days following my return: radios, examinations of all kinds and meeting with a psychiatrist.
It's not a session that does everything but we know that we can count on him, remind him if necessary, that we're not left to ourselves.
That's part of the procedure.
I also met with the intelligence services.
They asked me about the hostage-takers.
That made sense.
That didn't bother me.
How are you reacting today to the release of Niger's four hostages?
I'm listening to what's being said.
Debate on ransoms in particular.
Some things piss me off, some less.
I'm trying to make a difference between questions about my past as a hostage and my cap as president of the "Otages of the World" association that allows me to put distance, and seems to me much more interesting to address.
For example, I wonder about the definition of resilience.
Above all, I fight, with others, for the recognition of the legal status of the hostage.
In my opinion, there is a problem with semantics.
It is necessary to describe the taking of "political" hostage-taking - which is the case most of the time - and allow the hostages to have access to justice, to file complaints and to bring their hostage-takers before the International Criminal Court.
Today, once again, everyone is in compassion, in emotion, and rejoices at the release of these hostages.
But who says maybe these hostages could also have access to justice?
There's a court in The Hague for that.
Why don't we say to the hostage-takers "you have flouted the rules of war, like those of all Geneva conventions, we will bring you to justice"?
However, it seems well-founded, legitimate, struck with common sense.
This message is heard, but not listened to, and it shocks me.
That's my main fight.
Egyptian Islamists on the streets to denounce Morsi’s trial
Anger rumbles in the ranks of Egyptian Islamists.
Two days before the opening of the trial of ousted President Mohamed Morsi, they took to the streets.
A mobilization throughout Egypt to demand the restoration of the country's first democratically elected president.
This is not a trial underlines this Muslim Brotherhood activist.
So far, no lawyers have been able to meet him and no volunteers have been able to take a copy of the documents in the case.
It's not a trial. It's a farce.
The trial of President Morsi is a false trial reproaches a pro-Morsi, it is he who is supposed to judge Sisi, not the opposite.
Morsi must try Sisi for the Rabaa massacres, the Nahda massacres, and the massacre of the Republican Guard.
Sisi is a liar and traitor.
Clashes broke out in Alexandria, the police used tear gas, 60 protesters were arrested.
On Monday, 20 000 police officers will be deployed in front of the Cairo Police Academy, where Mohamed Morsi will be judged.
Hagel blames 9 U.S. states for violating gay rights
Since the federal state has recognized same-sex marriage, “all military spouses are entitled to an identity card of the Department of Defense and the benefits that come with it,” the minister recalled in New York in an address before the Anti-Defamation League to fight anti-Semitism.
"But some states refuse to issue these cards to same-sex spouses in the facilities of the National Guard" implanted in their territory, he denounced, blaming these states for violating federal law and the principle of equality.
Without these cards, these people cannot benefit from the many social or health services provided at these bases, or access their stores.
The Secretary of Defense said he had ordered the head of the National Guard, General Frank Grass, to ensure the implementation of federal law.
The slingshot of the nine states began by Texas, which refuses to implement these benefits on the settlements of the Texas National Guard, because of a conflict between the Texas law and the federal law on same-sex marriage.
He was joined in his refusal by Indiana, Georgia, Florida, Mississippi, Louisiana, Oklahoma, South Carolina and West Virginia, according to a senior defense official.
The Pentagon estimates the population concerned with the recognition of same-sex marriage to about 5600 active people, 17 000 including the National Guard, the reserve and pensioners.
Sixth edition of the Play Skateboard this Saturday in Hyères
The sixth edition of the Play Skateboard takes place this Saturday, November 2 at the skate park in Hyères.
Organized by the SK8 Unity section of the Bump association, the competition should bring together the best skateboarders in the region.
Enrolments are taken on site Saturday morning from 9 a.m. and the qualifying phases will begin immediately, at 10 a.m., before the final scheduled at 3 p.m
The public will be able to admire the technical prowess of young people who, for some, are already attending competitions at the highest level, such as the young Hyerois prodigy Lorenzo Palumbo.
At only 10 years old, he has already conquered his place this year for the European championships in Copenhagen and he likes nothing so much as to show competitors who exceed him by three heads.
Toronto mayor hunts journalists who came to question him about a drug case
In Canada, the mayor of Toronto is suspected of drugs, according to several media.
A video handed over to justice seems to support these suspicions.
He, Rob Ford, has always denied using crack, while acknowledging having a penchant for cannabis.
This Thursday, he chased from his property several journalists who came to interrogate him.
We also think that sometimes images do not need an explanation or comment.
Rabies detected in a cat in Val-d'Oise
The authorities announced Thursday that a case of rabies had been detected in a kitten in Val-d'Oise, obviously of foreign origin while France has been free of indigenous cases since 2001.
The kitten was found on October 25 in Argenteuil and he died on October 28.
Diagnosis of rabies has been confirmed by the Pasteur Institute.
"An epidemiological investigation has been undertaken to identify and take care of the people who could have come into contact with this kitten between October 8 and October 28 inclusive," the ministries said.
"Five people who have been in contact with the kitten have already been identified" and received preventive treatment.
"In humans, the preventive treatment of human rabies, administered after contact with the carrier animal, but before the onset of symptoms, is very effective," the statement said.
The ministries now call the people who would have been bitten, scratched, scratched, or licked on a mucous membrane or on skin harmed by this kitten or whose animal would have been in contact with this kitten between October 8 and 28 to contact 08.11.00.06.95 between 10 a.m. and 6 p.m. from November 1.
"France has been rage-free since 2001, this kitten or the mother has been imported from another country, not unscathed," the statement said.
The Ministry of Agriculture specifies that the last "aboriginal" case of rabies recorded dates back to December 1998 on a fox and that "France is declared officially free of this disease in November 2001 by the International Office of Epizootics (OIE)".
A case of rabies on a dog illegally imported from Gambia had been recorded in 2008.
“Rabies is a fatal disease if left untreated in time,” the ministries recall and is transmissible for nearly 15 days prior to the onset of the first symptoms of the disease.
Obama reverses his health system reform
Under an avalanche of critics, President Obama retracted his unambiguous and often repeated promise that “if you’re satisfied with your health plan, you can keep it.”
After hundreds of thousands of people have received notices of termination from their contractors, Republicans have criticized the president over the past few days accusing him of misleading the American people.
Yesterday, Barack Obama adjusted his original promise.
“The vast majority of people who have health insurance that’s right for them can keep it,” he said in a speech in Boston.
Speaking about what he calls “an effervescence in the news” about terminations, President Bamb has prompted Americans who were receiving these notices to seek new insurance in the market.
Most people will be able to obtain comprehensive and better-adapted health plans for the same or even cheaper than expected.
“You’ll come out the winner,” he said.
The Administration announced that it should not be surprising if the 5% of the population who take out their own insurance find themselves forced to change plans because their coverage does not meet the new standards required under the Affordable Care Act.
“Let me speak directly to these Americans: you deserve better,” Kathleen Sebelius said during her testimony before the Energy and Trade Committee in Washington.
Sebelius, who oversees the implementation of the Affordable Care Act, said that the October launch of the online marketplace had failed “dismatilately.”
“I’m as frustrated and irritated as anyone,” she added.
I can't wait to regain your trust.
It was an exasperated Kathleen Sebelius who uttered this sentence, while she believed her microphone cut, by addressing an assistant sitting behind her at yesterday’s Assembly session following a contentious exchange of views with Billy Long, a Republican representative from Missouri, to find out if she should join Obamacare.
For more than three hours, Billy Long repeatedly asked Ms. Sebelius why "architect" of the Affordable Care Act had not voluntarily renounced government-sponsored insurance to take out a plan through HealthCare.gov, which she now touts millions of Americans.
Vettel holds a special helmet in Abu Dhabi
The lucky winner, Jake Vite Prekop, combined the colors of the car brand and the German flag, while integrating the notions of speed and heat at the track level.
Germany's Sebastian Vettel, a four-time Formula 1 world champion, squeezed a special helmet from a 21-year-old Mexican fan on Friday.
Vettel has chosen among 1,500 drawings sent from all over the world, as part of a contest launched by one of the sponsors, an automotive brand, of its team.
He was invited to follow the Abu Dhabi GP, with a friend, and can therefore see the German champion closely, on the track and in his booth.
Vettel was supposed to wear this helmet on Friday and Saturday, in practice.
It is likely that he will have another special helmet on Sunday, for the race, intended to mark his 4th consecutive world title.
Aubervilliers resident launches his Suburban Party
Don’t be mistaken.
In the spirit of Abdel-Malik Djermoune, its founder, the "Party of the Suburbs" does not apply only to suburbs.
I have chosen a map of France as a logo, he justifies.
When I talk about the suburbs, I speak to all those who are excluded from the great national family.
Nevertheless, his project, presented Thursday at a press conference in his native commune of Aubervilliers, was born from the desire to better defend multiculturalism, this great cultural melting pot that characterizes above all neighborhoods.
“My proposal #1 is to create a Ministry of Multiculturalism,” he said.
Abdel-Malik Djermoune, 50, territorial attaché, now claims to be "100% apolitical" even if it has not always been neutral.
Activist in 2002 alongside Jean-Pierre Chevénement, he then locally supported Dominique de Villepin between 2010 and 2011.
“I know very well that the values of equality that I advocate in my program are attached to the left, but if the people of the right are ready to support me, I will also listen to them,” he said.
It's only the extremist parties I don't talk about.
In addition to multiculturalism, Abdel-Malik Djermoune has built his program - consultable on the Internet - based on many themes supposed to speak to suburban inhabitants and especially to young people: the right to vote for foreigners, the legalization of cannabis, the civil majority at 16, the rehabilitation of the function of building guardian...
It remains to find candidates to form lists and defend his ideas on the political scene. "For the municipal elections of 2014, it may be fair, he admits."
It's a matter of time and money.
The Suburb Party should still be represented in Aubervilliers through my candidacy and I also hope in other cities.
Abdel-Malik Djermoune claims to have already seven heads of lists in metropolitan France and Martinique.
"And the support emails I've been receiving since yesterday don't all emanate from the suburbs," he said.
John Kerry admitted, unprecedentedly, that U.S. spying "has gone too far inappropriately"
John Kerry pointed to a softening of the U.S.’s defensive attitude towards its surveillance programs by admitting, unprecedentedly, that on several occasions spying “has gone too far inappropriately.”
The Secretary of State also admitted that he, just like Barack Obama, ruled on “autopilot” when a series of incendiary revelations by whistleblower Edward Snowden about the NSA’s spying activities emerged.
Leaks put the U.S. government at the center of a diplomatic storm with its allies.
Speaking at an intergovernmental conference in London via video link, Kerry said: “It is undeniable that the President, myself and other members of the government became aware of certain things in autopilot mode because we had the opportunity, from World War II until the difficult years of the Cold War, then of course 9/11.”
He then became the first high-ranking member of the U.S. government to admit that U.S. espionage had crossed the line, but insisted that everyone's rights had not been violated.
He said: “In some cases, it went too far inappropriately.”
And the President is committed to trying to clarify and make it clear to the people that he is now conducting a thorough review of these practices so that no one feels deceived.
I assure you that innocent people will not be the victims of abuse as part of this process.
Kerry insisted, however, that the NSA was a force acting for good and that its surveillance operations had saved many lives.
He added: “We’re living in a new world where people are ready to blew themselves up.”
There is radical extremism in the world that is determined to try to kill people, blow them up and attack governments.
What if you could intercept their communications and stop them before they act?
We have indeed prevented planes from falling, buildings to explode and people from being murdered because we were able to know the facts in advance.
Meanwhile, U.S. legislators will travel to Europe to address concerns about the alleged U.S. espionage case and convince Europeans of the need to continue joint counter-terrorism efforts with the United States, the Senate subcommittee on European affairs said Thursday.
Senator Chris Murphy of Connecticut indicated that he had spoken to members of the European Parliament and others this week and that he was concerned about the threats they had made regarding a possible withdrawal of anti-terrorist organizations because of the sense of frustration raised by the surveillance of the National Security Agency.
“It’s really important for the protection of national security in the U.S. that Europeans stay by our side in the mutual commitment in the fight against terrorism,” Murphy, a Democrat serving his first term, and chairman of the U.S. Senate Subcommittee on European Affairs, said in an interview from Washington.
And I’m going to Europe to make it clear that we need to continue to work together to fight terrorism, despite the anger provoked by NSA programs.
The press reports that the NSA picked up millions of communications in Europe, which undermined relations with some U.S. allies, yet the agency’s director said this week that it was inaccurate and that it was a misunderstanding because it involved metadata that NATO allies had collected and shared with the United States.
Other revelations have reported documents leaked by Snowden that the NSA had intercepted data and communications from German Chancellor Angela Merkel’s mobile phone and those of 34 other heads of state.
Intelligence Director James Clapper justified allied espionage by stating that it was necessary and that it was common practice on both sides.
In this tumult atmosphere, Murphy indicated that his office was organizing a trip from Congress, which is expected to take place this year, and that he hoped the delegation would include members from both parties and both chambers.
The names of the other participating legislators should be disclosed in the coming days.
He indicated that the route remained to be defined.
Murphy said that the purpose of this trip was to improve relationships, but also to show "affectionate firmness."
He explained that European Heads of State must be honest with their citizens about the type of espionage programmes they have been applying themselves for years.
"We can change our surveillance programs in order to better protect the rights of Europeans, but they must also understand that we aren't the only ones in the world doing espionage," Murphy said.
In the meantime, Kerry planned to travel this weekend to the Middle East and Poland to manage the resentment generated by U.S. strategies in Syria, Egypt and Iran, as well as U.S. surveillance activities.
The London Stock Exchange closed down on Thursday, weighed down by Shell's bad results and the day after the Fed's announcement to maintain its support for the economy, as expected.
An Australian woman appeals her prison sentence in Thailand
A 21-year-old resident of Sydney, sentenced to 15 days in prison in Phuket for falsely claiming to have been assaulted by a taxi driver, appealed the trial and was released on bail.
Stevie Rochelle Bamford was initially found guilty by a Phuket Provincial Court on June 15 of misrepresentations after telling Thai police that a local taxi driver, accompanied by two other men, had assaulted her early on Sunday, June 10.
However, CCTV footage later revealed that she had safely returned to her hotel after separating from her Australian boyfriend.
Phuthee police interrogated Bamford for two days before she confessed to having fabricated the story.
She was detained in a cell of the local police station prior to the court hearing.
Bamford was sentenced to a 15-day prisone sentence at a low-security detainment center on the outskirts of Phuket rather than a women’s prison.
This is the daughter of former Australian Rugby League player Peter Tunks, who appealed to Canberra’s Foreign Ministry to help his daughter.
Tunks told Sydney’s Sunday Telegraph that the whole family was “extremely concerned” about her daughter’s well-being and wanted her to return to Australia.
“Obviously we’ve had a distressing time, but we hope to bring her home safely as soon as possible,” Tunks said.
Bamford appealed the judgment and was released following the payment of a bail of 50 000 baht.
In Australia, the press said that in the meantime, she was on vacation in the Krabi region of southern Thailand.
Thai judicial sources said Bamford was represented by a local lawyer in Phuket, but warned that the appeal could lead to an increase in the sentence of up to two years' imprisonment and incarceration in an adult prison.
However, after the recent murder in Phuket of Michelle Smith, an Australian travel agent, Thailand could also seek to restore its deteriorating image with tourists, which could lead to an acquittal.
The French police will arrest Anderlecht supporters who have come otherwise than with RSCA buses
The French police have decided to lay down strict rules concerning the meeting of the Royal Sporting Club of Anderlecht against Paris Saint-Germain set Tuesday, said Marie Verbeke, the spokeswoman of the Brussels-noon police zone, Friday.
Belgian RSCA fans who will want to go to this Champions League meeting will necessarily have to use the travel system set up by the RSCA.
"The bus convoy will be escorted by police from a former Rekem border post to PSG state," the spokeswoman said.
It will be the same for the return.
"If other supporters intended to travel to Paris by other means, the French police prefecture has indicated that measures have been taken to carry out arrests or even police custody," Verbeke said.
Living together in French, the challenge of the Marguerite-Bourgeoys School Board
At the Marguerite-Bourgeoys School Board, 62% of pupils have a mother tongue other than French.
This is what motivated, a year ago, a consultation with parents, students, teachers and members of education staff, to think about ways to better integrate students who live riding between several cultures.
The school board has just unveiled its vision of "Living Together in French".
For a year now, Vision diversité has been wandering in schools to help students of all origins discover common benchmarks that are not limited to the French language.
The discovery of neighborhoods, our architecture, our places are landmarks.
Great names of writers, artists, whether they are of stock or have come from elsewhere.
Who are our builders?
"So we're creating projects so that they identify with all this," explains the president of Vision diversity, Aïda Kamar.
Michel Venne, from the Institut du Nouveau Monde, has identified several challenges for the school board.
"On the transmission, for example, of what Quebec culture is, the feeling of belonging, support for the staff in the transformations that we live, within the student clientele," he enumerates.
The Marguerite-Bourgeoys School Board has created a research center that will give tools to professors who also sometimes come from elsewhere.
Rachida Azdouz, from the University of Montreal, will be the scientific director.
The preparation to manage a class in a North American, Quebec context.
“Different pedagogical strategies, that’s the real need,” she summarizes.
The research will focus on inclusion from all angles: linguistic, academic, social and cultural.
